&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/certificate-manager/"&gt;AWS Certificate Manager&lt;/a&gt; (ACM) now issues Amazon N2 certificates for mutual TLS (mTLS) and other authentication outside web browsers. ACM public certificates no longer include client authentication, in line with WebPKI requirements, so teams that needed client certificates had to define their own trust governance models and apply them to their private PKI. With Amazon N2 certificates, you can authenticate to partners and other organizations, such as a partner's client calling your API, without building and governing your own CA for them to rely on.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon N2 certificates come from the Amazon N2 certificate authorities (CAs), which are operated and governed by Amazon Trust Services, the same organization that operates the CAs issuing ACM public WebPKI certificates. Your peers add the &lt;a href="https://www.amazontrust.com/repository/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon N2 roots&lt;/a&gt; to their trust stores to verify your certificates. Certificates are valid for up to 395 days, and ACM renews them automatically: AWS integrated services use each renewed certificate, and you can also export them to your own clients and servers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You pay for each domain name in an ACM-issued Amazon N2 certificate, including when you use it with AWS integrated services. For details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/certificate-manager/pricing/"&gt;ACM pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon N2 certificates are available in all commercial AWS Regions where ACM is available. To learn more, see Amazon N2 certificates in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/acm-amazon-n2-certificates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Certificate Manager User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; multi-Region replication (MRR) now supports additional AWS Regions, extending the resilience benefits of MRR to more workloads. Amazon Cognito helps you implement secure sign-in and access control for users, AI agents, and microservices in minutes, and MRR lets you synchronize user and machine identity data, including credentials, user pool configurations, and federation setups, to a secondary user pool in a standby Region you designate in near real-time. With user data in the standby Region, you can redirect traffic and keep authentication available if there is a disruption in your primary Region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, MRR was available in 16 commercial Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. You can now also use MRR to replicate your user data to Asia Pacific (Hyderabad, Hong Kong, Jakarta, Malaysia, Melbourne, New Zealand, Osaka, Taipei, Thailand), Canada West (Calgary), Europe (Milan, Spain, Zurich), Israel (Tel Aviv), Mexico (Central), Africa (Cape Town) Regions, helping you meet resilience, data residency, and compliance requirements in the Regions that best align with your needs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;MRR is available as an add-on for user pools on the Essentials or Plus feature plans, and requires a multi-Region &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kms/"&gt;AWS KMS&lt;/a&gt; customer managed key (CMK). For the full list of AWS Regions where Amazon Cognito is available, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-multi-region.html" target="_blank"&gt;developer guide&lt;/a&gt; for instructions and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt; for pricing details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; now integrates with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager&lt;/a&gt;, so you can store and manage your user pool app client secrets in Secrets Manager. Amazon Cognito helps you implement secure sign-in and access control for users, AI agents, and microservices in minutes. An app client secret authenticates your application to Cognito. Previously, you had to manage and rotate these secrets in Cognito directly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this integration, you can leverage Secrets Manager for capabilities including rotation, access control with AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies, and encryption with an AWS managed key or your own AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer managed key. This can help with periodic credential rotation or compliance requirements; this also helps with keeping secrets out of the console. You can enable this integration for either a new or an existing app client, and disable it at any time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This integration is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito and AWS Secrets Manager are available, except AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany). Standard &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/pricing/"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager charges&lt;/a&gt; apply. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-client-apps.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Cognito Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; for instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Inspector Code Security now supports GitHub Enterprise Server (GHES) integration and private network connectivity for GHES and GitLab Self-Managed. Code Security is also launching automatic fix pull requests in public preview. Organizations that host GHES or GitLab Self-Managed in private networks can now connect through Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) configurations or AWS CodeConnections, so they can scan repositories without opening their firewalls to the entire AWS IP address range.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Inspector Code Security scans first-party application source code, third-party dependencies, and Infrastructure as Code for vulnerabilities in repositories on GitHub, GHES, and GitLab Self-Managed. With automatic fix pull requests, Amazon Inspector turns its suggested fix into a pull request that developers review and choose whether to merge, instead of applying the fix by hand. It opens pull requests only for high-confidence fixes to static application security testing (SAST) findings at or above a severity you set.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These features are available in all 10 AWS Regions where Amazon Inspector Code Security is available: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Stockholm), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Asia Pacific (Sydney), and Asia Pacific (Singapore). To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/code-security-assessments.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Inspector User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/"&gt;Amazon Inspector product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deploying and using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) account access manager is now even easier: you can use AWS managed policies to enable and administer it; you can include it in your infrastructure-as-code (IaC) configurations; and its role assignments are integrated in a growing number of third-party identity and entitlement management solutions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Administering workforce access with account access manager requires permissions across several services, including AWS IAM Identity Center, AWS Organizations, AWS KMS, and AWS IAM. Previously, defining administrative access required hand-assembling multi-service IAM policies. The new AWS managed policies enable faster ramp up with broadly scoped job-aligned permissions for: 1/ Full administrator (AWSAccountAccessManagerAdministrator) - used to enable and manage account access manager in the AWS organization management account and to map users/groups to IAM roles; 2/ Entitlement Manager (AWSAccountAccessManagerEntitlementManager) - used to assign and remove user access without the ability to change service configuration; and 3/ ReadOnly (AWSAccountAccessManagerReadOnlyAccess) - used by auditors and compliance reviewers for visibility into user access.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Account access manager streamlines assignment of IAM roles to workforce users and groups in IAM Identity Center, bringing together access management flexibility, user awareness, and a single point of federation. You can include account access manager in your IaC configurations managed through Terraform, Pulumi, or AWS CloudFormation. The entitlements that you assign with account access manager are reflected in your identity provider (IdP) and entitlements management system, including those offered by AWS partners Okta, PingIdentity, Cisco Duo, Apono, BeyondTrust, Britive, Sonrai Security, C1.ai, Lumos, and SailPoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;IAM account access manager is provided at no additional cost and available in all AWS Commercial Regions enabled by default. To learn more and get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/account-access-manager.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Cognito now supports authentication context class reference (ACR) and authentication methods reference (AMR), by adding ACR and AMR claims to access, ID, and refresh tokens. Your application can use the authentication level (ACR) to make authorization decisions, such as requiring additional factors for sensitive operations. It can also use completed methods (AMR), such as time-based one-time password (TOTP), for auditing and compliance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Cognito defines four authentication levels based on the factors a user completes, from password-only (level 1) to a password with a TOTP (level 4). Each token reflects the level achieved during sign-in, so your application can make authorization decisions based on how a user authenticated. If your application requires a stronger level, you can request a target level in your application's sign-in flow or through managed login, and Amazon Cognito issues new tokens once the user completes the required factors. You can also customize ACR level URIs for your user pool. For federated users from an OpenID Connect (OIDC) provider, Amazon Cognito maps the provider's ACR and AMR values to your levels.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ACR and AMR token claims are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Cognito is offered. Every user pool includes ACR and AMR token claims, regardless of feature plan. Requesting a target authentication level and configuring custom ACR level URIs are available with the Essentials or Plus feature plan. To get started, configure ACR and AMR using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs. See &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-step-up-authentication.html"&gt;developer guide&lt;/a&gt; for details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces that AWS Security Hub now monitors Google Cloud Platform (GCP) resources, extending risk analytics, cloud security posture management, vulnerability management, and security response management across both clouds. Many AWS customers running workloads in AWS and GCP have had to operate separate security tools for each environment, making it difficult to prioritize risks holistically or respond consistently. Security Hub now provides a single, unified experience to detect and respond to risks across your AWS and GCP environments in addition to existing support for monitoring Microsoft Azure environments.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security Hub automatically discovers GCP resources, including GCP Virtual Machines (VMs), GCP Google Artifact Registry (GAR) container images, GCP Cloud Run functions, and GCP identities, and evaluates them for misconfigurations, internet exposure, and software vulnerabilities. You receive posture checks against security standards including the CIS Benchmarks™ for Google Cloud Platform Foundations, unified resource inventory, risk and exposure analysis, and automated response through existing EventBridge integrations. AWS and GCP findings appear in the same prioritized view with the same finding formats and automation workflows, so security teams can operate from one console rather than switching between tools.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security Hub includes an independent 30-day free trial to monitor GCP resources that begins once you create your integration with Google Cloud Platform. After the trial, you pay the same price for monitoring GCP resources and equivalent AWS resources. You can create an integration to GCP from all AWS Regions where Security Hub is available except Middle East (UAE), Middle East (Bahrain), Asia Pacific (Taipei), and Asia Pacific (New Zealand). You can also create integrations to Google Cloud Platform for AWS Security Hub CSPM for posture management checks and Amazon Inspector for vulnerability management independently from AWS Security Hub. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Security Hub Pricing&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/what-is-securityhub-v2.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Security Hub documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center now supports interface VPC endpoints powered by AWS PrivateLink and network access controls, enabling you to keep IAM Identity Center traffic private and restrict which networks can reach your&amp;nbsp;IAM Identity Center services.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, IAM Identity Center rejected traffic arriving through VPC endpoints, requiring all sign-in and API traffic to use public endpoints. With this launch, you can create interface VPC endpoints for&amp;nbsp;AWS access portal and service APIs to route traffic over the AWS private network. IAM Identity Center sign-in traffic flows through the AWS Sign-in VPC endpoint. You can attach VPC endpoint policies to each endpoint to scope which AWS organizations, accounts and resources are reachable through your private connection.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Network access controls add a second layer of enforcement at the IAM Identity Center instance level. When enabled, you can require that all access arrive through a VPC endpoint, allow specific IP address ranges or VPCs as trusted sources, and exclude individual applications from enforcement to accommodate services that connect from outside your VPC. SCIM provisioning traffic from your external identity provider can be independently restricted to known IP address ranges. Network access controls are configured through the IAM Identity Center API and are disabled by default.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no additional cost. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/vpc-interface-endpoints.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Interface VPC endpoints&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Identity Center User Guide.&amp;nbsp;For more information, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/identity-center/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;IAM Identity Center product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center now gives you the option to require workforce users to re-authenticate with your external identity provider (IdP) each time their user interactive session expires. By default, when a user's session expires, IAM Identity Center renews it through the user's existing IdP session, and the user isn't prompted to sign in again. For organizations with strict security or compliance requirements that mandate explicit user verification at regular intervals, this automatic renewal may not meet policy requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When you set user re-authentication to Required and a user's user interactive session expires, IAM Identity Center prompts the user to re-authenticate with your external IdP. This helps you enforce stronger sign-in controls for regulated workloads. IAM Identity Center implements this by adding the SAML 2.0 ForceAuthn attribute to the SAML request sent to your IdP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can configure user re-authentication for any IAM Identity Center instance (organization or account) that's connected to an external IdP as its identity source. The setting applies only to service provider initiated sign-ins, where users sign in through your AWS access portal URL. Changes to the user re-authentication setting are logged in AWS CloudTrail, so you can audit when it was turned on or off.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;User re-authentication is available in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available at no additional cost. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/require-user-reauthentication.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Require user re-authentication&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS IAM Identity Center User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Hub now detects when software with a known vulnerability is actively running on your Amazon EC2 instances. Exposure findings now include a new trait, *The EC2 instance is running software with a known vulnerability*. These findings get a higher severity rating than findings for the same vulnerability where the software isn't running. This helps your security team focus on the vulnerabilities that pose the greatest risk,&amp;nbsp;without investing time and resources to investigate if the software is actually in use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security teams managing thousands of instances can now prioritize patching based on actual runtime risk.&amp;nbsp; Security Hub correlates data from Amazon GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring and Amazon Inspector VM Scanner to detect running vulnerable software. It then combines this with other signals to generate exposure findings for EC2 resources that may be at risk, so you can prioritize response and remediation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost with the AWS Security Hub Essentials plan in all AWS Regions where Security Hub is available . To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/what-is-securityhub-v2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, the exposure definition in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/exposure-ec2-instance.html#vulnerable-software-currently-running" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Remediating exposures for EC2&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/runtime-monitoring.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/inspector-vm-scanner.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Inspector VM Scanner User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. For the full list of Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regional Services List&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Secrets Manager now supports cross-account replication, enabling you to maintain a synchronized copy of a primary secret in a secondary AWS account for cross-account access, backup, and disaster recovery. Cross-account replication extends the existing multi-Region replication capability across account boundaries.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With cross-account replication, platform or security teams can own and rotate a secret centrally, and each consuming account gets a local, always-current copy without any shared KMS keys or cross-account secret policies. The replica secret is a full, independent resource, encrypted with a customer managed KMS key owned by the replica account. Secrets Manager updates it automatically whenever the primary secret's value changes, including rotations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can keep a live copy of critical secrets in a dedicated recovery or failover account, isolated from ransomware or a compromised primary account. If needed, the replica can be promoted to a standalone secret.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-account replication is available in all AWS Regions where Secrets Manager is available. For a list of regions where Secrets Manager is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/cross-account-replicate-secrets.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Replicate secrets across AWS accounts in the Secrets Manager User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/apireference/API_AssociateCrossAccountReplica.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AssociateCrossAccountReplica API reference&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/pricing/"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) now supports fine-grained access control, giving administrators precise control over who can access which workflows and resources in an Apache Airflow environment. Fine-grained access control is delivered through two new capabilities: IAM Identity Center integration and multi-team environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With IAM Identity Center integration, users sign in to the Apache Airflow UI with their corporate identity provider. Authorization is evaluated per request using IAM policies attached to IAM Identity Center Permission Sets, with condition keys that scope access by resource type (DAGs, connections, variables, pools, and more), action, and specific resource ID.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With multi-team environments, several teams can share a single MWAA environment. Each team gets its own DAG folder in Amazon S3, and users see only shared DAGs plus their own teams' DAGs based on IAM Identity Center group membership. Teams reduce cost through shared infrastructure and simplify management by configuring environment settings once. Multi-team environments are based on the Apache Airflow multi-team feature, which is currently in preview.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These features are available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/get-started.html#regions" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon MWAA is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/identity-center-integration.html" target="_blank"&gt;IAM Identity Center integration documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/multi-team-environments.html" target="_blank"&gt;multi-team environments documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS) now lets your workloads use their AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) identity in one partition to get temporary credentials for an IAM role in a different partition, without storing long-term credentials, which can help improve your security posture. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-fault-isolation-boundaries/partitions.html"&gt;Partitions&lt;/a&gt; are groups of AWS Regions, such as the commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and each partition has an independent instance of IAM, creating a hard boundary between them. Previously, organizations that run workloads across partitions commonly relied on long-term credentials, such as IAM user access keys, that don't expire automatically.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, your workload requests a short-lived token from AWS STS in its own partition and exchanges it for temporary AWS credentials in the other partition, using the existing &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_GetWebIdentityToken.html"&gt;GetWebIdentityToken&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.html"&gt;AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity&lt;/a&gt; APIs. You control which identities can access each role, apply governance across your organization, and audit token use in AWS CloudTrail in both partitions. This launch helps with scenarios such as consolidated security and compliance reporting, and centralized operations tooling. If your organization already operates a public key infrastructure (PKI), &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/roles-anywhere/"&gt;IAM Roles Anywhere&lt;/a&gt; is another option for cross-partition access.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-partition federation is available at no additional charge in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, the China Regions, and the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Region. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_providers_outbound_cross_partition.html"&gt;Federating AWS identities across AWS partitions&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM User Guide and the blog post &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/simplify-cross-partition-access-with-aws-iam-outbound-identity-federation/"&gt;Simplify cross-partition access with AWS IAM outbound identity federation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon MSK now supports version 1.3 of Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol for MSK Provisioned clusters through encryption policies. TLS 1.3 delivers faster, more secure encryption for streaming data and is increasingly required by compliance frameworks and enterprise security standards. It is also one of the prerequisites for post-quantum cryptography for protecting data against attacks that collect encrypted traffic now with the intent to decrypt it later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers can opt in to TLS 1.3 by selecting a named security policy in their MSK cluster configuration, with no change to existing clusters until a policy is applied. A named security policy defines which TLS version and cipher suites a cluster can accept. With this launch, Amazon MSK provides a set of policies customers can select and any connection that does not meet the selected policy requirements is rejected. This allows customers to stay in control of their security and compliance posture. Policies that support both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 allow connections to negotiate the highest version the client supports allowing clients that support TLS 1.3 to connect on it immediately while clients on TLS 1.2 continue to connect on TLS 1.2. This lets customers upgrade their clients at their pace before applying a stricter policy. Customers can also select stricter policies that align closer with their compliance requirements to restrict connections to TLS 1.3 only.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where Amazon MSK Provisioned is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/security.html"&gt;Amazon MSK documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center's Identity Store now delivers real-time events to Amazon EventBridge when users, groups, or group memberships or the identity store itself is created, updated, or deleted. AWS IAM Identity Center Identity Store is a central repository that allows you to manage identities and access across AWS accounts and applications, serving as the identity source for AWS IAM Identity Center. This new capability helps you automatically respond to identity lifecycle events and support your security and compliance processes by detecting changes as they happen.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With Identity Store change notifications, you can build automated workflows that respond to identity changes in your organization. For example, security teams can create EventBridge rules that trigger AWS Lambda functions to audit user provisioning activity, send alerts to security information and event management (SIEM) systems, or update access in downstream applications when users or groups change. Organizations that manage large workforces can also use this data to automate onboarding and offboarding when users are added to or removed from specific groups.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Change notifications are available in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available, and events are sent in each enabled Region of your instance. For the list of Regions, see &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?tab=service-feature&amp;amp;f=eJwdi0EKhDAUQ68yZK2L2fYAc4DZ-ouIZqQgtrRfQaR3909XeeElNwo3zsrlyzXEvcBhEByl51S0fws8OqQcE7Oen7Ap8_jjpEemTW9BEjhBzIJOoFYGb7D-wWxs1jV5NggLdw16vWZL2q36ivoAQn8tDw"&gt;AWS capabilities by Regions&lt;/a&gt;. Change notifications are available at no additional cost beyond standard Amazon EventBridge usage charges. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/event-notifications.html"&gt;Event notifications&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS IAM Identity Center User Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-identitystore.html"&gt;Identity Store events&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon EventBridge Events Reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trusted identity propagation carries a user's identity with their request, so AWS services can grant permissions based on who the user is and their underlying group associations, and log that user's access for auditing. With cross-Region support, this identity context can now be propagated to AWS services running in different AWS Regions, giving you greater flexibility in how you architect and deploy across AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers often distribute workloads across AWS Regions for many reasons including separating the compute from the data it queries, to running workloads where capacity is available, or for disaster recovery and global scale. Previously, trusted identity propagation required all of the AWS services accessing a user's identity to reside in the same AWS Region. To reach services in another AWS Region, customers had to either grant access through broad IAM roles that cannot distinguish individual users or consolidate all their services into a single AWS Region. With cross-Region trusted identity propagation, a user's identity travels with the request across AWS Regions automatically, so you can grant access based on who the user is and their underlying group associations, and audit who accessed which resources, while preserving fine-grained access control for every individual user.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-Region trusted identity propagation is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. For the list of Regions where IAM Identity Center is available, see &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?tab=service-feature&amp;amp;f=eJxtks1OwzAQhF8F7bk-kFKQKnFF4sCFHusIWck2jTCx5Z8CqvLuGKeOHTu3nW_Wu_LYV9DIsTHYvmPXi0HDHo4UrCbItCH3FDZRVUF9Y-p5NXnsRLSw5nwzmUzHBFUFNQhlzsuGiFa6tgFlOyZZLeRybEQrXdsSPRRoV6LHEj15hFY3pMUsw05cVkiSZcNIg4NRjEeQ2GgzOwFVAD6t6KdJzXJuXg5P3jGoKZqeZ4u_fnKAJUhXazbfvIYNSCUkKvP70nOD6uOEzFiF7utdKUgKewpC-XPGiWPtiu6_cO6nd28HXlvfJDx79vXF12-Wm55MP_ruYKV0oVAY6xHGP20D8yw"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region.&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp; For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/trustedidentitypropagation-overview.html"&gt;Trusted identity propagation&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Identity Center User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center now retains your workforce identities and their assignments when you change your identity source from Active Directory to an external identity provider (IdP), such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, or to the Identity Center directory. Specifically, it preserves your users, groups, group memberships, permission set assignments, and application assignments. Previously, this change required you to recreate them in IAM Identity Center.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;IAM Identity Center helps you connect your workforce identities to AWS once and manage their access centrally across AWS applications and accounts. When you change your identity source from Active Directory, your existing identity store remains in place, so your users and groups keep the same IDs. As a result, users retain their access to AWS accounts and applications, and policies that reference your user IDs, group IDs, or identity store ID continue to work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To make this change, configure your new identity source with the standard setup required for users to sign in. After the change, your workforce signs in through your dual-stack AWS access portal URL. Changing your identity source in the other direction, to Active Directory, works as before. This feature is available for organization instances of IAM Identity Center in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. IAM Identity Center is provided at no additional cost.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, navigate to the IAM Identity Center console. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/manage-your-identity-source-change.html"&gt;Change your identity source&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Identity Center User Guide. To learn more about IAM Identity Center, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/identity-center/"&gt;product detail page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Security Hub announces support for exporting findings to Amazon S3 in CSV or JSON (OCSF) format. Security teams that need findings outside the console for use cases such as compliance reporting and audit evidence can now export findings from every findings page in the Security Hub console, including Threats, Exposure, Vulnerabilities, Posture Management, Sensitive Data, and All Findings, without building and maintaining their own extraction pipelines.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can start an export on demand from the findings page you are viewing and have Security Hub deliver the results to an S3 bucket in your account. You can choose CSV when you want to review or share findings in a spreadsheet or export in JSON if you need these findings in Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Findings export is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Security Hub is available. To learn more, visit the AWS Security Hub User Guide at&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-findings-export.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-findings-export.html&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) now supports AWS PrivateLink for ACME public certificate issuance, allowing you to request and renew public TLS certificates over a private network path that stays within the AWS network. You can now create a VPC interface endpoint to the ACM ACME service and route issuance traffic from any ACMEv2-compatible client through your VPC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you're already using ACME with ACM, setup requires no changes to your ACME clients. After you create your managed ACME endpoint in ACM, you create a standard VPC interface endpoint using the VPC console, AWS CLI, or AWS CloudFormation. Private DNS resolves your existing ACME directory URL to the interface endpoint inside your VPC automatically, so the same client configuration and directory URL continue to work with no reconfiguration. Issuance operations—account creation, order creation, domain validation, finalization, and certificate retrieval—then flow over PrivateLink. All activity remains visible in the ACM console with AWS CloudTrail logging and Amazon CloudWatch metrics for auditability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS PrivateLink support for ACME certificate issuance is available in all commercial AWS Regions. Standard AWS PrivateLink charges apply for interface endpoints; see the AWS PrivateLink pricing page. For ACM pricing details, see the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/certificate-manager/pricing/"&gt;ACM pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To get started with ACME and PrivateLink, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/automate-public-tls-certificate-issuance-with-acme-support-in-aws-certificate-manager/"&gt;AWS News blog post&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;or read the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/acm-acme.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center helps you configure the single sign-on experience for your workforce to AWS accounts and applications. IAM Identity Center now supports network access controls for Identity Store, which stores your users and groups. You can restrict access to the Identity Store API and the SCIM API based on the network that requests originate from. Your custom applications and user provisioning workflows use the Identity Store API to manage and look up users and groups, and your external identity provider uses the SCIM API to synchronize users and groups.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For the Identity Store API, you can require that requests arrive only through allowed VPC endpoints in your account or organization, or from specific source VPCs. For both APIs, you can allow requests only from specific IP ranges. Within the same configuration, you can apply different restrictions to each API. For example, you can require that Identity Store API requests arrive only through VPC endpoints, while allowing SCIM requests from your external identity provider's published IP ranges.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Network access controls are optional and turned off by default. Requests that AWS services make on your behalf are exempt. You configure network access controls by using the Identity Store API through the AWS SDKs and AWS CLI. This capability is available in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is offered.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about IAM Identity Center, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/identity-center/"&gt;product detail page&lt;/a&gt;. To get started with network access controls, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/IdentityStoreAPIReference/API_UpdateIdentityStore.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Identity Store API Reference&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces that Amazon GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring is now included in the AWS Security Hub Threat Analytics plan. Runtime Monitoring inspects operating system, network, and file activity to surface threats such as container escapes, privilege escalation, and cryptomining on Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon EKS clusters, and Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate. Security Hub now bills this coverage through streamlined pricing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you have Security Hub enabled in an account and region, you no longer receive separate Amazon GuardDuty charges for Runtime Monitoring for that account and region. That usage now appears on your bill under AWS Security Hub, where Security Hub meters it as a single usage type that spans Amazon EC2, Amazon EKS, and Amazon ECS on AWS Fargate rather than as separate charges for each resource type.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your detection coverage, your finding types, and your GuardDuty security agents all remain the same, and you do not need to reconfigure anything. Please note that the free trial for the Threat Analytics plan remains separate from the free trial for the Security Hub Essentials plan, and that this change does not add a new free trial for Runtime Monitoring. To see how the change affects your bill, you can use AWS Cost Explorer or the Security Hub usage page.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For a list of AWS Regions where Security Hub is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, see the AWS Security Hub &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the AWS Security Hub &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/v2/home"&gt;console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;IAM Identity Center helps you connect your workforce identities to AWS once and streamline access management to AWS accounts and applications. You can now replicate IAM Identity Center to opt-in AWS Regions, and between Regions within the AWS GovCloud (US) and AWS China Regions. Previously, multi-Region support was available in the enabled-by-default commercial AWS Regions. This helps you improve the resilience of user access to AWS accounts and deploy AWS applications in the AWS Regions that best align with your business needs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When you enable multi-Region support, IAM Identity Center automatically replicates your identities, entitlements, and other information from the primary Region to additional Regions. If IAM Identity Center is affected by a disruption in the primary Region, users continue to have access to their AWS accounts using already provisioned entitlements in the additional Regions. AWS application administrators can use the standard application deployment workflow to deploy their application in an additional Region while you continue to administer IAM Identity Center in the primary Region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Multi-Region support is available for organization instances that use an external identity provider or the IAM Identity Center directory as the identity source, and requires a multi-Region customer managed KMS key (CMK). When you create a new instance, you can enable multi-Region support with a single click, which also creates the CMK. For existing instances, create a multi-Region CMK in AWS KMS, then configure it in IAM Identity Center. Standard&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kms/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS KMS charges apply&lt;/a&gt; for storing and using CMKs. IAM Identity Center is provided at no additional cost.
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 For the full list of AWS Regions where multi-Region support is available, see &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?tab=service-feature&amp;amp;f=eJxtks1OwzAQhF8F7bk-kFKQKnFF4sCFHusIWck2jTCx5Z8CqvLuGKeOHTu3nW_Wu_LYV9DIsTHYvmPXi0HDHo4UrCbItCH3FDZRVUF9Y-p5NXnsRLSw5nwzmUzHBFUFNQhlzsuGiFa6tgFlOyZZLeRybEQrXdsSPZRoV6LHEj15hFY3pMUsw05cVkiSZcNIg4NRjEeQ2GgzOwFVAD6t6KdJzXJuXg5P3jGoKZqeZ4u_fnKAJUhXazbfvIYNSCUkKvP70nOD6uOEzFiF7utdKUgKewpC-XPGiWPtiu6_cO6nd28HXlvfJDx79vXF12-Wm55MP_ruYKV0oVAY6xHGP20D8yw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;To learn more about multi-Region support, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/multi-region-iam-identity-center.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Using IAM Identity Center across multiple AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;. To find out which AWS applications support deployment in additional Regions, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/awsapps-that-work-with-identity-center.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS applications that you can use with IAM Identity Center&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;​​​AWS Secrets Manager now integrates with the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awsconsolehelpdocs/latest/gsg/recommended-actions.html"&gt;AWS Recommended Actions framework&lt;/a&gt; to surface contextual, actionable suggestions for your secrets directly in the Secrets Manager console. ​​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​​With this launch, you can view tailored recommendations alongside your secrets to improve your security posture and follow best practices without leaving the console. For example, you can identify secrets that need rotation configured, use a customer managed key instead of default service-provided key for encryption, and act on secrets configuration improvements, all from a single view.​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​​This feature is available in all AWS Commercial &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;Regions&lt;/a&gt; where AWS Secrets Manager is offered, at no additional cost. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager console&lt;/a&gt;.​&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center's Identity Store APIs now accept the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) for a user, group, group membership, or identity store anywhere the APIs previously accepted the resource ID. ARN support is additive and existing integrations continue to work unchanged.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you build on the Identity Store APIs, you may already hold resource ARNs — for example, from IAM policy evaluation, CloudTrail events, or cross-service integrations. Previously, you had to strip the ARN down to the resource ID before calling Identity Store APIs. With this change, you can pass either form directly, simplifying application code and eliminating potential parsing errors.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The change applies to every request identifier field across the Identity Store API surface. Responses continue to return resource IDs as they did before. Malformed or wrong-resource-type ARNs return a ValidationException.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS IAM Identity Center is offered, at no additional cost. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/IdentityStoreAPIReference/"&gt;Identity Store API Reference&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/identity/federation/outbound-federation/"&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) outbound identity federation&lt;/a&gt; now supports Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) endpoints for the OpenID Connect (OIDC) discovery APIs. You can now access the OIDC discovery metadata and JSON Web Key Set (JWKS) verification key endpoints from within your VPC using &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/privatelink/"&gt;AWS PrivateLink&lt;/a&gt;, without requiring traffic to traverse the public internet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;IAM outbound identity federation eliminates the need to use long-lived credentials when your AWS workloads access external services. Instead, your workloads request short-lived JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) from AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS). External services verify these tokens using public verification keys and metadata available at OIDC discovery endpoints. Previously, the OIDC discovery endpoints were only reachable over the public internet, so a verifying workload running in a VPC without internet access could not retrieve them. With this launch, you can create an interface VPC endpoint to reach these endpoints privately, keeping the verification key retrieval traffic within the AWS network. This capability helps you meet network security requirements for workloads that operate in VPCs with restricted internet access, while still enabling external services to verify JWTs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and China Regions. There is no additional charge for this feature beyond standard &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/privatelink/pricing/"&gt;AWS PrivateLink pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_sts_oidc_vpc_endpoint_create.html"&gt;IAM User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of AWS Network Security Manager, a comprehensive network security management solution designed to simplify security policy deployment and enforcement at scale. Network Security Manager supports AWS WAF and AWS Shield Advanced, with support for AWS Network Firewall soon to follow. AWS Network Security Manager enables security teams to consistently enforce firewall and DDoS protection configurations across their entire AWS organization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Network Security Manager features always-on security deployment automation that applies AWS WAF and AWS Shield Advanced security protection to Internet facing assets like AWS Application Load Balancer and Amazon CloudFront. This automated approach helps security teams deploy and manage WAF rules and Shield Advanced protections efficiently, ensuring consistent security posture across targeted accounts and resources. Teams can centrally manage security policies, automatically detect configuration drift, and apply remediation reducing the operational overhead of manual security management while maintaining comprehensive protection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Security Manager is generally available in US East (N. Virginia). For pricing information, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-security-manager/pricing/"&gt;AWS Network Security Manager Pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Redshift now supports AWS IAM Identity Center authentication for provisioned clusters and serverless workgroups configured with enhanced VPC routing (EVR). You can access Amazon Redshift with single sign-on with your corporate credentials, and the traffic traverses Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) and stays on the AWS network. This is valuable for customers with data residency, regulatory, or network-isolation requirements that mandate no public internet egress for analytics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With Redshift EVR, all traffic between your Redshift warehouse and other AWS services goes through your VPC, where you can govern it with security groups, network ACLs, and endpoint policies, and observe it in VPC Flow Logs. With this launch, Redshift validates and exchanges IAM Identity Center tokens over AWS PrivateLink interface VPC endpoints from inside your VPC, so authentication and authorization follows the same governed network path as the rest of your Redshift traffic. This feature also supports &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/multi-region-iam-identity-center.html"&gt;IAM Identity Center multi-Region replication&lt;/a&gt; for customers running Redshift in a different Region than their primary Identity Center instance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Read the Amazon Redshift enhanced VPC routing &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/redshift-iam-access-control-idp-connect-evr.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/integrate-amazon-redshift-and-iam-identity-center-with-enhanced-vpc-routing/"&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;to get started. This capability is available in all AWS Regions where both Amazon Redshift and IAM Identity Center are available.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announced four new capabilities for Automated Security Response on AWS (ASR) solution. Customers can now use an AI-driven Toolkit that generates custom remediations using any AI assistant with built-in safety guardrails. In addition, customers can automatically remediate findings from Amazon Inspector, Amazon GuardDuty, and Amazon Macie. Customers can also centrally configure and scope automated remediations by account, OU, region, and resource tags through an enhanced web console. Lastly, customers can configure notifications for AWS Security Hub findings with new multi-channel adapters for Email, Slack, Jira, and ServiceNow with severity-based filtering and configurable deadline enforcement.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt; ASR's AI Remediation Toolkit reduces custom remediation development time from weeks to hours using guided prompts with built-in safety guardrails, minimizing misconfigurations and eliminating reliance on deep SSM Automation expertise. Expanded service coverage enables automatic response to credential compromise, unpatched vulnerabilities, and sensitive data exposure with minimal manual triage. The enhanced web console centralizes management of 100+ security controls with built-in validation, replacing error-prone manual DynamoDB and SSM configuration. Configurable multi-channel notifications with remediation links, deadlines, and IaC code snippets help teams fix root causes and ensure timely, accountable response.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Automated Security Response on AWS is available to deploy in all commercial and opt-in AWS regions, including GovCloud (US) and China regions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/automated-security-response-on-aws/"&gt;ASR solution page.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SageMaker MLflow now enables customers to encrypt their data using customer-managed keys (CMK) through AWS Key Management Service (KMS).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This enhancement allows organizations with strict security and compliance requirements to manage their own encryption keys. With customer-managed keys, you gain enhanced security control and comprehensive audit capabilities through AWS CloudTrail integration. You can encrypt your data with your own KMS keys, trace all data access for security auditing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customer-managed keys must be created in the same AWS account and region as your MLflow App, and only symmetric AWS KMS keys are supported.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is generally available in all AWS Regions&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/mlflow.html#mlflow-regions"&gt;where MLflow App is available&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/mlflow-kms.html"&gt;SageMaker MLflow detail page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda MicroVMs now supports customer managed keys (CMK) in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kms/"&gt;AWS Key Management Service&lt;/a&gt; (KMS) for data encryption at rest. CMK gives you full control over the lifecycle and usage of your KMS keys&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/"&gt;,&lt;/a&gt; helping meet compliance requirements in regulated industries like financial services and healthcare.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda MicroVMs is a serverless compute primitive that provides VM-level isolation, near-instant launch and resume, and state preservation for executing user or AI generated code. Use MicroVMs to build applications like AI coding assistants, interactive development environments, and vulnerability scanners.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a CMK in AWS KMS and configure its cryptographic properties, metadata, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/"&gt;AWS IAM&lt;/a&gt; access permissions.&amp;nbsp;Then, specify this CMK as a parameter when you create a MicroVM image or run a MicroVM. Lambda uses this CMK to encrypt your MicroVM image, MicroVM image environment variables, and the payload passed to the MicroVM run hook.&amp;nbsp; You can use CMK with Lambda MicroVMs&amp;nbsp;through the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/lambda/"&gt;AWS Lambda console&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudformation/"&gt;AWS CloudFormation&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cdk/"&gt;AWS Cloud Development Kit&lt;/a&gt;, or with your preferred Agentic development tools using the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/serverless-skills/aws-lambda-microvms"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;​​CMK support for MicroVMs is available in the following AWS Regions:&amp;nbsp;Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Thailand), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (Spain), Europe (Stockholm), South America (São Paulo),&amp;nbsp;US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), and US West (Oregon). To learn more about Lambda MicroVMs, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-microvms-guide.html"&gt;AWS Lambda MicroVMs developer guide&lt;/a&gt;. For information on pricing for&amp;nbsp;CMK, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kms/pricing/"&gt;AWS KMS pricing&lt;/a&gt;.​&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) introduces &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/troubleshoot_access-denied-authorization-id.html" target="_blank"&gt;Access Troubleshooter&lt;/a&gt;, a new capability that gives you visibility into the full authorization context behind access denied errors so you can resolve them without trial-and-error. Previously, developers relied on the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the denying policy to debug a denial, with no visibility into the authorization context or other policies evaluated in the request that could contribute to the denial. This made troubleshooting access issues time-consuming and inefficient.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Starting today, access denied error messages for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/troubleshoot_access-denied-authorization-id.html#access-troubleshooter-supported-services" target="_blank"&gt;supported services&lt;/a&gt; and APIs will include a unique identifier that you can use to retrieve details of the authorization performed by the service. You gain visibility into what policies were evaluated for each request, the authorization context of those requests, and the results of the evaluation. This enables you to identify the root cause of authorization issues quickly, streamlining your access management workflows and reducing time spent debugging permission problems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Access Troubleshooter and how it can help you resolve access denied errors efficiently, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/troubleshoot_access-denied-authorization-id.html" target="_blank"&gt;User Guide on troubleshooting access denied error messages&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Key Management Service (KMS) now integrates with AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), giving you a simpler and more consistent way to share KMS keys across your AWS accounts, within your organization in AWS Organizations, and with specific IAM roles and users. You can now let trusted parties define the access they need through reusable managed permissions in RAM, while keeping full control of your keys and key policies. To share a key, create a resource share by selecting the keys to share, choosing a managed permission, and specifying the principals. You can use AWS managed permissions for common scenarios, or customer managed permissions (CMPs) authored by you or by trusted parties such as SaaS providers and central security teams.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Sharing KMS keys through RAM makes enabling cross-account access faster to set up and safer to maintain. The trusted parties who need access to your keys can publish the policy once as a CMP, without the need for you to modify the key policies by hand. You simply fill in the values that scope it to your environment and attach it to the resource share. Your existing key policy is untouched, so you are always in control of your key as your environment grows. When a new policy version is available via an updated CMP, you decide when to apply it to your resource shares.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This enhancement is built on new AWS RAM capabilities that are available for every resource type RAM supports. CMPs now support multiple policy statements and additional IAM condition operators, including StringNotEquals, BoolIfExists, and ArnLike, along with the ForAnyValue and ForAllValues prefixes, so you can express precise, least-privilege access. The&amp;nbsp;parameterized managed permissions lets you place a temporary placeholder in CMPs that allows each resource share owner to customize and scope down CMPs to their environment, enabling CMPs to be reused and serve many accounts. CMPs can now be published and shared across accounts, so the authors can maintain one permission, manage its versions centrally, and see every resource share that uses it, while other accounts can use it directly without keeping their own copy.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;KMS key sharing with RAM is available in all AWS Regions where both services are available, except the Middle East (UAE) and Middle East (Bahrain).&amp;nbsp;For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/key-sharing-ram.html"&gt;Sharing KMS keys with AWS RAM&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS KMS Developer Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ram/latest/userguide/working-with.html"&gt;Working with shared AWS resources&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS RAM Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/cspm/"&gt;AWS Security Hub CSPM&lt;/a&gt; now supports AWS Organizations declarative policies. You can use them to enable Security Hub CSPM across all accounts and Regions in your organization and to define which security standards and controls are enabled.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Policies can apply broadly to every account and Region, or target specific account and Region combinations. Declarative policies help keep each account's configuration aligned with its attached policy, which helps prevent configuration drift. You can also apply them automatically to new accounts, so each account starts with a baseline configuration as soon as it's created.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Declarative policies are now the recommended way to manage Security Hub CSPM across an organization. They align with how other security services, such as Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and AWS Security Hub allow customers to manage configuration of the service. They also provide more flexibility, such as configuring Region-specific settings. You can continue to use central configuration until you're ready to migrate. When you are, use the migration process in the Security Hub CSPM console. It converts your existing central configuration policies into declarative policies and applies them to the appropriate accounts and regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Declarative policies for Security Hub CSPM are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/orgs-policies-and-central-config.html#orgs-policies-import-tool" target="_blank"&gt;Migrating central configuration policies&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/accounts-orgs-policies.html" target="_blank"&gt;Declarative policies&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Security Hub CSPM User Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_security_hub_cspm.html" target="_blank"&gt;Security Hub CSPM policies&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Organizations User Guide. To receive notifications about new Security Hub CSPM features and controls, subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-announcements.html" target="_blank"&gt;Security Hub SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;. You can also &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/cspm/pricing/"&gt;try Security Hub CSPM at no cost for 30 days&lt;/a&gt; with the AWS Free Tier offering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces next-generation firewall (NGFW) policy for AWS Network Firewall, a new policy model that provides security teams with a simpler policy structure and increased rule capacity. AWS Network Firewall is a managed network security service that protects your Amazon VPCs with access control and threat detection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new policy model strengthens your security posture by layering firewall and IPS protections instead of combining them, which also makes policies easier to manage. You author firewall rules that enforce deny-by-default access control, such as allowing traffic only to approved domains. For IPS rules, you can use managed rule groups from AWS or AWS Marketplace partners, which update automatically as new threats emerge. You can also create your own custom IPS rule groups. Firewall and IPS rules each have their own capacity, so you no longer trade one off against the other. For rules you don't share across policies, you can create embedded rule groups that draw capacity on demand without pre-allocation. With NGFW policy, all rules are stateful and evaluate with full connection context, for consistent, predictable traffic handling. Your existing policies continue to operate without changes, and IPS rules remain backward compatible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;NGFW policy is available at no additional charge in all commercial AWS Regions and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For a full list of supported regions, visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?f=eJxVirEKg0AQBX9FXq1FWiGFjZAmRVKkcC0uusji4cneqYj477lcEUg3b94c8Gy5C9w_eBA3eZRoCIsv2PhQXAgt8l9TrUaseYuVsMewmvbsT-WY1c2sYa_FBtbYHIRAKJs2J8wRCE4JcQxfG98xSc-6Sse3Pn0uuWviNXH1emZ3DpvTMatFeTPWEs72xPkBR_VAow&amp;amp;tab=service-feature&amp;amp;refid=d08148e0-c462-4c19-ae34-467de69e31c6" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/firewall-policies-v2.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces that AWS Security Hub now provides a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) inventory, giving central security teams a continuously updated, organization-wide view of the software packages and applications installed on their compute resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security Hub builds the inventory from the SBOMs that Amazon Inspector generates for Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon ECR container images, and AWS Lambda functions, with no additional configuration. The SBOM page lists each package with its version, type, and the number of resources where it is installed. You can filter and group the list by package attributes, account, and region. On the resource detail page, the Installed software tab lists every package on that resource, so you can judge its function and criticality while triaging any finding. You can also query the inventory through the Security Hub API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The SBOM inventory is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost. It requires no new enablement and populates automatically where Amazon Inspector is enabled. It is available in all AWS Regions where Security Hub is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI now supports IAM Identity Center (IdC) multi-region replication, enabling you to deploy SageMaker AI Studio domains in different regions from your IdC instance's primary region. Previously, the primary IdC instance had to reside in the same region as the SageMaker AI Studio domain; now customers can replicate their primary IdC instance into the region where they wish to use SageMaker AI Studio domains. This capability helps customers maintain compliance while utilizing centralized workforce identity management.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;As an Amazon SageMaker AI Studio administrator, you can deploy SageMaker AI domains that align with your workforce's location and data residency requirements, independent of where your primary IdC instance is set up. Organizations can retain their primary IdC instance in one region while operating SageMaker AI Studio workloads in compliance-mandated regions, support global operations through centralized identity management, and satisfy data sovereignty requirements without compromising single sign-on (SSO) capabilities.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/onboard-custom.html"&gt;SageMaker AI documentation&lt;/a&gt; and to learn about setting up IAM Identity Center multi-region support see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/multi-region-iam-identity-center.html"&gt;IAM Identity Center User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Multi-region IdC replication is available in all standard &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker AI Studio is supported, including: Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Europe (Ireland), US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt), South America (São Paulo), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Europe (London), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Europe (Paris), Europe (Stockholm).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Cognito now supports the GetClientToken API operation, enabling app clients to obtain access tokens for machine-to-machine (M2M) authorization directly through the AWS SDK, CLI, or API — without configuring a user pool domain. This gives you an additional path to authorize service-to-service communication for applications, microservices, and automated workloads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The new GetClientToken API operation lets your app client authenticate with its client ID and secret to receive an access token authorized for custom scopes on your resource servers. As a native AWS API operation, GetClientToken integrates seamlessly with AWS SDKs and supports AWS WAF and VPC interface endpoints (AWS PrivateLink). The existing domain-based OAuth 2.0 client-credentials flow remains available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Cognito user pools are available. To get started, configure an app client and call GetClientToken using the AWS Management Console, CLI, or SDKs. Standard Amazon &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/pricing/"&gt;Cognito M2M pricing&lt;/a&gt; applies. See &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-define-resource-servers.html#get-client-token"&gt;Amazon Cognito Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_GetClientToken.html"&gt;GetClientToken API Reference&lt;/a&gt; for details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Sign-In now enables developers and AI agents to stay authenticated for up to 90 days without frequent sign-ins or consent prompts. With this launch, administrators can configure &lt;b&gt;Sign-in duration&lt;/b&gt;, which controls how long an OAuth Sign-in session remains active, for individual IAM users and roles.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability reduces authentication interruptions for long-running workflows and provides a more secure alternative to static access keys by using short-lived, renewable credentials. Existing session duration limits remain unchanged unless administrators explicitly configure longer durations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Sign-In Duration supports AWS CLI (aws login), AWS MCP Server, and other OAuth-enabled applications. It does not affect AWS Management Console sessions. Customers can configure the setting using the IAM console, AWS CLI, or IAM API in all AWS commercial Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;To learn more&lt;/b&gt;, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/oauth-sign-in-session-management.html"&gt;AWS Sign-In session management User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_update-role-settings.html#id_roles_update-signin-duration"&gt;Configure Sign-In duration on Role User Guid&lt;/a&gt;e, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/reference-signin-condition-keys.html#reference-signin-condition-keys-oauth"&gt;Condition Key User Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-194-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/20/2026 08:30 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Incorrect privilege assignment in the user and group reconciliation logic in AWS ssosync before 2.9.5 might allow an authenticated remote user who is assigned a reused Google Workspace e-mail address or group name to inherit the IAM Identity Center group memberships and permission set assignments of a previously synchronized, unrelated identity, via automatic synchronization of that Google Workspace account or group.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 2.9.5&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-194-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AWS Secrets Store CSI Driver Provider now supports AssumeRole (role-chaining), allowing Kubernetes pods to access secrets and parameters across AWS accounts by assuming an IAM role in the account where the secrets are stored. Previously, customers reading cross-account secrets needed to add a resource policy to each secret and use a customer-managed KMS key, as the AWS managed key (aws/secretsmanager) cannot be used for cross-account access. Now, teams can name a target role in the SecretProviderClass, and the provider assumes that role to fetch the secrets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With AssumeRole support, customers managing hundreds of AWS accounts and thousands of services can simplify cross-account secret access. A single trust policy on one target role replaces resource policies on each secret. Secrets encrypted with the AWS managed key (aws/secretsmanager) and SSM parameters become readable across accounts. The target account sees workload-specific session names in CloudTrail events for better attribution. This feature works with both IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) and EKS Pod Identity on initial mount and rotation, and supports failover regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AssumeRole support is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS and the AWS Secrets Store CSI Driver Provider are available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/integrating_ascp_assume_role.html" target="_blank"&gt;Assume an IAM role to retrieve secrets with the ASCP&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-191-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/19/2026 13:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-132607, an issue in the decryption component of Amazon S3 Encryption Client for PHP in the AWS SDK for PHP. The client did not verify the length of the AES-GCM authentication tag recorded in an object's metadata. A user with read and write access to an encrypted object, who knows or can guess its plaintext, might craft a ciphertext that decrypts to plaintext of their choice of the same length. This requires repeated writes to the object. The issue does not disclose the plaintext or the encryption key.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-191-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/roles-anywhere"&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) Roles Anywhere&lt;/a&gt; now supports automatic revocation checking using certificate revocation list (CRL) distribution points (CDPs). This helps keep revocation checks in sync with your certificate authority (CA) and can help improve your security posture. When you enable this capability on a trust anchor, IAM Roles Anywhere reads the CDP URLs embedded in authenticating certificates, downloads the corresponding CRLs, and denies credential requests that use revoked certificates.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, to check revocation status, you had to import CRLs into IAM Roles Anywhere and re-import them each time your CA published an update, which added operational overhead. With this launch, IAM Roles Anywhere downloads CRLs from your CDPs and refreshes them periodically, so you no longer need to re-import them. You can use this capability with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/private-ca/"&gt;AWS Private Certificate Authority&lt;/a&gt; or your own public key infrastructure (PKI), provided your certificates include publicly accessible CDP URLs that meet the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/rolesanywhere/latest/userguide/trust-model.html#crl-distribution-point-revocation"&gt;distribution point requirements&lt;/a&gt;. To enable it on a trust anchor, use the IAM Roles Anywhere console, AWS SDKs, or AWS CLI.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available at no additional cost in all commercial AWS Regions. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/rolesanywhere/latest/userguide/trust-model.html#crl-distribution-point-revocation"&gt;Automatic revocation checking with CRL distribution points&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Roles Anywhere User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS DataSync Enhanced mode now supports OpenID Connect (OIDC) authentication for Google Cloud Storage and Microsoft Azure Blob Storage locations. DataSync uses an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role to get short-lived credentials from Google Cloud or Microsoft Entra ID and refreshes them automatically so your transfers run without interruption.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now use DataSync to access your Google Cloud Storage and Azure Blob Storage without long-term credentials such as hash-based message authentication code (HMAC) keys or shared access signature (SAS) tokens. This lets you move data across clouds while complying with security policies that might prohibit long-term credentials.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can get started by adding an IAM role to your DataSync location with the AWS CLI, API, or AWS CloudFormation. This capability is available in all AWS Regions where DataSync Enhanced mode is supported. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/what-is-datasync.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS DataSync User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/video-streams/"&gt;Amazon Kinesis Video Streams&lt;/a&gt; now supports interface VPC endpoints powered by &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/what-is-privatelink.html"&gt;AWS PrivateLink&lt;/a&gt;, providing private connectivity from your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC). Traffic between your VPC and Kinesis Video Streams stays on the AWS network and is not exposed to the public internet, across the control plane and the video ingestion and playback data planes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customers with strict security, compliance, or network-isolation requirements can now ingest, store, and play back video without internet gateways, NAT devices, or public IP addresses. For example, connected-camera or IoT video workloads in a private subnet can send media to Kinesis Video Streams and retrieve it for playback and analytics entirely over private connectivity. You create the endpoint from the Amazon VPC console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs and can attach a VPC endpoint policy to control access.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;VPC endpoints for Amazon Kinesis Video Streams are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Kinesis Video Streams is available, including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions and the China (Beijing) Region, operated by Beijing Sinnet Technology Co., Ltd. ("Sinnet").&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesisvideostreams/latest/dg/vpc-interface-endpoints.html"&gt;Getting Started Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS partners can now access AWS Partner Central agents from tools they already use, such as Amazon Quick and Kiro, using OAuth through AWS Sign-In. Partners can authorize agent access with their existing AWS identities, sign-in methods, IAM permissions, and governance controls without installing or maintaining additional authentication software.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, AWS Partners needed to set up an MCP proxy with SigV4 credentials to access Partner Central agents from their existing tools, or sign in to AWS Partner Central through the AWS Management Console with IAM credentials. OAuth simplifies this by allowing partners to use AWS Sign-In to authorize tools such as Amazon Quick and Kiro to access Partner Central agents. Partners can use OAuth&amp;nbsp;from their existing tools for co-sell engagements,&amp;nbsp;AWS funding applications, and AWS Marketplace seller setup. Administrators can govern access with IAM policies, global condition keys, token introspection and revocation APIs, dynamic client registration, and CloudTrail audit events.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;OAuth support is available to AWS Partners through AWS Partner Central agents MCP Server, which is available in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. To learn more, visit &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/partner-central/latest/developer-guide/mcp-getting-started.html"&gt;Getting started with the Partner Central agents MCP Server&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Notebooks now support Trusted Identity Propagation (TIP) with Amazon Athena, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon EMR Serverless, enabling per-user access control for data analytics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When connected to a TIP-enabled compute in a TIP-enabled Project, each notebook user's IAM Identity Center identity flows through to AWS Lake Formation, ensuring they see only the tables, columns, and rows their permissions allow, without sharing a single broad execution role. With TIP, enterprises get per-user data boundaries enforced based on who is running the query, full audit attribution with CloudTrail recording which user accessed data, and reduced admin friction since identity propagates automatically through the existing compute connection with no extra login, token, or role management required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, use a notebook in a TIP enabled Project with the supported engines.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;This feature is available in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/supported-regions.html"&gt;all AWS Regions where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/trusted-identity-propagation.html"&gt;Trusted identity propagation&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio Administrator Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/notebooks.html"&gt;Notebooks&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Storage Gateway now supports FIPS 140-3 validated endpoints over AWS PrivateLink for Tape Gateway and Volume Gateway. Previously, FIPS endpoints were available only over the public internet. Now you can keep FIPS-compliant traffic on the private AWS network, making it easier to use Storage Gateway for regulated workloads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, your Tape Gateway and Volume Gateway can reach the Storage Gateway service endpoints privately through an interface VPC endpoint in your VPC, while using FIPS validated encryption. To get started, you can create a FIPS interface endpoint for Storage Gateway in your VPC, then choose the FIPS VPC endpoint option when activating your gateway. Once activated, your gateway connects to the Storage Gateway service over FIPS validated endpoint on the private AWS network. To activate a gateway with a FIPS PrivateLink endpoint, your gateway must be running software version 3.2.7 or later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This launch is available in the eight AWS Regions where Storage Gateway offers FIPS endpoints: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Canada West (Calgary), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/storagegateway/"&gt;AWS Storage Gateway User Guide&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/storagegateway/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/roles-anywhere"&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) Roles Anywhere&lt;/a&gt; now supports aliases, which are readable names that you assign to trust anchors, profiles, and certificate revocation lists (CRLs). Your workloads can request temporary credentials using a trust anchor or profile alias Amazon Resource Name (ARN) instead of an ARN with a system-generated ID, and you can use alias ARNs in many of the operations that manage these resources. Aliases make configurations easier to read and review: an alias ARN that ends in alias/trust-anchor/production-ca can show which certificate authority (CA) a workload uses.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, workload configurations, such as credential helper settings in machine images and configuration management templates, referenced trust anchors and profiles by ARNs that contain system-generated IDs, which differ in every account and Region. With this launch, you can assign the same alias in each account and Region, so one configuration template works across them with only the account and Region values changed. To set an alias, specify an alias name when you create, import, or update a resource using the IAM Roles Anywhere console, AWS SDKs, or AWS CLI.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available at no additional cost in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, the China Regions, and the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Region. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/rolesanywhere/latest/userguide/resource-aliases.html"&gt;Using aliases for trust anchors, profiles, and CRLs&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Roles Anywhere User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/dsql/?refid=f1097432-a884-4027-abe8-3dbd6d9c5cdc"&gt;Amazon Aurora DSQL&lt;/a&gt; now supports database-internal audit logging, a fully managed capability that captures SQL statements executed on your cluster. Previously, AWS CloudTrail recorded who connected to a DSQL cluster, but not what they did once inside it. Now, financial institutions and regulated enterprises can meet production compliance requirements by capturing DDL, DML, DCL, and SELECT statements with full statement text, bind parameters, acting principal (both PostgreSQL role and AWS IAM identity), timestamp, table name, and transaction identifier.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With audit logging, you can feed SIEM systems directly from Amazon S3, satisfy auditor requirements for change accountability, and reconstruct events after security incidents. Logs are delivered as structured JSON to your S3 bucket via CloudWatch Vended Logs. Configuration requires a single SQL setting—no extension to load and no log-shipping pipeline to build.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Audit logging is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora DSQL is available. Audit logging will incur Aurora DSQL DPU charges, charges for log delivery through CloudWatch Logs, and charges for storing audit records in Amazon S3. For pricing information, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/dsql/pricing/"&gt;Aurora DSQL pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aurora-dsql/latest/userguide/audit-logging.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;audit logging&lt;/a&gt; in the Aurora DSQL User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Hub Network Scanning now scans Amazon RDS DB instances to identify those that are reachable from the internet. Network Scanning automatically discovers existing and new RDS DB instances in your accounts and scans each instance that has Public Access configured. For each reachable port associated with a DB instance, Security Hub creates a finding with details about the instance and the reachable port.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;RDS scanning gives you more visibility into which resources in your environment are reachable from the internet. You can then investigate and update their access settings as needed. RDS scanning is turned on automatically for all customers who already have Network Scanning enabled.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Network Scanning probes your resources from the internet to detect reachability, beyond what security group rules and route tables suggest. Security Hub Exposures then correlates these findings with other findings and resource configurations to assess broader risk.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Existing Security Hub customers can enable Network Scanning in individual accounts and Regions, or across an organization with a configuration policy. For new Security Hub customers, Network Scanning is on by default. Network Scanning is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions that support Security Hub. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-network-scanning.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;. For the full list of Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regional Services List&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-093-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/01/2026 11:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SageMaker Python SDK's @step and @remote decorator pipeline component uses an HMAC key to protect the integrity of serialized function payloads stored in S3. We identified an issue where the HMAC secret key is stored in cleartext within pipeline definitions and accessible via the DescribePipeline API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This allows an actor with a role in that account that has permissions to invoke DescribePipeline to extract the key, create cloud-pickled payloads with valid HMACs, and overwrite S3 objects, achieving code execution in another user's pipeline execution context within the same AWS account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - HMAC Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v3 &amp;lt; v3.11.0&lt;br&gt; - HMAC Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v2 &amp;lt; v2.256.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall now supports IP validation, extending domain-based filtering beyond HTTP/HTTPS to protocols such as SFTP and SSH. This new capability validates that a connection's destination IP address was resolved through your VPC's Amazon Route 53 VPC Resolver, closing security gaps where clients could bypass domain rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature enables Network Firewall customers to implement domain filtering rules for any protocol, not just HTTP/HTTPS, providing more flexible security policies than IP-based rules alone. It simplifies protection against Server Name Identifier (SNI) spoofing by validating the IPs resolved by the Route 53 VPC Resolver against the SNI, ensuring traffic only reaches legitimately resolved destinations. Organizations can now enforce consistent domain-based security policies across all outbound traffic types, from web applications to file transfer protocols, without complex workarounds. Additionally, Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall can be used with your VPC Resolver for extended DNS security.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resolved IP validation is available in all AWS commercial regions where AWS Network Firewall and Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall are available, at no additional charge. For a full list of supported regions, visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?f=eJxVirEKg0AQBX9FXq1FWiGFjZAmRVKkcC0uusji4cneqYj477lcEUg3b94c8Gy5C9w_eBA3eZRoCIsv2PhQXAgt8l9TrUaseYuVsMewmvbsT-WY1c2sYa_FBtbYHIRAKJs2J8wRCE4JcQxfG98xSc-6Sse3Pn0uuWviNXH1emZ3DpvTMatFeTPWEs72xPkBR_VAow&amp;amp;tab=service-feature" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and service &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/route53-resolved-ip-validation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty now supports optional detection rules for Runtime Monitoring. You can enable more than 50 Runtime Monitoring rules that help detect process, file, and network activity that might be routine in one environment but a sign of compromise in another, such as modifying sudoers files or disabling audit logging.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The new rules provide detections for Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon EKS, and Amazon ECS on both EC2 and AWS Fargate, and span 13 finding types mapped to MITRE ATT&amp;amp;CK® tactics including persistence, privilege escalation, execution, defense evasion, discovery, exfiltration, and command and control. This release introduces four new finding types: CommandAndControl:Runtime/SuspiciousCommand, CredentialAccess:Runtime/SensitiveFileRead, Discovery:Runtime/SensitiveFileRead, and Exfiltration:Runtime/SuspiciousCommand.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the GuardDuty console. You can enable rules in dry-run mode to evaluate detection efficacy before enabling live detection.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Custom Detection Rules for Runtime Monitoring is available in all AWS commercial Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/custom-detection-rules.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty Custom Detection Rules&lt;/a&gt;. To receive programmatic updates on new Amazon GuardDuty features and threat detections, subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_sns.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Amazon Web Services (AWS) Advanced Ruby Driver Wrapper is now generally available for use with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon RDS&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; PostgreSQL and MySQL-compatible databases. This advanced database driver reduces RDS Blue/Green switchover, Aurora Global database switchover and database failover times, improving application availability. Additionally, it supports multiple authentication mechanisms for your database, including AWS Secrets Manager authentication, and token-based authentication with AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The AWS Advanced Ruby Driver Wrapper builds on top of the community &lt;a href="https://rubygems.org/gems/pg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pg&lt;/a&gt; (PostgreSQL), and the &lt;a href="https://rubygems.org/gems/mysql2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mysql2&lt;/a&gt; (MySQL) drivers to provide enhanced functionality beyond standard database connectivity. The wrapper is natively integrated with Aurora and RDS databases, enabling it to monitor database cluster status and quickly connect to newly promoted writers during unexpected failures that trigger database failovers. Furthermore, the wrapper seamlessly integrates with the ActiveRecord through provided aws_postgresql and aws_mysql2 adapters, so you can enable it without changing your application code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The driver is available as an open-source project under the Apache 2.0 license. Refer to the instructions on the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-advanced-ruby-driver-wrapper" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub repository&lt;/a&gt; to get started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty now supports AWS Organizations declarative policies, enabling you to centrally enable GuardDuty threat detection across every account and Region in your AWS organization. Using an organization policy, you can now apply a centrally managed GuardDuty enablement configuration. The configuration applies to existing accounts and is automatically maintained as new accounts join your organization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Enabling GuardDuty across all relevant accounts and Regions helps ensure comprehensive threat detection coverage. Previously, keeping enablement aligned across a large multi-account, multi-Region environment meant configuring GuardDuty's enablement settings separately in each Region, which could drift over time. Now you can define a central GuardDuty policy from your delegated administrator account that sets an enablement baseline across your organization (at the organization root, OUs, or individual accounts). The policy supports a default configuration that applies in every Region where GuardDuty is available, as well as per-Region overrides for Regions that require different enablement. Enablement set by a policy cannot be overridden via the GuardDuty console or API.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GuardDuty declarative policy support is available in all AWS commercial Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To get started, make sure the delegated administrator has permission to manage GuardDuty policies. Then, sign in to the GuardDuty console and choose Organization policies, or create a policy programmatically using AWS Organizations APIs. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty-organization-policies.html"&gt;Managing accounts using organization policies&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon GuardDuty User Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_guardduty.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty policies&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Organizations User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Hub now offers remediation plans that group related exposure findings sharing a root cause. Instead of addressing each exposure individually, you can now fix a single underlying resource, such as a misconfigured setting or overly permissive policy, and resolve or reduce the severity of multiple exposures at once.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each remediation plan includes prioritization guidance (Critical, High, Medium, or Low), impact assessment, and detailed step-by-step instructions with examples in multiple formats including AWS CLI, Terraform, CloudFormation, Python, and CDK. Security Hub automatically prioritizes plans so those reducing the most risk appear first, helping you focus remediation efforts where they matter most. AI agents can also programmatically consume remediation plans through the API to automate security fixes across your environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Remediation plans are available in every AWS Region where AWS Security Hub is available and at no additional cost under AWS Security Hub Essentials plan. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;​​Amazon Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall support for Palo Alto Networks (PANW) Advanced DNS Security is now generally available across 32 AWS Regions, allowing security teams to detect and block malicious DNS traffic directly from their VPCs using PANW Advanced DNS Security rules without deploying separate firewalls or modifying VPC configurations.​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​​&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-route-53-resolver-dns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Previewed&lt;/a&gt; at AWS Summit New York City, the feature enables security administrators to enforce DNS threat protections (Command and Control, Malware, Phishing, Newly Registered Domains, etc.) from Palo Alto Networks directly using Route 53 DNS Firewall rules to inspect and block malicious DNS query traffic from Amazon VPCs and hybrid cloud (via Route 53 Resolver Endpoints).​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​​With general availability, PANW Advanced DNS Security on DNS Firewall is now supported in all AWS Regions where DNS Firewall is offered. Customers can subscribe to and enable the feature directly via the Route 53 DNS Firewall console, share licenses across organization accounts via AWS License Manager, and associate DNS Firewall rule groups containing PANW rules with VPCs using AWS Resource Access Manager, Route 53 Profiles, or AWS Firewall Manager.​&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​​​To learn more and get started, see Route 53 DNS Firewall &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To view Route 53 DNS Firewall pricing, visit Route 53 &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about the AWS Marketplace listing and pricing for PANW Advanced DNS Security, see the AWS Marketplace &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-lrceyvgks3z6o?sr=0-19&amp;amp;ref_=beagle&amp;amp;applicationId=AWSMPContessa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;listing&lt;/a&gt;.​​&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Hub AI Inventory now supports discovering and cataloging AI assets running on self-hosted instances in Microsoft Azure. This expansion extends Security Hub's existing self-hosted discovery capabilities beyond AWS, enabling central security teams to gain a continuously updated, organization-wide view of AI assets and their security posture across multi-cloud environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Security Hub leverages the software bill of materials (SBOM) analysis from Amazon Inspector, which has been enhanced to identify inference endpoints, models, and AI agents installed on Azure virtual machines, including frameworks such as Ollama, vLLM, Hugging Face TGI, and others. Each discovered AI asset is mapped to its underlying infrastructure and correlated with security findings, enabling teams to filter, group, and query their AI inventory across both AWS and Azure environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost. It is available in all AWS commercial Regions where Security Hub is offered. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-ai-inventory.html"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Cognito now provides a new admin API operation to reset a user's time-based one-time Password (TOTP) multi-factor authentication (MFA) configuration. When users lose access to their TOTP device, administrators can remove the device association, allowing the user to enroll a new device on their next sign-in.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This removes the need to recreate accounts to recover locked-out users if they lose access to their TOTP device. Customers can maintain MFA enforcement while providing a recovery path.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This new capability is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito is available. To get started, access the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_AdminDeleteSoftwareToken.html"&gt;AdminDeleteSoftwareToken&lt;/a&gt; API using the AWS CLI, SDKs, or APIs. See the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-mfa-totp.html#user-pool-settings-mfa-totp-remove"&gt;developer guide&lt;/a&gt; for instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Inspector has expanded its vulnerability management capabilities with runtime reachability analysis for findings originating from Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) and Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instances. Previously, Inspector reported every vulnerability found in a resource, including packages that were never used by applications. Security teams had to manually assess which vulnerabilities actually posed risk in their deployed environments. Now, Inspector automatically identifies which vulnerabilities are potentially reachable by analyzing package placement in container images and process activity on EC2 instances.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With runtime reachability analysis, you can focus remediation efforts on vulnerabilities that matter most to your security posture. For container images, Inspector determines whether a vulnerable package is within your application's dockerfile paths defined by WORKDIR, ENTRYPOINT, or CMD instructions. For EC2 instances, Inspector identifies whether a vulnerable package was observed running as a process during the most recent scan. For EC2, customers must use the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/inspector-vm-scanner.html" target="_blank"&gt;Inspector VM Scanner&lt;/a&gt; for agent-based scanning to get this capability. This helps organizations managing large container fleets or complex applications prioritize remediation by distinguishing between vulnerabilities that are potentially reachable versus those in unused dependencies, reducing noise and enabling development and security teams to work more efficiently. Newly generated findings will have runtime reachability analysis included, and backfill of existing findings will be completed over time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Runtime reachability for Inspector findings is available at no additional charge and is available in all AWS Regions where Inspector is already available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/"&gt;Amazon Inspector product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Agent Registry now supports registering &lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/webbotauth/about/" target="_blank"&gt;WebBotAuth&lt;/a&gt; (WBA) bots as a new record type, making it easier to verify legitimate bot traffic through a standardized protocol. Previously, organizations lacked a standardized way to distinguish verified bots from malicious traffic. Now, bots can prove domain ownership through WebBotAuth, and you can integrate this verification with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/aws-waf-bot-control-onboarding/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS WAF Bot Control&lt;/a&gt; to write rules that combine verification status and bot identity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This new capability gives content providers and application developers fine-grained control over which bots can access your resources. You can enhance security and improve traffic management by distinguishing legitimate bots from malicious actors. For example, you can allow WBA verified search engine crawlers while blocking unverified traffic or create custom access policies based on both bot identity and verification status.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Agent Registry is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/registry.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Agent Registry documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-bot-control.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS WAF Bot Control documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now associate AWS Network Firewall with NAT Gateway and use it both as an explicit proxy and a transparent firewall with native network address translation. You can use this capability with existing Network Firewall features to filter traffic to and from your applications and prevent data exfiltration and malware injection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall is a stateful, managed, network firewall and intrusion detection and prevention service for your VPC. Customers use it to filter their traffic and secure AWS resources. With this launch, you can configure Network Firewall with native NAT Gateway association and use it for both explicit proxy and routed filtering functionalities. Both these functionalities are governed by a single security policy that you maintain centrally. This capability works with existing Network Firewall features including managed rule groups from Amazon or Marketplace, active threat defense, Geo-IP filtering, URL and domain category filtering, container attribute-based rules for Amazon EKS and Amazon ECS, etc. You can access Network Firewall from any VPC in your environment, even the ones with overlapping IP CIDR space, using VPC endpoints. Your traffic reaches the firewall via the VPC endpoint and the filtered traffic exits directly from the NAT Gateway using its IP address.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Frankfurt), Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Europe (Stockholm), Asia Pacific (Thailand), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Europe (Spain). To learn more about Network Firewall proxy and NAT Gateway association, please visit Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/what-is-aws-network-firewall.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall attached to AWS Transit Gateway now allows you to use context-aware rules in your firewall policy. Network Firewall is a managed firewall and intrusion prevention service that inspects and filters traffic across your virtual private clouds (VPCs). With context-aware rules, you write rules that reference context from VPC resources instead of IP addresses. This makes it easier to apply consistent security controls as your organization grows. Existing rules automatically cover new workloads, so you don't need to update rules manually.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Context-aware rules match traffic using VPCs, subnets, security groups, and Transit Gateway attachment identifiers, collectively known as context identifiers. You can reference context identifiers in a rule's source for any protocol, and in its destination for TCP-based protocols such as HTTP, TLS, and SSH. Flow and alert logs include context identifiers, so you can investigate activity based on context.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Context-aware rules are available at no additional charge in all commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For a full list of supported Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?f=eJxVirEKg0AQBX9FXq1FWiGFjZAmRVKkcC0uusji4cneqYj477lcEUg3b94c8Gy5C9w_eBA3eZRoCIsv2PhQXAgt8l9TrUaseYuVsMewmvbsT-WY1c2sYa_FBtbYHIRAKJs2J8wRCE4JcQxfG98xSc-6Sse3Pn0uuWviNXH1emZ3DpvTMatFeTPWEs72xPkBR_VAow&amp;amp;tab=service-feature&amp;amp;refid=d08148e0-c462-4c19-ae34-467de69e31c6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/context-aware-rules.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Hub Network Scanning feature is now supported in AWS GovCloud (US).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Network Scanning probes your resources from the internet to detect actual reachability, not just what could be reachable based on security group rules and route tables. It discovers public IP addresses, virtual machines, and load balancers across your AWS environments, identifies reachable ports, and determines what services are running behind them. This complements Security Hub's existing network reachability findings, which identify configurations that could make a resource reachable from the internet. Network Scanning confirms that configurations do exist which make a resource reachable from the internet. Each reachable port generates a Security Hub finding with evidence of the port and service discovered. Security Hub Exposures then automatically correlates these findings with other findings and resource configurations to determine broader risk.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Existing customers can enable Network Scanning in individual accounts and regions, or across an organization via a configuration policy. For new Security Hub customers, Network Scanning is on by default. Network Scanning is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost in all AWS commercial and GovCloud (US) Regions that support Security Hub. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-network-scanning.html"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;. For the full list of Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regional Services List&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Sign-In now supports OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693), enabling applications to exchange third-party OpenID Connect (OIDC) tokens for short-lived, AWS service-scoped OAuth access tokens. This allows customers to securely integrate third-party applications with supported AWS services without sharing AWS credentials, while maintaining access controls through AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available for supported &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/oauth-services-supporting.html"&gt;AWS services that use AWS Sign-In OAuth&lt;/a&gt;, enabling customers to use existing identity providers to securely access AWS services without managing additional credentials.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;To learn more&lt;/b&gt;, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/oauth-sign-in-overview.html#oauth-sign-in-authorization-models"&gt;AWS Sign-In User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/aws-mcp-server.html#aws-mcp-server-auth-models"&gt;AWS MCP Server User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/glue-catalog-oauth-iceberg-rest/"&gt;AWS Glue launch announcement&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Braket is now in scope for FedRAMP Class C in AWS US Regions where Braket is available. The Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program (FedRAMP) is a US government-wide program that delivers a standard approach to the security assessment, authorization, and continuous monitoring of cloud products and services.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With FedRAMP Class C authorization, government customers and the organizations that serve them can use Amazon Braket to explore quantum computing within an authorized boundary. The AWS-operated components of Braket, including the API and SDK, job orchestration and management, compilation, and result storage in Amazon S3, are within the FedRAMP-certified boundary, while quantum workloads execute on third-party Quantum Hardware Providers (QHPs) outside that boundary. All data sent to QHPs meets AWS' privacy and security requirements including removal of all personally identifiable information, data encryption in transit and at rest, and adherence to AWS' standard data retention and deletion policies.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about FedRAMP, visit AWS FedRAMP services and the FedRAMP Marketplace. To learn more about Amazon Braket, visit the Braket product page and Getting Started on Braket resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Regional Availability: US East (N. Virginia), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Security Hub adds resource criticality to exposure prioritization, helping central security teams focus on the exposures that threaten their most business-critical resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can set criticality with an automation rule, for example marking every compute resource in a specific account as Very High. The rule applies to resources already in your inventory and to new resources as Security Hub discovers them. Security Hub also infers criticality from resource tags and resource names, with no configuration required, and records which source determined the value. Criticality appears as a column on the Resources page for sorting, filtering, and grouping, and on the Impact assessment tab of an exposure finding. Resource criticality is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost and is always on for all customers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resource criticality is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost and requires no new enablement. To learn more, see&amp;nbsp; &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/what-is-securityhub-v2.html"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For a list of AWS Regions where Security Hub is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS HealthOmics now supports IAM session policies, enabling you to restrict permissions for individual runs without creating and managing multiple IAM roles. Until now, there was no way to dynamically scope down permissions for a single run, requiring you to create a separate IAM role for each tenant or run. AWS HealthOmics is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs with fully managed bioinformatics workflows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An IAM session policy is an inline policy that limits the maximum permissions of a run without modifying the underlying service role. Your effective permissions during a run are the intersection of permissions allowed by both the underlying identity-based policy and the temporary session policy. For example, if you operate a multi-tenant application, you can pass a session policy that limits a run's access to only that tenant's Amazon S3 buckets, without provisioning a dedicated IAM role for that tenant. You can also use IAM session policies to grant temporary access to specific Amazon S3 objects for a single run, and isolate access to sensitive resources on a per-run basis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;IAM session policy support is available in all AWS Regions where AWS HealthOmics is available: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Tokyo, Singapore, Seoul). To learn how to configure IAM session policies for your runs, visit the Permissions section of the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/permissions-service.html"&gt;AWS HealthOmics User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about the service, visit &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now configure Amazon API Gateway REST APIs to present an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate to your backend during the TLS handshake, enabling mutual TLS (mTLS). Previously, API Gateway could present only a self-signed certificate that it generated; now you can use a certificate signed by a certificate authority you trust. Your integration endpoint validates this certificate during the handshake to confirm the connection comes from your API.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can import a certificate into ACM from your existing public key infrastructure (PKI), or have ACM issue and manage one for you through AWS Private Certificate Authority. When a certificate is reimported or renewed in ACM, API Gateway propagates the update automatically, with no redeployment and no downtime. Combined with the existing inbound mTLS support for client connections, you can apply mutual authentication across both the client-to-API and API-to-backend connections, which is a common requirement in financial services, healthcare, and other regulated or zero-trust environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutual TLS for backend integrations is available in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, where API Gateway REST APIs are available. You can configure it through the API Gateway console, AWS CLI, or AWS CloudFormation. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/rest-api-backend-authentication.html"&gt;Amazon API Gateway documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/bring-your-own-client-certificate-for-backend-mtls-in-amazon-api-gateway/"&gt;AWS blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-004-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/02/02 14:30 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-1777 - Exposed HMAC in SageMaker Python SDK&lt;br&gt; SageMaker Python SDK’s remote functions feature uses a per‑job HMAC key to protect the integrity of serialized functions, arguments, and results stored in S3. We identified an issue where the HMAC secret key is stored in environment variables and disclosed via the DescribeTrainingJob API. This allows third parties with DescribeTrainingJob permissions to extract the key, forge cloud-pickled payloads with valid HMACs, and overwrite S3 objects.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-1778 - Insecure TLS Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK&lt;br&gt; SageMaker Python SDK is an open source library for training and deploying machine learning models on Amazon SageMaker. We identified an issue where SSL certificate verification was globally disabled in the Triton Python backend. This configuration was introduced to work around SSL errors during model downloads from public sources (e.g., TorchVision) and it affected all HTTPS connections when the Triton Python model was imported.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;- HMAC Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v3 &amp;lt; v3.2.0&lt;br&gt; - HMAC Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v2 &amp;lt; v2.256.0&lt;br&gt; - Insecure TLS Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v3 &amp;lt; v3.1.1&lt;br&gt; - Insecure TLS Configuration in SageMaker Python SDK v2 &amp;lt; v2.256.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) now enables AWS workloads in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Region to securely authenticate with external services using short-lived JSON Web Tokens (JWTs). The AWS European Sovereign Cloud is an independent cloud for Europe entirely located within the European Union (EU), designed to help customers meet their evolving sovereignty requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt; With outbound identity federation, AWS workloads can securely authenticate with third-party cloud providers, SaaS providers, and self-hosted applications without using long-term credentials or implementing complex workarounds. Customers can exchange their AWS IAM credentials for cryptographically signed, short-lived JWTs, providing a simple and secure mechanism to access external services. These tokens contain rich context about the AWS workloads, enabling external services to implement fine-grained access control. Administrators can control access to token generation and enforce token properties (such as lifetime, audience, and signing algorithms) using IAM policies and audit token usage using CloudTrail logs, allowing them to meet their organization's security and compliance requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt; To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/identity/federation/outbound-federation/"&gt;outbound identity federation product page&lt;/a&gt;, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_providers_outbound.html"&gt;IAM user guide&lt;/a&gt;, or read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/simplify-access-to-external-services-using-aws-iam-outbound-identity-federation/"&gt;AWS News Blog Post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS WAF now offers simplified pricing that follows what you protect instead of how you configure it. Previously, you paid separate charges for each web ACL, rule, rule group subscription, and capacity tier, making every security decision a billing calculation. Now, your bill follows three dimensions: the resources you protect, the traffic AWS WAF inspects, and the level of protection you apply. This makes the cost-benefit trade-off easy to understand, and adding or changing rules no longer changes your rate.&amp;nbsp;This new pricing goes into effect on Feb 1, 2027.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The new model makes protection costs predictable before you deploy. Each web ACL now includes up to 2,500 WCU at no extra charge, up from 1,500, and you can add rules such as Bot Control common protections or the Anti-DDoS rule group without additional fees. You pay a resource fee for each protected resource, a base request fee for every request AWS WAF inspects, and a premium request fee only for traffic evaluated by advanced protections like Bot Control targeted protections, Fraud Control, API Security, and AI Security.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS WAF's new pricing is effective from Feb 1, 2027 in all AWS commercial Regions, including Cape Town, Hong Kong, Hyderabad, Jakarta, Malaysia, Melbourne, Mumbai, New Zealand, Osaka, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Taipei, Thailand, Tokyo, Calgary, Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Milan, Paris, Spain, Stockholm, Zurich, Tel Aviv, Sao Paulo, N. Virginia, Ohio, N. California, Oregon, as well as in AWS GovCloud, China Regions, and AWS Isolated Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/waf/pricing/"&gt;AWS WAF pricing page&lt;/a&gt; and review the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/"&gt;AWS WAF Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;as well as the blog post&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-aws-waf-simplified-pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF pricing now follows what you protect, not how you configure it&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated API discovery and protection are now generally available, enabling you to discover every API behind Amazon API Gateway, Application Load Balancers, and Amazon CloudFront across your AWS Organization and protect them using AWS WAF.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Discovery is available through the new API Gateway API Portal, which identifies the APIs automatically from traffic logs and service configuration records, with no agents to deploy or logs to forward. The API Portal classifies the APIs it finds, identifies zombie and shadow APIs, evaluates each API's security posture, and reports findings, including the ones that are missing WAF protections. For unprotected APIs, customers can enable protection using WAF.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Protection is available through the new AWS WAF API Security managed rule group, which protects your APIs against the most exploited API attack categories: Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA), where the attackers change record IDs in requests to read other users' data, Broken Authentication and Credential Stuffing, where the attackers forge tokens or try stolen passwords at scale, and API Reconnaissance and Enumeration, where the attackers map an API by guessing hidden routes and probing for exposed docs. The events detected by WAF are consolidated into the API Portal findings to provide a complete security picture of your APIs. You can use API Security alongside other AWS Managed Rules or your own custom rules to protect your applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Together, API Portal and API Security provide you a complete solution from discovery of all APIs in your AWS environment to protecting them, without having to write any custom rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;API Portal is available in 32 AWS Regions. See the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/faqs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon API Gateway FAQ&lt;/a&gt; for the list. API Security is available in all AWS Regions where AWS WAF is available, except the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, the China Regions, and the AWS European Sovereign Cloud.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the following resources:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/waf/"&gt;AWS WAF product page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-api-security.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF API Security in the AWS WAF Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apiportal.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API Portal Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-api-and-ai-security-on-aws-from-discovery-to-protection/"&gt;Introducing API and AI security on AWS, from discovery to protection blog post&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS WAF now gives you one place to find and stop unwanted traffic across every application in your account. The new AWS WAF security analytics dashboard brings together traffic from all of your protection packs (web ACLs), whether they protect Regional resources or Amazon CloudFront distributions, into a single view. The dashboard also lets you search request logs right in the console, with no logging pipeline to build, and includes 21 days of full-fidelity request data at no additional cost. It is available alongside the existing dashboard for each protection pack (WebACL), which continues to work as it does today.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Until now, spotting a trend across your applications, such as a bot campaign hitting several endpoints or a spike in blocked requests, meant opening each web ACL in turn or individually querying AWS WAF logs. With the AWS WAF security analytics dashboard, you can analyze traffic data from every protection pack (WebACL) at once. You can filter and group traffic by dimensions such as client IP address, country, ASN, user agent, JA4 fingerprint, host, path, rule triggered, and labels. You can see which endpoints are affected and how traffic is changing across all of them. The data is full-fidelity rather than sampled, and filters apply across the whole dashboard.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Investigating individual requests has also required delivering AWS WAF logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon S3, or Amazon Data Firehose and writing queries. AWS WAF Security analytics dashboard includes a logs widget that lets you search and explore raw request logs in the console, down to the full HTTP request, with no log delivery to configure. When you find unwanted traffic, you can block an IP address or a country directly from the page. You can also filter by the labels that managed rules attach to requests to see what a rule in Count mode would block before you enforce it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The AWS WAF security analytics dashboard is available at no additional cost in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS WAF is available. This feature is not available in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions and China Regions. To get started, open the AWS WAF console and choose Security analytics in the navigation pane.&amp;nbsp;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/web-acl-security-analytics.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-the-new-aws-waf-security-analytics-dashboard/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;launch blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS) announces GetScopedCredentials, a new API that lets you scope down the permissions of existing temporary security credentials. With GetScopedCredentials, you can give your agents, sub agents and workloads their own set of credentials with only the permissions they need.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can call GetScopedCredentials with the temporary security credentials you already use to reduce permissions, session duration, or both. The resulting credentials preserve the caller's original identity, and permissions and session duration are monotonically narrowed so they never exceed those of the caller. Access to the API is controlled with IAM policies, and each call is logged to AWS CloudTrail for traceability. These capabilities make it easier to scope down permissions at runtime without changing how you manage identities.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;GetScopedCredentials is available in all AWS commercial Regions, AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Regions and China Regions. To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_GetScopedCredentials.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GetScopedCredentials API reference&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_temp_control-access_getscopedcredentials.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Permissions for GetScopedCredentials&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; now supports OAuth dynamic client registration (DCR, RFC 7591). Amazon Cognito helps you implement secure sign-in and access control for users, AI agents, and microservices in minutes. With DCR support, Model Context Protocol (MCP) clients and AI agents can register themselves at runtime. Previously, you had to create an application client in advance for each AI client before it could connect to an MCP server that uses Cognito for authorization.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With DCR, Cognito advertises a registration endpoint in its OAuth authorization server metadata. MCP clients that support dynamic client registration discover the endpoint, register to receive a client ID, and then sign users in. You control who can register: either require registration requests to present an access token with a registration scope, or turn on open registration so that any client can register. Dynamically registered clients appear with your other app clients with a client ID prefix and a client type you can filter on, so you can manage them with the same console and APIs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;DCR is available on the Essentials and Plus feature plans in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-dynamic-client-registration.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Dynamic client registration&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Cognito Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; now supports point-in-time recovery (PITR), so you can restore the user data in a user pool to an earlier time. Amazon Cognito helps you implement secure sign-in and access control for users, AI agents, and microservices. Previously, you had to build and maintain your own export and restore process, and restored users received new identifiers and had to reset their passwords.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With PITR, you choose a recovery point, preview how many users, groups, multi-factor authentication (MFA) settings, and passkeys the recovery will create, update, or delete, and then start or cancel the recovery. Recovered users keep their identifiers, passwords, MFA settings, and passkeys, so they can sign in without resetting their credentials. For example, if a script deletes or modifies users by mistake, you can restore the user data to the moment before it ran.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;PITR is enabled automatically for user pools on the Essentials and Plus feature plans at no additional charge. The recovery window is up to 1 day on Essentials and up to 14 days on Plus. You can initiate and manage a recovery using the AWS Management Console, the Amazon Cognito API, AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI), or AWS SDKs. PITR is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-point-in-time-recovery.html"&gt;Point-in-time recovery&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Cognito Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-186-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/17/2026 11:00 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A time-of-check time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition in the local port selection in AWS efs-utils (amazon-efs-utils) before 3.3.5 might allow a user that can start EFS mounts on a shared host to read and write a different Amazon EFS or S3 Files file system that is mounted on that same host, under that mount's IAM identity and access point, via concurrent mount operations. Only file systems mounted on the same host at the same time can be affected.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - amazon-efs-utils &amp;lt; v3.3.5&lt;br&gt; - aws-efs-csi-driver &amp;lt; v3.5.4&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-186-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The open source &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-workload-credentials-provider" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Workload Credentials Provider&lt;/a&gt; can now export AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificates to applications running in containers on Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS), including AWS Fargate, Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS), and Docker on Amazon EC2. You can now automate certificate deployment and renewal for containerized TLS workloads the same way you do on EC2 instances, on both Linux and Windows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The provider runs as a sidecar in your ECS task or EKS pod. It exports each certificate and private key to a shared volume and writes the renewed files when ACM rotates the certificate. For TLS servers that don't reload certificate files on their own, such as NGINX, Apache, HAProxy, or IIS, a companion watcher in the TLS container runs your reload command after each renewal. The watcher needs no AWS credentials. TLS servers that reload automatically, such as Envoy, Traefik, and Caddy, need only the shared volume. Access is controlled through IAM: the sidecar assumes a per-workload role that is allowed to export only that workload's certificates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, download the provider and watcher binaries for Linux (x86_64 and Arm64) or Windows, and follow the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-workload-credentials-provider#-running-as-a-container-sidecar-" target="_blank"&gt;setup steps and ECS and EKS examples&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Workload Credentials Provider README on GitHub. The provider is available at no additional charge, and standard &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/certificate-manager/pricing/"&gt;ACM charges&lt;/a&gt; apply to exported certificates. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Certificate Manager documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Cognito now supports session management for user pools, giving you visibility into and control over the sessions your users establish when they sign in to your applications. Single sign-on (SSO) sessions are created when a user signs in through managed login and let them access other app clients without re-authenticating. Token sessions are created when a user signs in to an app client and receives tokens. Session management lets you list a user's active SSO and token sessions across all devices and app clients and revoke any of them on demand. You can also now configure how long each app client reuses an SSO session before requiring re-authentication, instead of the previous fixed one-hour duration.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Each session includes details such as session type, app client ID, device type, last activity time and location, and initial sign-in time, so you can build your own end-user or admin session experiences. You can revoke an individual session, optionally with its linked sessions, or use global sign-out to end all sessions associated with one user. You can also set a maximum number of active token sessions per user.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Session management is available for user pools in the Essentials or Plus feature plans in all AWS Regions where Amazon Cognito is available.&lt;i&gt; &lt;/i&gt;You can manage sessions using the AWS Management Console, API, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS Software Development Kits, and configure settings with AWS CloudFormation. Visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/managing-user-sessions.html"&gt;Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_Operations.html"&gt;API Reference&lt;/a&gt; for details.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey now supports public endpoints in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. A public endpoint lets you connect to your cache from a serverless function or any application running outside a VPC, without setting up a VPN, a bastion host, or an SSH tunnel.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With a public endpoint, ElastiCache Serverless gives you a fully managed cache with no VPC to configure and no infrastructure to provision, and you can create one in under a minute. Every connection uses IAM authentication over TLS 1.3, so there's no password to store or rotate. Connect using Valkey GLIDE 2.2 or later, which has built-in IAM support, or another Valkey client paired with the Developer Toolkit for ElastiCache, an open-source library that generates and refreshes IAM authentication tokens for you.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, public endpoints for ElastiCache Serverless are now available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. There is no additional charge for using public endpoints beyond standard ElastiCache Serverless pricing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a Valkey 9.0 or later serverless cache with a public endpoint using the AWS Management Console, AWS SDK, or AWS CLI. To learn more about public endpoints, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/serverless-public-endpoints-chapter.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Create a Valkey serverless cache with a public endpoint&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Key Management Service (KMS) now supports Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM), a post-quantum cryptography standard under &lt;a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final"&gt;NIST FIPS 203&lt;/a&gt;, which&amp;nbsp;enables two parties to establish a shared secret.&amp;nbsp;NIST designed ML-KEM to replace the classical key-establishment schemes, like DH/ECDH key agreement, and RSA-based key transport, in their strategic cryptographic roadmap&amp;nbsp;(see &lt;a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8547/ipd"&gt;NIST IR 8547&lt;/a&gt;).&amp;nbsp;ML-KEM addresses the "harvest now, decrypt later" threat to those classical schemes, where an adversary records the key-establishment exchange today and recovers the shared secret once sufficiently powerful quantum computers become available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;KMS supports all three ML-KEM key specs defined in NIST FIPS 203: ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024. The Encapsulate API uses the public key to produce a random 32-byte shared secret and its ciphertext form. The Decapsulate API recovers the shared secret from ciphertext using the corresponding private key. KMS protects the private key of an ML-KEM key pair in FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validated hardware security modules (HSMs). You can use the GetPublicKey API to export the public key and distribute it to partners, client applications, or external systems for performing the encapsulate operation locally using cryptographic libraries like AWS-LC, OpenSSL, or Bouncy Castle.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ML-KEM is available in all AWS Regions where KMS is available, except the the Middle East (UAE), Middle East (Bahrain), and China Regions. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/pqkeys.html#mlkem"&gt;ML-KEM keys in AWS KMS&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/symm-asymm-choose-key-spec.html#key-spec-mlkem"&gt;Key spec reference&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the AWS KMS Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/"&gt;AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)&lt;/a&gt; has increased the default quota for managed policies per role from 10 to 20.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This higher default quota reduces the need to make Service Quota requests when following IAM best practices like separating permissions into purpose-specific policies or when onboarding to AWS Partner products that require attaching additional managed policies. If you need more than 20 managed policies per role, you can &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/servicequotas/latest/userguide/request-quota-increase.html"&gt;request a quota increase&lt;/a&gt; up to 25 using Service Quotas.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This change is available in all commercial AWS Regions, AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions and applies automatically to all IAM roles in your account with no action required. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_iam-quotas.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;IAM and AWS STS quotas&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Global Accelerator now preserves the client IP address for traffic sent to Network Load Balancer (NLB) endpoints with TLS listeners. You can terminate TLS at your NLB and still see the original client IP address in your application. This means you can apply security groups, geo-blocking, rate limiting, fraud detection, audit logging, allowlists, and compliance controls based on the real client IP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, you couldn't add an NLB with a TLS listener to an accelerator that had client IP address preservation enabled. You had to choose between terminating TLS at the NLB and seeing the real client IP address. With this launch, you get both. This helps with workloads that need TLS termination at Layer 4, such as non-HTTP TCP protocols, applications that require static or Elastic IP addresses, and high-throughput traffic where a Layer 7 load balancer isn't a fit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To enable this feature, add an NLB with a TLS listener as an endpoint to an accelerator with client IP address preservation setting turned on, the same way you add any NLB. No additional configuration is required! Client IP address preservation for NLBs with TLS listeners is available at no additional charge in all AWS commercial Regions, excluding the AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. Standard Global Accelerator pricing applies.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/latest/dg/preserve-client-ip-address.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Client IP address preservation&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Global Accelerator Developer Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/create-listener.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Create a listener for your Network Load Balancer&lt;/a&gt; in the User Guide for Network Load Balancers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) Serverless now supports Apache Airflow connections and variables, so your workflows can reach databases, APIs, and other external systems. You can manage credentials and configuration outside your workflow code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You store sensitive connections and variables in the secrets backend of your choice, such as AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, or your own custom backend, configure it on your workflow, and grant your workflow execution role access. Tasks retrieve them at run time through standard Apache Airflow methods. You can define non-secret values as environment variables on the workflow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon MWAA Serverless is available. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/what-is-mwaa-serverless.html#regions" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon MWAA Serverless Regions&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/connections.html" target="_blank"&gt;Managing connections and variables&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/workflows-environment-variables.html" target="_blank"&gt;Using environment variables in Amazon MWAA Serverless workflows&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon MWAA Serverless User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-099-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/04/2026 11:45 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Amazon EFS CSI Driver is an open-source Kubernetes Container Storage Interface (CSI) driver that lets Kubernetes workloads use Amazon EFS file systems. We identified CVE-2026-85781, an issue in the driver's volume-deletion logic. When the controller is configured with the non-default --delete-access-point-root-dir=true option, it did not verify that the EFS access point referenced by a PersistentVolume's volume handle belonged to the file system referenced by that same volume handle. An authenticated Kubernetes user with permission to create PersistentVolumes could use a crafted volume handle to cause the controller to recursively delete directories from an Amazon EFS file system that the user was not otherwise authorized to modify. This issue does not affect Amazon EFS itself; Amazon EFS access controls operated as designed.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;=3.4.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS HealthOmics now supports sharing VPCs across accounts, enabling your workflows to access resources in Amazon Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) managed by other AWS accounts with AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt; is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs at scale with fully managed bioinformatics workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With shared VPCs, customers can centralize networking administration by sharing subnets and security groups from a single VPC across multiple accounts in their organization, and HealthOmics then places each account's workflows directly into those shared subnets. With cross-account VPC sharing, multiple accounts run inside one centrally managed VPC, instead of duplicating endpoints across VPCs. Workflows in participating accounts can access data privately without separate VPC peering connections or duplicated VPC endpoints. Previously, customers with centralized networking had to create separate AWS PrivateLink VPC endpoints in each VPC and maintain individual route tables using VPC peering. Cross-account VPC sharing reduces infrastructure overhead and simplifies network management for organizations with centralized networking while allowing researchers and developers to run bioinformatics workflows in their own accounts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Shared VPCs is available in all Regions where AWS HealthOmics is available: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Singapore, Seoul, Tokyo). To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/workflows-vpc-cross-account.html"&gt;AWS HealthOmics User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty announces an expansion of RDS Protection that extends threat detection beyond login anomalies to identify data exfiltration and data destruction attacks targeting Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL databases. Available as RDS Protection for data activity, this new capability uses machine learning (ML)-based anomaly detection and integrated threat intelligence to continuously monitor database query patterns, helping you detect unusual data activity without requiring any agents, additional infrastructure, or database configuration changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As attackers increasingly target databases to exfiltrate or destroy critical data after obtaining valid credentials through phishing, social engineering, or misconfiguration, their malicious database activity can appear indistinguishable from legitimate application behavior.&amp;nbsp; RDS Protection for data activity establishes behavioral baselines for normal query patterns enabling GuardDuty to detect threats that traditional tools miss.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can enable GuardDuty RDS Protection for data activity as an add-on to RDS Protection for login activity. With a single click, they can enable it for their entire organization in the GuardDuty console, or programmatically via APIs, SDKs, CLI, or AWS CloudFormation. A 30-day free trial is available per account per Region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available today in all AWS commercial Regions and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, where Amazon GuardDuty RDS Protection is offered with support for Aurora PostgreSQL and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. For supported versions visit GuardDuty RDS Protection &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/rds-protection.html#rds-pro-supported-db"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more and start your free trial, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/guardduty"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Private CA announces detailed certificate issuance logs, a new AWS CloudTrail service event that records the complete certificate content, issuing CA information, requester identity, and signing status for every issuance. You can use these events for compliance auditing, certificate inventory, algorithm migration tracking, and failed-issuance monitoring. Previously, the CloudTrail management event for the IssueCertificate API confirmed that the API call succeeded by providing a certificate ARN but did not capture the certificate content, information about the CA that signed it, or issuances that failed before signing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new IssueCertificateDetails event captures the complete to-be-signed (TBS) certificate with all X.509 fields and extensions, plus convenience fields for the subject, issuer, serial number, validity period, template, and signing algorithm. Events are emitted for both successful and failed issuance, so pre-signing failures such as name constraints violations now produce a record with a failure explanation. Each event identifies the requester: the account and IAM principal for direct API callers, or the service principal for certificates issued through AWS Private CA connectors or integrated AWS services. In cross-account configurations, the event is delivered to the CA owner account.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The event is delivered automatically as a CloudTrail management event, with no configuration or opt-in required and no additional cost beyond standard AWS CloudTrail pricing. Because it flows through CloudTrail, you can act on it in real time with Amazon EventBridge or query it in batch with Amazon Athena for certificate auditing, inventory, tracking, and monitoring.&amp;nbsp;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Private CA is offered. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/privateca/latest/userguide/logging-using-cloudtrail-pca.html#pca-service-events" style="background-color: rgb(255,255,255);"&gt;AWS Private CA User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Transcribe now lets you encrypt your custom vocabularies, custom vocabulary filters, and custom language models at rest with a customer-managed AWS KMS key that you own and control.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, these custom resources were always encrypted with an AWS owned key. Now you can supply your own symmetric AWS KMS key when you create or update these resources, so the artifacts Transcribe stores on your behalf are encrypted under a key in your account. If you do not provide a key, your resources continue to be encrypted with an AWS owned key—no action is required unless you opt in.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With a customer-managed key, you control key permissions and decide exactly which principals and services can encrypt and decrypt your custom resources. Every use of your key is logged in AWS CloudTrail, giving you a full audit trail for compliance and monitoring. You can also disable the key or transition resources to a different key to revoke access on your own schedule.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Transcribe is offered. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transcribe/latest/dg/what-is.html"&gt;Amazon Transcribe Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The&amp;nbsp;Amazon Elastic VMware Service (EVS) service is now in scope for FedRAMP Class C (formerly Moderate baseline) in all United States Regions.&amp;nbsp;
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 The&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/fedramp/"&gt;Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program&amp;nbsp;(FedRAMP)&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a US government-wide program that delivers a standard approach to the security assessment, authorization, and continuous monitoring for cloud products and services.&amp;nbsp;Amazon EVS lets you run the latest VMware Cloud Foundation (VCF) software directly within your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) on EC2 bare-metal instances. You can set up a complete VCF environment in just a few hours, enabling rapid workload migration to AWS to help you eliminate aging infrastructure, reduce operational risks, and meet critical timelines for exiting your data center.
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 To learn more, visit the FedRAMP&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/services-in-scope/FedRAMP/"&gt;AWS services in scope page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about Amazon EVS, visit the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/evs/"&gt;product detail page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/evs/latest/userguide/what-is-evs.html"&gt;user guide&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Security Token Service (STS) now enforces a single 4,096-byte size limit on session tokens. Previously, STS enforced separate limits on session token size and passed-in parameters (i.e., inline policies, managed policies, and session tags). STS has removed that separation, providing more flexibility for larger combinations of session policies and session tags.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, STS now returns response elements indicating session token size and percentage utilization relative to the token size limit. STS logs these values in AWS CloudTrail and publishes corresponding metrics in Amazon CloudWatch. A new optional API parameter also lets you generate larger session tokens (up to the 4,096-byte limit) to test whether your applications and infrastructure can handle them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the AWS European Sovereign Cloud Region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, please read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/aws-sts-simplifies-session-token-size-limits-and-adds-session-token-size-monitoring/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Security Blogpost&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_temp_request.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS IAM User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/Welcome.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS STS API Reference&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall announces the public preview of automated Internet firewall, a new centralized control that allows you to configure Network Firewall, with native network address translation (NAT) capability, on the internet gateways of all the Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) in your environment. Using automated Internet firewall mode, you can centrally define your Network Firewall security rules and enforce them on all inbound and outbound traffic across the internet gateways of your VPCs. You are not required to make routing or architectural changes to deploy automated Internet firewall. You can deploy it across select VPCs or your entire account, organization or organization unit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall is a stateful, managed, network firewall and intrusion detection and prevention service for your VPC. Customers use it to inspect their traffic and secure AWS resources. Prior to this launch, customers would configure AWS Network Firewalls inside their VPCs in the traffic path of all internet communication to achieve thorough security coverage against the threats on the internet. They deployed Network Firewall endpoints in dedicated subnets and made route updates for every subnet that needed internet access across all their VPCs. In addition, they monitored the environment to ensure that no new internet network paths bypassed these security controls. Automated Internet firewall makes this deployment and management simpler. You just define and maintain filtering rules, and AWS enforces it directly at the internet gateway of every VPC in scope, ensuring that all intended traffic entering or leaving your VPCs via the internet gateway is inspected. Automated Internet firewall comes with built-in NAT functionality for your egress-only internet connectivity needs. You can use it to filter traffic, perform NAT and log all internet activity across all internet gateways. You don't need to make architectural changes inside every VPC or monitor every internet network path. Automated Internet firewall allows you the option to automatically extend your organization's security policy to all future VPCs and subnets at creation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To help you get started quickly, automated Internet firewall comes with a default security policy, which uses Amazon managed rule groups including active threat defense that uses AWS unique threat intelligence to protect your workloads against evolving threats on the internet. You can also create your own custom rule groups or use any of the managed rule groups from AWS Marketplace with automated Internet firewall.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall operating in automated Internet firewall mode is available in preview in all AWS Regions except AWS GovCloud and China. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/"&gt;Network Firewall documentation&lt;/a&gt; and see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/pricing"&gt;AWS Network Firewall pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the Amazon VPC or Network Security Manager console.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) IP Address Manager (IPAM) policies now support AWS Network Firewall automated internet firewall and AWS Lambda Web Functions and MicroVMs. You can centrally configure IPAM policies across AWS accounts and AWS Regions to allocate public IPv4 addresses to these resources from specific IPAM pools, such as Bring Your Own IP (BYOIP) addresses or Amazon-provided contiguous IPv4 blocks. This enables a stronger security posture, simplifies network and security operations, and can reduce public IPv4 costs when you use BYOIP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Many customers allowlist the public IPs of their AWS resources in network access control lists (ACLs) and firewalls. Previously, keeping these resources on allowlisted addresses depended on each application team. With this launch, you can define allocation rules for them alongside existing resource types, so security teams can add IP-based filters with confidence that these resources draw addresses from approved ranges. For Lambda Web Functions and MicroVMs, this covers the public IPv4 addresses used for outbound traffic through Lambda Network Connectors. If a pool runs out of addresses, resources fall back to Amazon-provided addresses. You can also share allocated ranges with partners through IPAM integration with AWS prefix lists.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The feature is available in all AWS commercial Regions, in both the Free Tier and Advanced Tier of VPC IPAM. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/ipam/define-public-ipv4-allocation-strategy-with-ipam-policies.html"&gt;IPAM policies documentation page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/ipam/what-it-is-ipam.html"&gt;IPAM documentation&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, see the IPAM tab on the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/vpc/pricing/"&gt;Amazon VPC Pricing page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Aurora PostgreSQL databases created with express configuration can now move into an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with a single API call or a modify action in the Amazon RDS console. This helps you take AI-built prototypes and other applications to production without migrating data, while meeting your security and compliance requirements. You can also create a new database cluster inside a VPC using express configuration.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With express configuration, Aurora PostgreSQL lets you create and query a serverless database in seconds. Connectivity runs through an internet access gateway, which makes this a good fit for rapid prototyping and AI agent-driven development. When your application is ready for production, a single ModifyDBCluster API call moves the cluster into a VPC and turns off the internet access gateway. That gives you security groups, private subnets, and features such as Aurora Global Database, Amazon RDS Blue/Green Deployments, and Amazon RDS Proxy. The move requires no data migration, keeps your cluster endpoint the same, and interrupts connectivity only briefly. You can also reverse it with the same API call.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.html#CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.SupportedRegions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora PostgreSQL with express configuration is available. To get started, use the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/home?refid=35100cff-4aaa-46ac-bc1d-71576bef47be" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS console&lt;/a&gt;, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.html#CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.MoveIntoVPC" target="_blank"&gt;Aurora User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-187-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/17/2026 12:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Incorrect authorization in the credential loading logic in AWS Private CA Connector for Kubernetes(aws-privateca-issuer) before 1.12.0 might allow an authenticated remote user with permission to create namespace-scoped issuers to obtain certificates, including subordinate CA certificates, signed by another namespace's AWS Private CA with that namespace's stored AWS credentials, via a namespace-scoped issuer that references a credentials Secret in that namespace.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - aws-privateca-issuer before v1.12.0&lt;br&gt; - AWS Private CA Connector for Kubernetes EKS add-on before v1.12.0-eksbuild.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-187-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Security Hub adds installed software and resource usage as signals for resource criticality, which indicates how important a resource is to your business. Security Hub uses criticality to rank exposure findings, so that central security teams can prioritize the exposures that affect their most important resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security Hub already infers criticality from resource tags and names, but inconsistently tagged resources often have an Unknown criticality, which makes their exposures harder to prioritize. With installed software and resource usage as additional signals, more of your resources receive a meaningful criticality with no tagging or configuration required. For example, an EC2 instance that runs a database engine and serves steady traffic receives a higher criticality than an instance that runs only monitoring agents and sits idle.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These signals are included with the Security Hub Essentials plan at no additional cost and require no new enablement. To learn more, see Resources in Security Hub in the AWS Security Hub &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/resources-adv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the AWS Security Hub &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;. For a list of AWS Regions where Security Hub is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall now automatically generates domain allow-lists from your VPCs' observed DNS query traffic, helping you enforce least-privilege DNS access and reduce the risk of DNS-based data exfiltration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, implementing a least-privilege model required security teams to manually analyze DNS query logs to identify the domains their VPCs depend on before deploying allow-list rules. This created operational overhead and delayed enforcement of a deny-by-default (walled-garden) posture.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With automated allow-list generation, DNS Firewall analyzes DNS query traffic using a lookback window configurable from 1 hour to 7 days to create an allow-list of observed domains. It then continuously monitors DNS queries and flags newly observed domains, so you can review them before adding them to the allow-list. You can deploy the rule across multiple VPCs and accounts using AWS Firewall Manager or Route 53 Profiles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost to DNS Firewall Advanced customers, in all AWS Regions where Route 53 DNS Firewall is supported, except in AWS GovCloud (US) and Amazon Web Services China Regions. To get started, use the Route 53 DNS Firewall console, AWS CLI, or API.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/resolver-dns-firewall/"&gt;Amazon Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall product page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-advanced.html" target="_blank"&gt;Route 53 DNS Firewall Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; now supports OAuth 2.0 token exchange (RFC 8693). Amazon Cognito helps you add sign-up, sign-in, and access control to your web applications, mobile applications, and AI agents. With token exchange, a service or AI agent acting on a user's behalf can exchange the user's token for a new token scoped to a specific downstream service. Previously, a service that called another service on a user's behalf had to forward the user's original token or build and maintain its own token minting logic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With token exchange, you can issue a new token that has a narrower scope than the original token, is bound to a single audience, and comes without a refresh token. When you include an actor token, Cognito adds an act claim that identifies the intermediary, so downstream services can tell a request that an agent made on a user's behalf from one the user made directly. For example, a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server can exchange a user's token for a narrowly scoped token to call a backend API, with the act&amp;nbsp;claim recording the MCP server as the actor.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can exchange tokens through the token endpoint or with the new &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_GetDerivedToken.html"&gt;GetDerivedToken API&lt;/a&gt;. Token exchange is available on the Essentials and Plus feature plans in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-token-exchange.html"&gt;exchanging tokens for downstream services&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Cognito Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-176-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/10/2026 08:30 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-125271 of AWS API MCP Server. The server normalized operation names incorrectly before comparing them to the operator ' s security policy, so operations whose names contain consecutive capital letters never matched a correctly written denyList or elicitList entry. This might allow an actor to execute AWS API operations that the operator's security policy was configured to deny or require elicitation for.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 0.2.13 AND &amp;lt; 1.5.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-176-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Glue Data Catalog now supports external catalog's vended credentials in Apache Iceberg REST catalog federation. You can now read external Iceberg tables from Amazon Athena, Amazon EMR, Amazon Redshift, and AWS Glue using coarse-grained table grants in your external Iceberg catalog.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Glue Data Catalog already federates to external Iceberg REST catalogs through an AWS IAM role that you configure with access to your Amazon S3 buckets. This enhancement in Glue Data Catalog provides another way to connect to an external Iceberg catalog and read only the tables that the external catalog authorizes. It uses temporary security credentials that are scoped to the Amazon S3 storage prefix and vended by your external Iceberg catalog. AWS Lake Formation manages access control when you query an external table using any AWS analytics engine, letting you restrict access further down to specific rows and columns and share external Iceberg tables across AWS accounts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available through the AWS Lake Formation Management Console, AWS CLI, and the AWS Glue and Lake Formation SDKs. This feature is available in all commercial AWS Regions where Glue and Lake Formation are supported. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/catalog-federation.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Inspector now extends its vulnerability scanning to EC2 Amazon Machine Images (AMIs), enabling you to identify software vulnerabilities in your AMIs without launching instances or installing additional software. This helps you fix issues at the source before AMIs are used to launch instances across your fleet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this capability, you can run continuous evaluations to monitor your AMI catalog for newly discovered vulnerabilities, or perform targeted one-time scans for specific AMIs directly from the EC2 Console. You can enable AMI scanning for an account or across your entire organization in Amazon Inspector. For AWS Security Hub customers, you can enable AMI scanning as an add-on across your organization, and findings are also delivered to Security Hub. Based on the scan results, you can enable the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-allowed-amis.html"&gt;Allowed AMIs&lt;/a&gt; setting and specify security criteria that AMIs must meet to be available within your account and enforce this setting across the entire organization using Declarative Policies. Customers building AMIs with &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/imagebuilder/latest/userguide/integ-inspector.html#integ-inspector-ami-scan"&gt;EC2 Image Builder&lt;/a&gt; can integrate scanning directly into their pipelines and restrict distribution of AMIs that do not meet their security standards.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AMI Scanning helps you detect and remediate vulnerabilities earlier in your deployment pipeline, reducing the risk of deploying compromised compute resources. Security teams can now establish a consistent vulnerability scanning process across all AMIs improving their security posture before instances are launched.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all commercial AWS Regions where Amazon Inspector is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ami-scanning.html"&gt;EC2 User Guide&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/what-is-inspector.html"&gt;Amazon Inspector User Guide&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Inspector pricing page&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/"&gt;Amazon Inspector product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub CSPM&lt;/a&gt; now supports automated security checks that align with the Cyber Risk Institute (CRI) profile. The CRI Profile is a cybersecurity framework developed by the Cyber Risk Institute, a non-profit coalition of financial institutions and trade associations. The framework harmonizes cybersecurity and regulatory expectations that apply to the financial services sector into a common set of requirements, so that an organization can assess itself once and report against multiple regulations. AWS Security Hub CSPM provides 404 controls that support a subset of CRI Profile v2.2 requirements; the controls don't support requirements that require manual checks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new standard is now available in all AWS Regions where Security Hub CSPM is currently available, including the AWS GovCloud (US) and the China Regions. To quickly enable the standard across your AWS environment, we recommend that you use Security Hub central configuration. With this approach, you can enable the standard in all or only some of your organization's accounts and across all AWS Regions that are linked to Security Hub with a single action.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/standards-reference-cri-profile.html"&gt;CRI Profile v2.2&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Security Hub CSPM user guide. To receive notifications about new Security Hub CSPM features and controls subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-announcements.html"&gt;Security Hub SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;. You can also &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/cspm/pricing/"&gt;try Security Hub CSPM at no cost for 30 days&lt;/a&gt; with the AWS Free Tier offering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS DataSync now supports shared Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs). You can create DataSync agents and run transfer tasks using subnets shared across AWS accounts with AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). It allows you to transfer data privately over a shared subnet and VPC endpoint managed in a central account, rather than one per account.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customers that centralize their networking previously had to create a separate DataSync VPC endpoint in every account that connected privately through AWS PrivateLink. Each endpoint consumed IP addresses and added operational overhead to maintain across accounts. With shared VPC support, a single endpoint in the account that owns the VPC serves every account the subnet is shared with, conserving IP address space and removing the need for a per-account endpoint. This launch also helps within a single account setup. You can now use one VPC endpoint across multiple subnets, removing the earlier requirement for a matching VPC endpoint in each subnet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Shared VPC is supported for both Enhanced mode and Basic mode agent-based tasks. To get started, create a DataSync agent using the console or the CreateAgent API and specify a subnet shared with your account via AWS RAM. AWS DataSync support for Shared VPCs is available in all AWS Regions, except AWS Secret Regions, where AWS DataSync is offered. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/choose-service-endpoint.html#datasync-in-vpc" target="_blank"&gt;AWS DataSync feature documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Storage Gateway now supports FIPS 140-3 validated endpoints over AWS PrivateLink for Amazon S3 File Gateway. Previously, FIPS endpoints for File Gateway were available only over the public internet. Now you can keep FIPS-compliant traffic on the private AWS network, making it easier to use Storage Gateway for regulated workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, your File Gateway can reach the Storage Gateway service endpoints privately through a FIPS interface VPC endpoint in your VPC. You can also create NFS and SMB file shares that reach Amazon S3 through an S3 FIPS interface endpoint, enabling FIPS-compliant private connectivity for your end-to-end file transfer workloads. To get started, create FIPS interface endpoints for Storage Gateway and Amazon S3 in your VPC, then choose the FIPS VPC endpoint option when activating your gateway and configuring your file shares. To activate a gateway with a FIPS PrivateLink endpoint, your gateway must be running software version 2.1.10 or later.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This launch is available in the eight AWS Regions where Storage Gateway offers FIPS endpoints: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Canada West (Calgary), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/storagegateway/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Storage Gateway User Guide&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/storagegateway/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty now offers Custom Detection Rules, a library of 35 prebuilt, opt-in rules for CloudTrail management events that let you extend threat detection coverage to match your environment. Custom Detection Rules produces 26 unique finding types mapped to 10 MITRE ATT&amp;amp;CK® tactics, without the heavy lifting of log ingestion, normalization, or storage.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Some threat techniques, such as sharing an AMI externally, disabling flow logs, or signing in without MFA,&amp;nbsp;could be meaningful indicators of compromise in some accounts but routine in others. Custom Detection Rules lets you enable these detections only where the activity is unexpected — expanding your TTP coverage tailored to your environment.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, browse Custom Detection Rules via the GuardDuty console or API, and enable rules in dry-run mode to evaluate detection efficacy before going live.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Custom Detection Rules is available in all AWS commercial Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/custom-detection-rules.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty Custom Detection Rules&lt;/a&gt;. To receive programmatic updates on new Amazon GuardDuty features and threat detections, subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_sns.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory now supports fine-grained access control (FGAC), enabling you to enforce per-user and per-tenant memory isolation through AgentCore Gateway without building custom authorization logic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt; With FGAC, you can front your Memory resource with an AgentCore Gateway configured for OAuth (JWT) authentication and attach Cedar policies that restrict access based on the authenticated caller's identity. You can enforce that each user only accesses their own actor's data, restrict memory records to namespaces derived from the user's token claims, and allow or deny specific Memory operations per caller. This lets you move access control enforcement from application code to the infrastructure layer using cryptographic proof of identity. FGAC for Memory is built on the AgentCore Memory connector, a managed gateway connector that wires a gateway target to the Memory data plane and exposes 12 Memory operations as Cedar actions with their request attributes available for policy conditions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/memory-gateway-fgac.html"&gt;Fine-grained access control for Memory&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Secrets Manager now extends its managed external secrets capability to include Cisco Security Platform API keys and Netskope API tokens, enabling you to automatically rotate these third-party credentials directly from the AWS console without writing any custom rotation code.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For Cisco Security Platform (Security Cloud Control), Secrets Manager rotates the API key's refresh token on your schedule, keeping the credential active and capturing the new refresh token Cisco periodically reissues. Following Cisco's standard OAuth pattern, your applications exchange the stored refresh token for short-lived access tokens on demand. For Netskope, Secrets Manager rotates RBACv3 service-account REST API tokens through Netskope's SCIM API and validates the newly generated token before completing rotation. Both integrations are self-authenticating — the stored credential authorizes its own rotation — so no separate administrator credential is required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These integrations join existing managed external secrets support for BigID, Confluent Cloud, Datadog, GitLab, Jenkins, MongoDB Atlas, Okta, Paddle, Salesforce, Snowflake, and SonarQube.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cisco Security Platform and Netskope managed external secrets are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets is supported. To learn more, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/managed-external-secrets.html"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets documentation&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated AI endpoint discovery and protection are now generally available, enabling you to discover language model (LLM), Model Context Protocol (MCP), and Agent2Agent (A2A) endpoints behind Amazon API Gateway, Application Load Balancers, and Amazon CloudFront across your AWS Organization and protect them using AWS WAF.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Discovery is available through the new API Gateway API Portal, which recognizes the AI endpoints among the APIs it discovers automatically from traffic logs and service configuration records, with no agents to deploy or logs to forward. An undocumented, public-facing MCP server appears as a shadow API. For unprotected AI endpoints, customers can enable protection using WAF.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Protection is available through the new AWS WAF AI Security managed rule group, which protects your AI endpoints. AWS WAF implements protocol recognition and compliance checks to block malformed MCP and A2A messages. When you enable content inspection, the rule group calls Amazon Bedrock Guardrails InvokeGuardrailsCheck API to detect prompt injection, jailbreak attempts, harmful content in requests, and sensitive information in responses. You can use AI Security alongside other AWS Managed Rules or your own custom rules to protect your applications. Additional fees apply, and Amazon Bedrock charges separately for content inspection calls.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Together, API Portal and AI Security provide you a complete solution from discovery of all AI endpoints in your AWS environment to protecting them, without having to write any custom rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;API Portal is available in 32 AWS Regions. See the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/faqs/"&gt;Amazon API Gateway FAQ&lt;/a&gt; for the list. AI Security is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (London), Europe (Stockholm), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Asia Pacific (Sydney).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the following resources:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/waf/features/ai-security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF product page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-ai-security.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF AI Security in the AWS WAF Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apiportal.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API Portal Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-api-and-ai-security-on-aws-from-discovery-to-protection/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Introducing API and AI security on AWS, from discovery to protection blog post&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; now supports OAuth client ID metadata documents (CIMD). Amazon Cognito helps you implement secure sign-in and access control for users, AI agents, and microservices in minutes. With CIMD, Model Context Protocol (MCP) clients and AI agents can sign users in to your user pool without registering a separate app client. Previously, each external client needed its own app client, either created in advance or through dynamic client registration, and you had to manage and clean up those clients over time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With CIMD, a client uses an HTTPS URL as its client ID and publishes a JSON metadata document at that URL. When the client starts sign-in, Cognito fetches and validates the document and uses it to identify the client and validate its sign-in request. You decide which clients to accept with CIMD policies that map URL patterns to one of your app clients, and Cognito grants only the scopes and grant types that both the app client and the metadata document allow. For example, a company that runs an MCP server can accept an AI assistant by allowing the URL where that assistant publishes its metadata without creating or maintaining an app client for it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CIMD is available on the Essentials and Plus feature plans in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Cognito is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-client-id-metadata.html" target="_blank"&gt;Client ID metadata documents (CIMD)&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Cognito Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 adds post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange support to S3 Vectors and AWS PrivateLink for Amazon S3 interface endpoints. This extends the post-quantum cryptography support already available on regional S3, S3 Tables, and S3 Express One Zone endpoints.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;S3 Vectors endpoints and PrivateLink interface endpoints now support Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM), one of the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) standardized post-quantum cryptographic algorithms. PQ-TLS key exchange is available to all clients configured to use the ML-KEM key exchange algorithm. S3 Vectors and PrivateLink endpoints automatically negotiate the strongest key exchange that your client software supports, so clients that support ML-KEM receive the benefits of PQ-TLS key exchange without any changes to your applications or endpoint configuration.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;PQ-TLS key exchange for S3 Vectors and AWS PrivateLink for S3 is supported at no additional cost in all AWS Regions. To learn more about PQ-TLS support in S3, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingEncryptionInTransit.PQ-TLS.html"&gt;Amazon S3 User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Web Services announces the preview of Infoblox Threat Defense on &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/resolver/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall&lt;/a&gt;. Security administrators can now apply Infoblox threat intelligence directly in DNS Firewall rules to preemptively protect applications in their Amazon Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) from cyber threats, by subscribing to the Infoblox product listing on AWS Marketplace from the Route 53 DNS Firewall console.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can add Infoblox managed rules to your DNS Firewall rule groups to filter and block queries to malicious and high-risk domains at the DNS layer. Infoblox continuously updates its threat intelligence feeds to preemptively protect cloud workloads from phishing, malware, ransomware and other advanced attacks. You can apply these protections to DNS queries from your VPCs and to hybrid traffic forwarded through Route 53 Resolver endpoints, so security teams keep consistent DNS policies across AWS and on-premises environments and add a layer of protection on top of AWS managed domain lists.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Infoblox Threat Defense on Route 53 DNS Firewall is available in preview in all AWS commercial Regions where Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall is available. DNS Firewall Advanced customers can add Infoblox rules to existing rule groups at no additional DNS Firewall charge, and the Infoblox subscription in AWS Marketplace is free during preview.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-infoblox.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Route 53 DNS Firewall documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To view Route 53 pricing, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/pricing/"&gt;Route 53 pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about the AWS Marketplace listing and pricing for Infoblox Threat Defense for Route 53 DNS Firewall, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-sempmhxl3aqgg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;see here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS WAF announces a simplified console experience for Bot Control at the targeted inspection level. A single setting, Targeted bot control, turns on targeted protection for your Amazon CloudFront distributions with the configuration AWS recommends. It comes with two additions to the Bot Control managed rule group: bot confidence levels, which summarize the targeted detections on each request as High, Medium, or Low, and a LATEST version that keeps you on the newest detections automatically. Together, they help you protect your applications against sophisticated and AI-powered bots without writing label match rules or changing application code.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The targeted inspection level uses machine learning and client-side signals to detect evasive bots. Previously, acting on those signals meant integrating the AWS WAF JavaScript SDK into every page, learning dozens of labels, and writing label match rules to combine them. Now, the rule group combines the detections into one confidence level per request, with one rule per level, and the Targeted bot control setting adds the rule group, applies the recommended action for each level, sets the version to LATEST, and turns on automatic SDK integration so CloudFront adds the SDK to your pages. You can change any action or start in Count.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Targeted bot control setting is available today for protection packs (web ACLs) used with Amazon CloudFront. Bot confidence levels and the LATEST version are available in all AWS Regions where AWS WAF Bot Control is available. Standard pricing for the targeted inspection level applies, with no additional charge for the setting or automatic SDK integration. To get started, open the AWS WAF console. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-bot-control-targeted-console.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Turning on Targeted bot control&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/aws-managed-rule-groups-bot-confidence.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bot confidence levels&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS WAF Developer Guide, and read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-simplified-onboarding-for-aws-waf-bot-control-at-the-targeted-inspection-level/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Security Blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transit Gateway now supports network function attachments of type Gateway Load Balancer, simplifying the deployment and management of network security across your AWS network.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With a network function attachment, you can connect your transit gateway to a Gateway Load Balancer VPC endpoint service that fronts firewalls and other security appliances without complex VPC configurations. Transit Gateway creates and manages the Gateway Load Balancer endpoints in the Availability Zones you choose, removing the need to create dedicated endpoint subnets and route tables. The endpoint service can be in another AWS account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Network function attachments also support context sharing with security services. Transit Gateway adds resource context to traffic sent to your firewall, including the transit gateway attachment, VPC, subnet, and security groups. Your firewall can use this context alongside IP addresses to enforce segmentation policies.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all commercial AWS Regions where both AWS Transit Gateway and Gateway Load Balancer are available, except Middle East (Bahrain) and Middle East (UAE). It is also available in the AWS GovCloud (US-East) and AWS GovCloud (US-West) Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/tgw/tgw-nf-fw.html"&gt;Network function attachments&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Transit Gateway Guide and visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transit-gateway/"&gt;AWS Transit Gateway product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon MWAA Serverless provides the native Apache Airflow UI for your workflows. You can browse workflows, inspect workflow runs and task instances, read task logs, view a workflow's source and versions, and start a workflow run. The UI uses the permissions of the IAM identity that opens it and shows only what that identity can read, so read-only access is possible. AWS CloudTrail records the calls the UI makes under your identity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can open the Airflow UI from the Amazon MWAA console, the AWS CLI, or the API. Teams already familiar with Airflow can use the interface they know to work with their workflows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon MWAA Serverless is available. For more information, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/what-is-mwaa-serverless.html#regions" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/workflows-airflow-ui.html" target="_blank"&gt;Access the Airflow UI&lt;/a&gt;. Apache Airflow is a trademark of the &lt;a href="https://www.apache.org/" target="_blank"&gt;Apache Software Foundation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Route 53 now supports record set protection, which helps DNS administrators prevent accidental changes to critical DNS records in their hosted zones. This feature allows you to protect records such as those at the zone apex, MX records, and production endpoints directly in the hosted zone. Any change to a protected record is blocked until the protection is removed by an authorized user.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, to lock specific records, you had to write record-level conditions into the IAM policies of every user and role that could change your hosted zones. When those policies blocked a change, the caller had no indication of whether the record was protected or why. Now you can protect records by name and type directly in the hosted zone. A single protection covers all records with that name and type, including records with different set identifiers; weighted, latency, and other routing policy records are protected together. You can also create a protection proactively before the record exists, preventing records from being created for that name and type. Each protection can include an optional comment of up to 256 characters, allowing you to indicate the reason for the protection. You can manage protections using the Route 53 console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs through the CreateResourceRecordSetProtections, DeleteResourceRecordSetProtections, and ListResourceRecordSetProtections API actions. To control who can remove protections, restrict the DeleteResourceRecordSetProtections permission in IAM policies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Record set protection is available in all AWS commercial Regions where public and private hosted zones are available today. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/rrset-protection.html" target="_blank"&gt;Protecting records from changes&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Route 53 Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security teams need powerful tools to find vulnerabilities, test exploits, and build defenses. Daybreak Red from OpenAI is now available to eligible customers on Amazon Bedrock in AWS GovCloud (US-East), bringing advanced AI to this work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Part of OpenAI’s Daybreak cyber defense initiative, Daybreak Red: GPT-5.6 Cyber is built for authorized vulnerability research, exploit reproduction, and mitigation development. It gives verified defenders more room to investigate complex security problems, with fewer refusals for this work and stronger identity verification, monitoring, and access controls.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Daybreak Red runs on Bedrock’s next-generation inference engine, with zero-operator access enforced at the chip. Your inference data is not used to train models, and you do not need to share your Daybreak Red data with OpenAI.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The model is available in US East (Ohio) and AWS GovCloud (US-East). Access to the model requires &lt;a href="https://openai.com/form/enterprise-trusted-access-for-cyber/"&gt;enrollment in Daybreak access&lt;/a&gt; from OpenAI.&amp;nbsp;To get started, contact OpenAI or your AWS account team about Daybreak eligibility. Once approved, work with your AWS account team to enable access.&amp;nbsp;Learn more in the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/accelerate-cyber-defense-with-openai-and-aws-daybreak-red-daybreak-blue-now-available-to-eligible-customers-on-amazon-bedrock/"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-124-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 10/02/2026 12:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Loom is an AWS Labs open-source AI agent orchestration platform. We have identified and addressed three issues in Loom for AWS, described below. We recommend upgrading to the latest version (1.7.0) and ensuring any forked or derivative code is patched to incorporate the new fixes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;- CVE-2026-103956 ‐ Authentication bypass in Loom for AWS (CWE-306, CWE-1188)&lt;/b&gt; An issue in the authentication dependency in Loom for AWS versions &amp;lt;1.6.1 allowed any network client to obtain full administrative authority over the agent control plane ‐ including registering tool servers, reading stored integration credentials, and rewriting IAM role policies attached to managed agent roles ‐ via any request to the application API in a deployment where no identity provider was configured. This issue was addressed in version 1.6.1, released 2026-08-04.&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;- CVE-2026-103957 ‐ OAuth2 token and credential disclosure via outbound request handling in Loom for AWS (CWE-918, CWE-201)&lt;/b&gt; An issue in the OAuth2 discovery handling in Loom for AWS versions &amp;lt;1.7.0 allowed an authenticated user with the mcp:write or a2a:write scope to configure a well-known discovery URL whose document directed the backend to send OAuth2 client secrets or another user's access token to a third-party-controlled endpoint. The 1.6.1 release blocked internal-address reach for this code path but did not fully address the token disclosure. This issue was fully addressed in version 1.7.0.&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;- CVE-2026-103958 ‐ Outbound request handling issue in tool server and remote agent connections in Loom for AWS (CWE-918)&lt;/b&gt; An issue in the tool server (MCP) and remote agent (A2A) connection handling in Loom for AWS versions &amp;lt;1.7.0 allowed an authenticated user with the mcp:write or a2a:write scope to direct connection requests to arbitrary internal network locations — including the container's credential-vending endpoint ‐ and read the responses. This issue was addressed in version 1.7.0.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API activity across your AWS account for security auditing, compliance, and operational troubleshooting, now integrates with Amazon Q Console to help you investigate your AWS account activity using natural language. You can ask Amazon Q Console questions about your CloudTrail configuration, query your logged events for security investigations, and troubleshoot operational issues without writing queries or manually parsing log files.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this integration, you can ask Amazon Q Console to check whether your CloudTrail trails are properly configured, identify gaps in your logging coverage, and confirm which data event sources you are tracking. You can investigate security concerns by asking who accessed a specific IAM role, what changes were made to your VPC configuration, or whether there were unauthorized access attempts in the past week. For operational troubleshooting, you can ask Amazon Q Console to find who created or deleted specific resources, identify which API calls are generating errors, trace activity from a specific IP address, or determine why your bill spiked. Amazon Q Console can query your CloudTrail trails, associated CloudWatch log groups, and event data stores on your behalf, providing answers grounded in your actual account activity rather than generic documentation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This integration is available in all AWS commercial regions where Amazon Q Console is supported. To get started, open Amazon Q in AWS Management Console and ask questions about your CloudTrail configuration or account activity. For more information, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/"&gt;AWS CloudTrail documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) Volume Clones now supports copying EBS volumes across AWS accounts with re-encryption. You can copy EBS volumes to any AWS account, re-encrypting with an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key in the target account. This enables organizations that separate production and development workloads into different accounts to copy data across those account boundaries.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With cross-account copy, you can clone a production database volume into an isolated development account, giving developers a fresh copy of production data to experiment with safely. This capability also supports teams that require encryption key separation across environments, such as maintaining separate KMS keys for production and non-production accounts. Cross-account copy is supported for all volume types, including unencrypted volumes and volumes encrypted with customer managed keys.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To copy a volume across accounts, you first share the volume with the target account using AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM), and then the target account creates a copy of the shared volume in the same Availability Zone. You can access this capability using the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), and AWS SDKs. It is available in all AWS Regions that support Amazon EBS Volume Clones, including all Commercial Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, AWS China Regions, and supported Local Zones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the Amazon &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/ebs-copying-volume.html"&gt;EBS Volume Clones documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon API Gateway announces &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apiportal.html"&gt;API Portal&lt;/a&gt;, a new feature that discovers, governs, and documents the APIs and AI endpoints behind Amazon API Gateway, Application Load Balancer, and Amazon CloudFront across your AWS organization.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Many organizations can't see which APIs are running, which were never registered, which are no longer maintained, and which lack protection. API Portal builds a continuously updated inventory using AWS-side traffic logs and service configuration records, with nothing to install, no logs to forward, and no traffic to reroute,&amp;nbsp;for a single account or your entire AWS organization.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Every discovered API starts as a shadow API until you choose to manage it. API Portal labels each API as public or private and identifies AI endpoints, including MCP servers, A2A servers, and LLM endpoints. For managed APIs, it flags those that stop receiving traffic or lose their owner and evaluates security posture against best practices such as authentication, throttling, and AWS WAF protection. With &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/waf/features/api-security/"&gt;AWS WAF API Security&lt;/a&gt;, it also shows the security events detected per API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can publish any managed API in a documentation portal for internal teams or external partners, with your branding, Amazon Cognito access control, and interactive Try it pages. If you already use API Gateway Portals, your portals continue to work and are now managed from the Documentation Portals section of API Portal.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;API Portal is available in 32 AWS Regions; see the Amazon API Gateway &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/faqs/"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/pricing/"&gt;pricing&lt;/a&gt; pages for details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the following resources:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/"&gt;Amazon API Gateway product page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/introducing-api-and-ai-security-on-aws-from-discovery-to-protection/"&gt;Introducing API and AI security on AWS blog&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apiportal.html"&gt;API Portal documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-api-security.html"&gt;AWS WAF API Security documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-190-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/19/2026 10:00 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS is aware of security releases that the PHP Foundation published on November 19, 2026. PHP versions 8.2.36, 8.3.37, 8.4.28, and 8.5.13 address 17 issues across the PHP 8.2, 8.3, 8.4, and 8.5 branches. The issues are in the following components:&lt;br&gt; - The PHP engine&lt;br&gt; - PHP-FPM and the PHP CGI binary&lt;br&gt; - The SOAP, Phar, session, DOM, and date extensions&lt;br&gt; - The HTTP and FTP stream wrappers&lt;br&gt; - The PHP 8.5 RFC 3986 URI implementation&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Patching the affected software remains the only complete fix and should be the first priority.&lt;/b&gt; We will update this bulletin as more information becomes available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-190-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity now supports private certificate authorities (CAs) for custom OAuth 2.0 credential providers. If you already connect your agents to identity providers (IdPs) hosted in your VPC, you can now configure those connections to trust certificates issued by your own CA — including AWS Private CA, enterprise CAs, and self-signed root CAs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AgentCore Identity has supported private IdP connectivity through Amazon VPC Lattice, but until now the IdP still had to present a publicly trusted TLS certificate over that private connection. With private CA support, you store your CA certificate in AWS Secrets Manager or Amazon S3 and reference it from your credential provider configuration. AgentCore Identity uses it to verify the IdP's server certificate directly, removing the need for a publicly trusted certificate or additional infrastructure on the private network path.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can rotate the CA certificate without downtime by combining the current and new certificates in a single PEM file, and remove private CA trust by updating the credential provider without the certificate reference.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Private CA support for AgentCore Identity is available in all AWS Regions where AgentCore Identity supports private IdP connectivity. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/identity-private-idp.html" target="_blank"&gt;Connect to private identity providers&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Developer Guide. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Secrets Manager now supports managed external secrets for Auth0, Okta, and Palo Alto Networks. Long-lived credentials that are not rotated increase the risk of unauthorized access if exposed. Rotating a third-party credential requires coordinated updates to the external service and every application that uses it. You set a rotation schedule from the AWS Management Console or API. Secrets Manager rotates the credential and stores the current value without a custom rotation Lambda function.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Secrets Manager offers self-rotation and administrator-assisted rotation for Auth0 credentials. With self-rotation, the target application replaces its own secret. With administrator-assisted rotation, a separate Auth0 application with Management API permissions replaces the target application's secret. For Okta, Secrets Manager offers administrator-assisted rotation with a separate administrator JSON Web Token (JWT) key and validates the replacement before deactivating the old secret. For Palo Alto Networks, Secrets Manager offers self-rotation, in which the current API key authorizes its replacement.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Applications that already retrieve credentials from Secrets Manager receive the rotated value without a code change or deployment. &lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These integrations join existing managed external secrets support for BigID, Cisco Security Platform, Confluent Cloud, Databricks, Datadog, GitLab, Jenkins, MongoDB Atlas, Netskope, Paddle, Salesforce, Snowflake, SonarQube, and Twilio.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Auth0, Okta, and Palo Alto Networks managed external secrets are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets is supported. To learn more, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/managed-external-secrets.html"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets documentation&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-179-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/11/2026 12:30 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Missing authorization in the external management API in Amazon mcp-gateway-registry 1.0.9 through 1.24.7 might allow an authenticated remote user to assign the registry administrator scope to their own identity group and obtain administrative control of every registered gateway asset via a crafted group import request.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 1.0.9 AND &amp;lt;= 1.24.7&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-179-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 Tables now support server access logging, providing you detailed records of the requests made to your table buckets. You can now track table operations, namespace management, and data access to support security audits, access analysis, and cost attribution. Log delivery is available at no additional cost, and you pay only for the storage of the log files.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When you enable server access logging on a table bucket, S3 delivers logs for requests to all tables and namespaces in that bucket to a general purpose bucket in the same account and Region. Each record captures the requester, operation, table name, namespace, response status, and request timing, using the same space-delimited format as S3 server access logs for general purpose buckets. This lets you reuse your existing log analysis workflows to query S3 Table logs with any query engine of your choice such as Amazon Athena and DuckDB.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Server access logging for S3 Tables is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-tables-regions-quotas.html#s3-tables-regions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where S3 Tables are available. You can enable it using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, or AWS CloudFormation. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;S3 Tables overview page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-tables-server-logs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;S3 Tables server access logging&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon S3 User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 server access log delivery to Amazon CloudWatch Logs is now available in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, AWS European Sovereign Cloud, and AWS China Regions. With your access logs in CloudWatch Logs, you can query them as they arrive, set alarms, and encrypt them with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys. This new delivery path complements the existing delivery of server access logs to S3 general purpose buckets, giving you more flexibility in how you monitor and analyze access to your data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With delivery to CloudWatch Logs, you can set alarms on error rates, monitor traffic patterns, investigate access incidents, and correlate S3 access activity with the rest of your operational data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;S3 server access log delivery to CloudWatch Logs is now available in all AWS Regions. Amazon CloudWatch Logs charges for vended logs apply. For pricing details, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon S3 User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/"&gt;Amazon Redshift&lt;/a&gt; long-term retention for system table data through native integration with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/"&gt;Amazon S3 Tables&lt;/a&gt; is now available in additional AWS Regions. With this feature, you can configure your Redshift system table data retention beyond the current 7-day limit to meet your compliance, auditing, and observability requirements. Once enabled, AWS automatically writes system table data to S3 Tables in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/"&gt;Apache Iceberg format&lt;/a&gt; and manages partitioning, compaction, and retention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers use Redshift system tables to monitor query performance, audit data warehouse activity, and meet compliance requirements. With this feature, system table data is replicated automatically, eliminating the need for custom extract transform load (ETL) pipelines and any resource contention with your production workloads. If you operate multiple data warehouses, you can consolidate their system table data into a single location for cross-warehouse observability and analysis. Because the data is stored in the open Apache Iceberg format, you can query it through Redshift, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/athena/"&gt;Amazon Athena&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://duckdb.org/"&gt;DuckDB&lt;/a&gt;, or any Iceberg-compatible engine, and build observability dashboards using AWS services or third-party tools without additional operations overhead. Additionally, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/system-table-skills"&gt;AWS Agent Toolkit&lt;/a&gt; provides skills for querying system table data to surface performance insights and optimization recommendations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available for Amazon Redshift Provisioned &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/rg/"&gt;RG&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/ra3/"&gt;RA3&lt;/a&gt; instances and Amazon Redshift &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/"&gt;Serverless&lt;/a&gt; in the following additional AWS Regions: Asia Pacific (New Zealand), Canada West (Calgary), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). To learn more, visit our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/system-table-s3-tables.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/long-term-system-tables-retention-in-amazon-redshift-with-amazon-s3-tables/"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda now supports OAuth authentication for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kafka.html"&gt;self-managed Apache Kafka event source mappings&lt;/a&gt; (ESM), including&amp;nbsp;Kafka clusters that customers run themselves and managed offerings such as Confluent Cloud, Aiven, and Redpanda. With this launch, customers can authenticate their Lambda Kafka consumers through their enterprise identity provider, helping them meet the security and compliance requirements for their Kafka applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customers building event-driven Kafka workloads for use cases such as payment processing, fraud detection, and real-time data pipelines use Kafka ESM to build serverless Kafka consumers. Kafka ESM provides automatic scaling, error handling, batching, and event filtering. Previously, Kafka ESM supported only SASL/PLAIN, SASL/SCRAM, and mutual TLS (mTLS) as&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/kafka-cluster-auth.html"&gt;authentication methods&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;so customers in regulated industries that require OAuth could not use Kafka ESM with their clusters. With OAuth support, customers can use their enterprise identity provider, such as &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; or Okta,&amp;nbsp;to authenticate their Kafka ESM and apply the same identity governance and access policies across their Kafka clusters and Lambda consumers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS commercial Regions&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;where self-managed Kafka ESM is available. To use OAuth authentication, create a new Kafka ESM with your authentication configuration through the AWS Management Console, Lambda API, AWS CLI, AWS CloudFormation, or AWS SAM. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kafka.html"&gt;AWS Lambda developer guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall now supports wildcard matching in container attribute-based inspection filters for Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) and Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS). This enhancement allows you to define attribute filters using wildcard patterns to match multiple container workloads in a single rule, eliminating the need to create individual container associations for each variant.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With wildcard support, you can use patterns like app=payments-* to automatically cover all variants of an application, such as payments-api, payments-worker, and payments-cron. This simplifies firewall rule management for dynamic container environments where new application variants are frequently deployed, reducing operational overhead and ensuring consistent security coverage across your containerized workloads.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wildcard support for container attribute filters is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall container attribute-based inspection is supported. For a full list of supported Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region Table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and service &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/container-associations.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Batch now supports Amazon EKS access entry authentication for compute environments. EKS access entries provide an API-driven approach to granting IAM principals access to Kubernetes clusters, complementing the existing &lt;i&gt;aws-auth&lt;/i&gt; &lt;i&gt;ConfigMap&lt;/i&gt;. AWS Batch can now authenticate to your cluster through the access entry mechanism, simplifying cluster setup and authentication lifecycle management.
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 To get started, use the &lt;i&gt;CreateComputeEnvironment&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;UpdateComputeEnvironment&lt;/i&gt; APIs to set &lt;i&gt;accessEntry.desiredState&lt;/i&gt; to ENABLED on all AWS Batch compute environments targeting your cluster. AWS Batch creates one access entry per cluster and associates the &lt;i&gt;AWSBatchClusterPolicy&lt;/i&gt; with it. You can configure access entries through the AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, or AWS Management Console.
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 Access entry authentication for AWS Batch on Amazon EKS is now supported in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where AWS Batch is available. For more information, see Amazon EKS access entry authentication in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/batch/latest/userguide/eks-access-entries.html"&gt;AWS Batch User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-119-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/29/2026 08:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;GluonTS is an open source library for deep learning based time series models. We identified CVE-2026-100308 that allows arbitrary command execution upon deserialization of untrusted model artifacts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Deserialization of untrusted data in the model loading component in Amazon GluonTS before 0.17.0 might allow context-dependent attackers to execute arbitrary operating system commands with the privileges of the loading process via a crafted serialized model directory. The issue arises whenever a user calls Predictor.deserialize() or any RepresentablePredictor.deserialize() on a model directory they do not fully control.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;0.17.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-119-aws/"&gt;View article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio now supports two new connection capabilities: (1) Iceberg REST Catalog (IRC) connections for external Apache Iceberg catalogs, and (2) IAM authentication for Amazon DocumentDB. Together, they let data teams connect to a broader set of governed data sources and use credential-less authentication across projects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Iceberg REST Catalog connections.&lt;/b&gt; SageMaker Unified Studio now supports Iceberg REST Catalog (IRC) connections, letting customers connect to external Apache Iceberg catalogs that follow the Iceberg REST specification. Supported catalog types include Snowflake Open Catalog (Polaris), Databricks Unity Catalog, and a Generic IRC type for any other spec-compliant catalog. Once connected, customers can browse the catalog in Data Explorer (list catalog/schema/table, view columns, sample data), read and write it in Visual ETL as a source or append/overwrite sink, and query it from data notebooks. Authentication uses OAuth2 or bearer token, and data access uses vended, short-lived Amazon S3 credentials. The connection lifecycle is fully supported - create, edit, delete, and test connection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;IAM authentication for Amazon DocumentDB.&lt;/b&gt; SageMaker Unified Studio now supports IAM authentication for Amazon DocumentDB connections, so customers can connect to DocumentDB without storing a database username or password in the connection or notebook. The connection authenticates using its own IAM role, which DocumentDB validates via AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS). This requires Amazon DocumentDB 5.0 or later instance-based clusters with TLS enabled. The connection can be used from data notebooks, Data Explorer, Test Connection, and Visual ETL data preview.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These connection capabilities are available today in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available, at no additional cost.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/what-is-sagemaker-unified-studio.html"&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Continuum for penetration testing is a frontier agent that proactively secures applications throughout the development lifecycle by offering on-demand, customized penetration testing with real exploitability testing. Developers and security teams can now test login credentials and receive suggested domains before a penetration test runs. This makes it easier to configure an accurate network scope from the start, reducing misconfiguration and wasted test cycles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, identifying all the URLs your application reaches required manual effort, and authentication failures were only discovered after a full test cycle completed, costing time and resources. With this launch, when you add login credentials during test configuration, AWS Continuum authenticates into your application exactly as a real user would, capturing every accessible domain reached during login and surfacing them as in-scope URL suggestions. You can review accessible domains, validate credentials, and confirm the agent covers the right endpoints, all before the real test begins. Accessible domains are returned regardless of whether the credential test succeeds, fails, or times out. You can learn more about this feature in our updated &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityagent/latest/userguide/perform-penetration-test.html#_configure_authentication_credentials_optional"&gt;documentaiton&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all regions where AWS Continuum for penetration testing is available and is detailed on the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/continuum/?trk=77f1fbea-4ad5-4c1d-b16e-e5634ca5086e&amp;amp;sc_channel=ps&amp;amp;ef_id=CjwKCAjwwrPVBhA1EiwAv_YO-dtnJZvxRUPyj3N8BdcJLvN4fjq5kQwIththbsuo7ZvdNlCIcxxmSRoCX2kQAvD_BwE:G:s&amp;amp;gclid=CjwKCAjwwrPVBhA1EiwAv_YO-dtnJZvxRUPyj3N8BdcJLvN4fjq5kQwIththbsuo7ZvdNlCIcxxmSRoCX2kQAvD_BwE"&gt;AWS Continuum product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS PrivateLink customers can now use VPC endpoint to privately and securely access network segments in another VPC/account. They can use a ‘tunnel’ endpoint, a new type of VPC endpoint, to tunnel into the network segment and access resources located within it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS PrivateLink is a highly available and scalable technology that enables private access across VPC and account boundaries to load balanced services, appliances, and resources such as databases and domains. Prior to this launch, customers who wanted to share their resources with another party such as an external vendor had to do it one at a time by creating a Resource Configuration for every resource. Now, customers can create a Resource Configuration to represent a CIDR range in their network, and share it with a vendor via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). The vendor can then create a tunnel endpoint and use GENEVE encapsulation to tunnel through it into the customer’s VPC to access resources located in CIDR range specified by the customer. There is an hourly charge for the tunnel endpoint and a per-GB charge for data processed through it. Please refer to the pricing page for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/privatelink/pricing/"&gt;AWS PrivateLink&lt;/a&gt;. &amp;nbsp;
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 The capability is available in the following &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Melbourne), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Osaka), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), Canada West (Calgary), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Milan), Europe (Paris), Europe (Spain), Europe (Stockholm), Europe (Zurich), Mexico (Central), South America (São Paulo).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about this capability and get started, please refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/what-is-privatelink.html"&gt;AWS PrivateLink documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Builder ID, your personal profile for accessing AWS applications including AWS Builder Center, AWS Training and Certification, Amazon Quick and Kiro, now offers new ways to protect and recover your profile. You can add a recovery email and use new self-service options to regain access if you're locked out. You can also register multi-factor authentication (MFA) devices for any sign-in method, including third-party logins such as Google or Apple.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With these enhancements, you have more self-service options to recover your AWS Builder ID. Adding a recovery email provides a second verification factor that makes self-service recovery possible for more scenarios without the need to contact AWS Support. You can reset a forgotten password using a link sent to your primary or recovery email. If you lose access to your MFA device, you can regain access by verifying both your primary and recovery emails. If you use Google, Apple, GitHub or Amazon to sign in to Builder ID, you can now register MFA devices directly in AWS Builder ID, bringing the same strong account protection previously available to email and password users, and you can permanently switch your sign-in method to an email address and password if you lose access to the third-party account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about AWS Builder ID and how to set up account recovery and MFA, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/sign-in-builder-id.html"&gt;AWS Builder ID documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AWS Transform service is now in scope for FedRAMP Class C (formerly Moderate baseline) in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. You can use AWS Transform from this region to migrate and modernize resources that are or will be located in any of the US East/US West Regions in order to satisfy FedRAMP Class C compliance requirements. Additionally, AWS Transform MGN is in scope for FedRAMP Class D, and can be used independently to migrate resources that require FedRAMP Class D compliance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program (FedRAMP) is a US government-wide program that delivers a standard approach to the security assessment, authorization, and continuous monitoring for cloud products and services. &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform"&gt;AWS Transform&lt;/a&gt; is an agentic migration and modernization service designed to compress enterprise transformation timelines from years to months — handling everything from large-scale infrastructure migrations to continuous tech debt reduction, without the manual handoffs and lost context that commonly stall these programs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/fedramp/"&gt;FedRAMP&lt;/a&gt;, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/services-in-scope/FedRAMP/"&gt;AWS services in scope page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about AWS Transform, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform/"&gt;webpage&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/what-is-service.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-002-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Informational&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/01/15 07:03 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A security research team identified a configuration issue affecting the following AWS-managed open source GitHub repositories that could have resulted in the introduction of inappropriate code:&lt;br&gt; - aws-sdk-js-v3&lt;br&gt; - aws-lc&lt;br&gt; - amazon-corretto-crypto-provider&lt;br&gt; - awslabs/open-data-registry&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Specifically, researchers identified the above repositories' configured regular expressions for AWS CodeBuild webhook filters intended to limit trusted actor IDs were insufficient, allowing a predictably acquired actor ID to gain administrative permissions for the affected repositories. We can confirm these were project-specific misconfigurations in webhook actor ID filters for these repositories and not an issue in the CodeBuild service itself. The researchers carefully demonstrated the potential to commit inappropriate code, through an empty code commit, to one repository and promptly informed AWS Security of their research activity and its potential negative impact.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;No inappropriate code was introduced to any of the affected repositories during this security research activity, the demonstrated empty code commit to one repository had no impact to any AWS customer environments and did not impact any AWS services or infrastructure. No customer action is required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-007-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/03/16 09:15 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The AWS API MCP Server is an open source Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that enables AI assistants to interact with AWS services and resources through AWS CLI commands. It provides programmatic access to manage your AWS infrastructure while maintaining proper security controls.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This server acts as a bridge between AI assistants and AWS services, allowing you to create, update, and manage AWS resources across all available services. The server includes a configurable file access feature that controls how AWS CLI commands interact with the local file system. By default, file operations are restricted to a designated working directory (workdir), but this can be configured to allow unrestricted file system access (unrestricted) or to block all local file path arguments entirely (no-access).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-4270: Improper Protection of Alternate Path exists in the no-access and workdir feature of the AWS API MCP Server versions &amp;gt;= 0.2.14 and &amp;lt; 1.3.9 on all platforms may allow the bypass of intended file access restriction and expose arbitrary local file contents in the MCP client application context.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; awslabs.aws-api-mcp-server &amp;gt;= 0.2.14, &amp;lt; 1.3.9&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/"&gt;Amazon Redshift&lt;/a&gt; now supports long-term retention for system table data through native integration with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/?trk=a0cfbe47-9163-4866-b531-ae6e42b850fb&amp;amp;sc_channel=ps&amp;amp;ef_id=CjwKCAjws_DTBhB_EiwAXZknGWBvpgJrpG58UgmwS6tvQHUY9CSWOHPTWD3gwcvOPs-HvoPrPkEMlBoC1DcQAvD_BwE&amp;amp;gads_camp=23522747487&amp;amp;gads_ag=199083605197&amp;amp;gads_ad=820200021722&amp;amp;gads_kw=amazon%20s3%20tables&amp;amp;gads_matchtype=e&amp;amp;gads_network=g&amp;amp;gads_device=c&amp;amp;gads_geo=1027744&amp;amp;gad_campaignid=23522747487&amp;amp;gbraid=0AAAAADjHtp9cwZBKiv30JXu5qrPDkKSyb&amp;amp;gclid=CjwKCAjws_DTBhB_EiwAXZknGWBvpgJrpG58UgmwS6tvQHUY9CSWOHPTWD3gwcvOPs-HvoPrPkEMlBoC1DcQAvD_BwE"&gt;Amazon S3 Tables.&lt;/a&gt; With this feature, you can configure your Redshift system table data retention beyond the current 7-day limit to meet your compliance, auditing, and observability requirements. Once enabled, AWS automatically writes system table data to S3 Tables in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/"&gt;Apache Iceberg format&lt;/a&gt; and manages partitioning, compaction, and retention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers use Redshift system tables to monitor query performance, audit data warehouse activity, and meet compliance requirements. Previously, extending retention required building and maintaining custom extract transform load (ETL) pipelines to copy system table data, adding development effort and ongoing operational overhead. Customers operating multiple data warehouses faced additional complexity, relying on Redshift data sharing to consolidate system table data from each warehouse into a central location for cross-warehouse analysis. With this feature, system table data is replicated automatically, eliminating the need for custom ETL pipelines and any resource contention with your production workloads. &amp;nbsp;If you operate multiple data warehouses, you can consolidate their system table data into a single location for cross-warehouse observability and analysis. Because the data is stored in the open Apache Iceberg format, you can query it through Redshift,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/athena/"&gt;Amazon Athena&lt;/a&gt;, or any Iceberg-compatible engine, and build observability dashboards using AWS services or third-party tools without additional operations overhead. Additionally, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/system-table-skills"&gt;AWS Agent Toolkit&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;provides skills for querying system table data to surface performance insights and optimization recommendations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available for Amazon Redshift Provisioned &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/rg/"&gt;RG&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/ra3/"&gt;RA3&lt;/a&gt; instances and Amazon Redshift &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/"&gt;Serverless&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Taipei), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Osaka), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Asia Pacific (Melbourne), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Thailand), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Zurich), Europe (Stockholm), Europe (Milan), Europe (Spain), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Paris), Israel (Tel Aviv), and South America (Sao Paulo). To learn more, visit our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/system-table-s3-tables.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; or&amp;nbsp;read the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/long-term-system-tables-retention-in-amazon-redshift-with-amazon-s3-tables/"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Streams now supports attribute-based access control (ABAC), enabling you to use tag-based conditions in your Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies to control access to your data streams. ABAC is an authorization strategy that simplifies access management by allowing you to enforce different access levels for multiple teams and applications using fewer IAM policies. This capability is built for teams that manage DynamoDB Streams access across multiple applications and environments and need finer-grained, scalable access control.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With ABAC for DynamoDB Streams, you can attach up to 50 tags to each stream and use these tags in IAM policy conditions to grant or deny access to specific actions. For example, you can allow users to read records only from streams tagged with "environment:production" while restricting access to other environments. Stream tags are managed independently from their parent table tags, giving you flexibility to implement environment segregation, team-based isolation, and compliance requirements without creating numerous individual IAM policies.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ABAC for DynamoDB Streams is in all commercial AWS Regions and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions where Amazon DynamoDB Streams is available. There is no additional cost to use ABAC for DynamoDB Streams. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb"&gt;Amazon DynamoDB&lt;/a&gt; page and see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/abac-streams.html"&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Streams ABAC&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-197-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/23/2026 13:30 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon SSM Agent is the AWS Systems Manager agent that runs on EC2 instances so they can be managed through Systems Manager.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-134069, an issue in Amazon SSM Agent on AWS-provided Windows Server AMIs released before 2026-09-09, where instances launched from the same AMI shared one instance registration key. On instances managed through Default Host Management Configuration, a local user without administrative privileges might be able to obtain the instance's Default Host Management Configuration role credentials.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon SSM Agent &amp;lt; 3.3.5226.0 on AWS Windows Server AMIs released before 2026-09-09. Instances launched from these AMIs remain affected after an in-place agent upgrade.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-197-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront is now available in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud. Organizations with strict EU sovereignty requirements can now deliver websites, applications, and APIs with the performance, security, and scale of CloudFront, all within the EU. Until now, customers had to build and run their own delivery and TLS termination infrastructure to keep traffic and data inside the EU.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With CloudFront in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud, content delivery, TLS termination, logs, and customer configuration stay within the European Sovereign Cloud boundary. The AWS European Sovereign Cloud is physically and logically separate from other AWS Regions, and only EU-resident personnel operate it. This helps governments, healthcare, financial services and other regulated industries meet data residency and operational sovereignty requirements without trading away performance and scale. Customers use the same CloudFront APIs and console experience they already know making it easy to transfer over existing deployments and automations.&amp;nbsp;If you plan to run media streaming or other high-volume workloads, contact your AWS account team and we can help you find the right solution, including options in AWS commercial Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, sign in to your AWS European Sovereign Cloud account and create a distribution in the CloudFront console. To learn more about available features and pricing, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.eu/esc/latest/userguide/cloudfront.html"&gt;CloudFront Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;a href="https://www.aws.eu/cloudfront/pricing/"&gt;CloudFront pricing page in ESC&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/private-ca/"&gt;AWS Private Certificate Authority&lt;/a&gt; (AWS Private CA) now supports IAM condition keys that describe the certificate being issued. You can write IAM policies that allow or deny a certificate request based on its subject, subject alternative names (SANs), public key, signature algorithm, validity period, key usage, extended key usage, and basic constraints. You can share one private CA across teams and accounts, and each team can issue only the certificates your policies allow.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, IAM policies could control who requests certificates from which CA, and with which certificate template, but not what the certificate contains. To keep each team to its own domain names, customers ran a separate CA for each team or built their own authorization layer in front of AWS Private CA. Now, AWS Private CA evaluates your policies against the certificate as it would be issued, before anything is signed, and denies requests that don't comply. For example, you can limit each account to DNS names in its own subdomain, cap certificate lifetimes, require specified key algorithms and key sizes across your organization, and reserve CA certificates for your PKI team.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use the condition keys in identity-based policies, service control policies (SCPs), resource control policies (RCPs), VPC endpoint policies, and customer managed permissions in AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM). Each key takes its value from the final certificate, whether the attribute comes from the certificate signing request (CSR), the certificate template, or API passthrough, so moving an attribute to a different input doesn't get it past a policy.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;X.509 IAM condition keys are available at no additional charge in all AWS Regions where AWS Private CA is available. AWS Private CA charges still apply. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/privateca/latest/userguide/certguard-condition-keys.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;X.509 certificate condition keys&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Private CA User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-192-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/19/2026 15:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS is aware of the nginx issues published on November 18, 2026 (CVE-2026-123007, CVE-2026-123633, CVE-2026-123634, CVE-2026-113425, CVE-2026-121810 and CVE-2026-118899). These issues have been addressed in nginx version 1.30.7 (stable) and 1.31.9 (mainline). We recommend upgrading to the latest version. We are actively deploying mitigations worldwide to address these issues.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Because these issues can be leveraged at the HTTP/2 protocol layer before a complete HTTP request is formed, AWS WAF managed and custom rules cannot effectively detect or block them.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-192-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Control Tower now lets you use an existing Amazon S3 bucket in your landing zone to store AWS Config and AWS CloudTrail logs. Previously, AWS Control Tower required you to use the Amazon S3 bucket it creates and manages for log storage. Now, customers who want full control of bucket policies, lifecycle rules, and logging infrastructure can consolidate centralized logs in an Amazon S3 bucket they already manage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can use your own Amazon S3 bucket for both AWS Config and AWS CloudTrail, giving you full control of bucket policies, lifecycle rules, and logging infrastructure. You can switch between self-managed and AWS Control Tower managed buckets at any time, and your Amazon S3 buckets are preserved during landing zone updates, resets, and decommissioning. The bucket must be owned by an AWS account in your organization, be located in your AWS Control Tower home Region, and be preconfigured with the bucket policy that AWS Control Tower requires.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is supported in landing zone version 4.0 and later and is available in all commercial &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the AWS European Sovereign Cloud where AWS Control Tower is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about configuring self-managed Amazon S3 bucket for your AWS Control Tower landing zone, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/controltower/latest/userguide/custom-logging-bucket.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Control Tower documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker HyperPod now supports higher pod density per node on EKS-orchestrated clusters, allowing you to get more out of every instance. This leaves more room for your applications alongside system DaemonSets for monitoring, logging, and security, and helps increase GPU, CPU and memory utilization on nodes, allowing you to run the same work using fewer instances.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;As you schedule more pods, the Amazon VPC CNI automatically adds network capacity to the node. Autoscaling with Karpenter also supports the higher pod capacity, so new nodes take on more pods as your cluster scales out.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Here are a few examples of workloads that benefit from higher pod density:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Model inference - Serve more model replicas or more models from each GPU instance, including on smaller types such as ml.g5 and ml.g6.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Interactive development - Host more notebooks, IDEs, and data science environments on each node.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;GPU partitioning - Use all the Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) slices on instances such as ml.p5en.48xlarge, where each slice runs its own pod.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Operators and Controllers - Run operators, controllers, and observability agents on CPU nodes alongside your workloads, without crowding them out.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available for HyperPod EKS-orchestrated clusters in all AWS Regions where SageMaker HyperPod is available. To get started, update your cluster's IAM permissions, cluster software, and Amazon VPC CNI add-on. To learn more, please check the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-hyperpod-eks-dynamic-eni-attachment.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 now records the TLS key exchange algorithm in AWS CloudTrail for S3 requests, so you can see which requests and clients use post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS). With this visibility, you can measure the share of your S3 traffic that is protected with post-quantum cryptography, and identify the applications, SDK versions, and IAM principals that still connect with classical key exchange and need to be upgraded.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The new keyExchange value in the tlsDetails field of CloudTrail management and data events shows the key exchange negotiated on each TLS connection, such as the hybrid post-quantum algorithm X25519MLKEM768 or a traditional algorithm such as x25519. For example, you can query your CloudTrail logs to find the user agents of S3 requests that did not use ML-KEM, then prioritize those clients for your post-quantum migration. The keyExchange value is recorded for requests to general purpose buckets, directory buckets, table buckets, and vector buckets, including requests made through AWS PrivateLink.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Key exchange details for S3 requests are available in all AWS Regions at no additional cost. Standard AWS CloudTrail pricing applies for S3 data events. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingEncryptionInTransit.PQ-TLS.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;post-quantum TLS in the Amazon S3 User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-event-reference-record-contents.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudTrail record contents reference&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) now supports associating additional AWS Key Management Service (KMS) keys with EBS snapshots. This new capability enables you to share and copy encrypted snapshots across accounts without first re-encrypting them, simplifying your disaster recovery and backup workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt; 
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Customers use EBS snapshots as durable, point-in-time backups of EBS volumes. By associating an additional customer-managed KMS key (CMK) with a snapshot encrypted with an AWS managed key (AMK), for example, you enable cross-account sharing for that snapshot without re-encrypting its data. This feature accelerates your disaster recovery workflows and enhances operational efficiency for enterprise backup administrators and disaster recovery teams.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt; 
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Support for associating additional KMS keys with Amazon EBS snapshots is available in all AWS commercial, China, AWS GovCloud (US), and AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Regions, plus AWS Local Zones and AWS Outposts. You can access this capability through the AWS Console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), and AWS SDKs. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/associated-kms-keys.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon EBS documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS WAF advanced rate-based rules are now available in the Asia Pacific (Thailand), Mexico (Central), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West) Regions. Customers in these Regions can now aggregate requests using custom keys and choose a 1 or 2 minute evaluation window in addition to the existing 5 and 10 minute options, the same capabilities already available in other AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A rate-based rule counts requests and applies an action, such as Block or CAPTCHA, when the request rate exceeds a limit you set. Previously, rate-based rules in these Regions could aggregate requests only by IP address and used a 5 or 10 minute evaluation window. Now you can aggregate by request components such as HTTP headers, cookies, query strings, URI paths, HTTP methods, and label namespaces, and combine up to five of them into a composite key. For example, you can limit login attempts per IP address and User-Agent pair, or per session cookie, which helps against attacks spread across many IP addresses. A shorter evaluation window detects request floods sooner, and a longer window catches slower, sustained attacks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can configure these options in the AWS WAF console, the AWS CLI and SDKs, and AWS CloudFormation. There is no additional charge for these capabilities, and standard AWS WAF pricing applies. Advanced rate-based rules are now available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS WAF is available and in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-rule-statement-type-rate-based.html" target="_blank"&gt;Using rate-based rule statements&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS WAF Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup logically air-gapped vault now supports Amazon EBS Snapshots Archive. Logically air-gapped vaults are a type of AWS Backup vault that allows secure sharing of backups across accounts and AWS Organizations, supporting direct restore to reduce recovery time from a data loss event.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now archive Amazon EBS backups held in a logically air-gapped vault to Amazon EBS Snapshots Archive, a low-cost tier for snapshots you rarely access. A logically air-gapped vault stores immutable backups that are locked by default, and isolated with encryption using AWS owned keys or customer-managed keys. This helps you meet long-term retention and compliance requirements at lower storage cost.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can get started using the AWS Backup console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. In a backup plan rule that runs monthly or less often, specify a logically air-gapped vault as the primary backup destination or copy destination. Then enable Archive Amazon EBS snapshots in the backup rule lifecycle or copy lifecycle and set the time in warm storage before the backup transitions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This support is available in all AWS Regions where both logically air-gapped vault and Amazon EBS Snapshots Archive are available. For more information and detailed regional availability, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/backup-feature-availability.html#features-by-region" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Backup documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about logically air-gapped vaults, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/logicallyairgappedvault.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon EKS Auto Mode now supports security groups for Pods, letting you apply dedicated Amazon EC2 security group rules to individual Pods. Different workloads on the same node can carry different security groups, giving your Pods network-level segmentation without creating separate node pools for each application.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security groups for Pods extends your existing AWS network controls to the Pod level, so your Pods can reach resources such as Amazon RDS and Amazon ElastiCache databases that authorize connections by security group under the same rules the rest of your VPC uses. You define which Pods receive which security groups with a SecurityGroupPolicy resource, and Pods not matched by a policy keep using their node’s security groups.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Security groups for Pods is included as part of EKS Auto Mode at no additional charge and is available on clusters running Kubernetes version 1.36 or later, in all AWS Regions where EKS Auto Mode is supported. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/auto-sgp.html"&gt;Security groups for Pods&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon EKS User Guide and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/eks/auto-mode/"&gt;Amazon EKS Auto Mode&lt;/a&gt; product page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-174-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/09/2026 13:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS ParallelCluster CLI is an open source command line tool, distributed on PyPI as aws-parallelcluster. We identified CVE-2026-124033, an issue where the export-cluster-logs and export-image-logs commands in the AWS ParallelCluster CLI before version 3.16.0 contain a relative path traversal issue. An authenticated remote user with write access to the log export Amazon S3 bucket could use a crafted S3 object key to write files outside the intended export directory on the machine running the command.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 3.0.0 AND &amp;lt; 3.16.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Refer to the article for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-174-aws/"&gt;Read this article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-173-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/06/2026 09:00 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-122193, an OS command injection issue in an example provided in the set-up documentation. The example included a script that creates AWS Systems Manager (SSM) documents (Rex-RO and Rex-RW) for running scripts on managed instances. Due to how the example script handled input parameters, a user with permissions to invoke the SSM document could supply crafted input that would run unintended operating system commands on the managed instance, outside of the REX sandbox. The scriptArgs parameter is affected by the same class of issue.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This is not an issue in a shipped REX artifact version.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-173-aws/"&gt;Read this article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting November 1, 2026, the Amazon Cognito Service Level Agreement (SLA) commits to a Monthly Uptime Percentage of 99.99% for user pools on the Plus feature plan, up from 99.9%. User pools already on Plus are automatically covered by the new Service Commitment. User pools on Essentials and Lite stay at the 99.9% Service Commitment with no change to their terms.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To qualify for the 99.99% Service Commitment, you can switch a user pool to the Plus feature plan using the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS Software Development Kits (SDKs). The Plus feature plan also includes threat protection features such as adaptive authentication, compromised credentials detection, and threat and user activity logging and export.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For full terms and exclusions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/sla/"&gt;Amazon Cognito SLA&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;To learn more about feature plans, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-sign-in-feature-plans.html"&gt;user pool feature plans&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-098-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/04/2026 10:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;log4j-cve-2021-44228-hotpatch is a tool which injects a Java agent into a running JVM process. The agent will attempt to patch the lookup() method of all loaded org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.JndiLookup instances to unconditionally return the string "Patched JndiLookup::lookup()". It is designed to address the CVE-2021-44228 remote code execution issue in Log4j without restarting the Java process. We identified CVE-2026-85656, an OS command injection issue in the log4j-cve-2021-44228-hotpatch package in Amazon Linux before 1.3-9.amzn2 might allow a local user to execute arbitrary commands with root privileges via a Java process whose executable path contains embedded newline characters.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;=1.3-8.amzn2&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-091-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/28/2026 11:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Systems Manager Agent (amazon-ssm-agent) is Amazon software that runs on Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instances, edge devices, on-premises servers, and virtual machines (VMs). Amazon-ssm-agent makes it possible for Systems Manager to update, manage, and configure these resources. We identified CVE-2026-81849, where an improper limitation of a pathname to a restricted directory in the aws:downloadContent plugin in amazon-ssm-agent before 3.3.4515.0 might allow an authenticated remote user whose ssm:SendCommand permission is restricted to the AWS-DownloadContent document, to write arbitrary files outside the intended download directory with root privileges, via crafted object keys in the S3 source the document is directed to retrieve. This issue may lead to arbitrary code execution as root if specific sensitive files are overwritten.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To remediate this issue, customers should upgrade amazon-ssm-agent to version 3.3.4515.0 or later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; Amazon amazon-ssm-agent from 2.0.767.0 to 3.3.4364.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-085-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/20/2026 13:30 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Improper input validation in the Time Series Visual Builder (TSVB) plugin in OpenSearch Dashboards before 3.8 allows a remote authenticated user with standard data access permissions to execute arbitrary code on the server by sending a crafted JSON payload to the metrics visualization API endpoint. To mitigate this issue, users should upgrade to OpenSearch Dashboards 3.8 or later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted products and versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - OpenSearch-Dashboards (open-source, self-managed): &amp;gt;=3.0.0, &amp;lt;3.8.0&lt;br&gt; - OpenSearch-Dashboards (AWS Managed): &amp;gt;=3.0.0, &amp;lt;3.8.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transfer Family now lets you store SFTP server host keys in AWS Secrets Manager in your own AWS account. This gives you greater control over your server identity and helps simplify server migrations and failover.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, Transfer Family stored host keys within the service, so you could not retrieve the associated private key material. With this launch, you can choose Secrets Manager as the storage type when creating a server or importing a host key. You can then retrieve the private key material and reuse the same host key across servers and AWS Regions. For example, you can use it on a standby server for failover or on a replacement server during migration, without requiring clients to trust a new host key. AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies let you control who can retrieve the stored keys. You can also migrate existing host keys to Secrets Manager, which may require additional steps; see the AWS Transfer Family User Guide for details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Transfer Family is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/configuring-servers-change-host-key.html" target="_blank"&gt;Store SFTP server host keys in AWS Secrets Manager&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Transfer Family User Guide. To get started, go to the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/transfer/home" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Transfer Family console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service now supports index-level encryption, letting you encrypt each index at rest with its own AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer managed key.&amp;nbsp;This helps you protect data with different ownership or sensitivity under separate keys on the same domain. For example, you can encrypt each tenant's data with its own key in a multi-tenant application.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Index-level encryption builds on the existing encryption at rest capability in Amazon OpenSearch Service. To enable it, set the encryption mode to Native and specify a customer managed AWS KMS key when you create a new domain using the AWS Management Console or the AWS CLI. You can then register additional keys with the domain and use them to encrypt individual indexes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Index-level encryption is available for domains running OpenSearch version 3.3 or later in 14 AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris), and South America (São Paulo).&amp;nbsp;There is no additional charge for the feature. Standard AWS KMS and OpenSearch Service charges apply.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a title=" Index-level encryption" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/index-level-encryption.html" target="_blank"&gt;Index-level encryption&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon OpenSearch Service Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 Tables now support up to 100 table buckets per AWS Region in an AWS account, increased from 10. This allows you to create up to 1 million tables per AWS Region in an AWS account. With a higher table bucket allowance, you can create a separate table bucket for each dataset, workload, or team, and apply table bucket-level settings such as encryption, access policies, and replication to each. The higher quota applies by default to all accounts at no additional cost.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;S3 Tables deliver the first cloud object store with built-in Apache Iceberg support, and the easiest way to store tabular data at scale. S3 Tables perform continual table maintenance to automatically optimize query efficiency and storage cost over time, even as your data lake scales and evolves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The higher default quota is available in all AWS Regions&amp;nbsp;where S3 Tables are available. If you need a quota beyond the default, you can request an increase through AWS Support. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;S3 Tables overview page&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-tables.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;user guide&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-tables-regions-quotas.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;quotas documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transfer Family now preserves the client's source IP address using Proxy Protocol v2 (PPv2) when you place a Network Load Balancer (NLB) in front of your SFTP server that uses a VPC-hosted endpoint. You can now retain visibility of the client's source IP for IP-based auditing, access controls, and compliance when you use your own NLB.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, an NLB replaced the client's source IP with its own private IP address, so your Transfer Family logs and events recorded the NLB's address instead of the client's source IP. Because the NLB's private IP was the address presented to your custom identity provider during authentication, you couldn't authorize users based on their true source IP. With this launch, you can enable source IP preservation on your SFTP server so that the client's source IP is preserved. The preserved source IP is recorded in your logs and events and presented to your custom identity provider during authentication. You can enable the feature on each Transfer Family server individually through the console, CLI, or API.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source IP preservation for SFTP servers is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions where AWS Transfer Family is available&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the AWS Transfer Family console or use the AWS CLI/SDK. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/working-with-nlb.html#nlb-sftp-source-ip"&gt;Transfer Family User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Systems Manager extends its diagnosis capability to identify six additional categories of issues that can prevent Amazon EC2 instances and hybrid-activated nodes from becoming managed by Systems Manager. An instance must be managed by Systems Manager before you can patch it, run commands, connect with Session Manager, or collect inventory, and when an instance is unmanaged the cause can be difficult to isolate. The diagnosis previously covered network connectivity, and it now also identifies issues with IAM permissions, SSM Agent version, instance status checks, operating system configuration, Default Host Management Configuration, and hybrid activation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this broader coverage, more of your instances return a specific, actionable cause instead of an unidentified result, so you can bring your fleet under management faster. You run a diagnosis across your instances in the Systems Manager unified console experience, and Systems Manager reports the specific issues it finds in each category. Every diagnosed issue comes with step-by-step guidance to help you resolve it, and for some issues you can also run an AWS Systems Manager Automation runbook from the console to remediate the issue directly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Systems Manager diagnosis capability is available in all AWS Regions that are enabled by default. The capability runs as AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks, so you pay standard Automation usage charges for the runbooks you run; see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/pricing/"&gt;AWS Systems Manager pricing&lt;/a&gt; for details. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/diagnosing-ec2-category-types.html"&gt;AWS Systems Manager User Guide&lt;/a&gt;, or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/"&gt;AWS Systems Manager product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-006-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Informational&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/03/03 10:15 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon RDS/Aurora is a managed relational database service. We identified CVE-2026-3494. In MariaDB server version through 11.8.5, when server audit plugin is enabled with server_audit_events variable configured with QUERY_DCL, QUERY_DDL, or QUERY_DML filtering, if an authenticated database user invokes a SQL statement prefixed with double-hyphen (‐‐) or hash (#) style comments, the statement is not logged.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;- MariaDB Server (10.6.24 and prior, 10.11.15 and prior, 11.4.9 and prior, and 11.8.5 and prior)&lt;br&gt; - Amazon Aurora MySQL (2.12.5 and prior, 3.01.0 to 3.04.5, 3.05.1 to 3.10.2, and 3.11.0)&lt;br&gt; - Amazon RDS for MySQL (5.7.44-RDS.20251212 and prior, 8.0.11 to 8.0.44, and 8.4.3 to 8.4.7)&lt;br&gt; - Amazon RDS for MariaDB (10.6.24 and prior, 10.11.4 to 10.11.15, 11.4.3 to 11.4.9, and 11.8.3 to 11.8.5)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Earlier this month, we announced &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/amazon-bedrock-web/" style="background-color: rgb(255,255,255);"&gt;Web Search on Amazon Bedrock&lt;/a&gt;, a built-in server-side tool that allows you to ground model responses with current web knowledge, while maintaining data within your secured AWS environment with zero data egress. Today, we are expanding Web Search to enable the external_web_access parameter allowing Web Search to retrieve content directly from the public web so models can ground responses in the latest information.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To enable external_web_access, grant the bedrock-websearch:ExternalWebAccess IAM permission to the request identity and leave the external_web_access parameter at its default of true. &amp;nbsp;In doing so, Web Search can then fetch content live from the public web for use cases that need the freshest possible information, such as latest sports score, live pricing, or newly released documentation. If handling sensitive data, to keep retrieval entirely within your AWS boundary, set external_web_access: false. By setting it false, Web Search serves results only from Amazon's in-AWS web index and knowledge graph, with no request data leaving the AWS boundary. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Enabling External Web Access is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), and US West (Oregon). To learn more, read our blog post &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/introducing-web-search-on-amazon-bedrock-for-foundation-model-grounding/"&gt;Introducing Web Search on Amazon Bedrock for foundation model grounding,&lt;/a&gt; review &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/web-search.html#web-search-controlling-external"&gt;Controlling external web access&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock User Guide, and visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock pricing page&lt;/a&gt; for cost details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Aurora PostgreSQL and Aurora MySQL clusters can now move from one Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to another without data migration. You can move a cluster with a single API call or a modify action in the Amazon RDS console. This lets you move a cluster to a VPC with more IP address space or a non-overlapping CIDR range, consolidate your network architecture, or meet changing security and compliance requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, moving an Aurora cluster to a different VPC meant creating a new cluster in the target VPC, migrating your data, and updating your applications with new connection strings. Now, a single ModifyDBCluster API call moves your existing cluster to the new VPC. Your data stays in place, your cluster endpoint stays the same, and connectivity is interrupted only briefly during the move. Before you start, turn off features that are tied to the cluster's current VPC, such as Aurora Global Database, Amazon RDS Proxy, and Amazon RDS Blue/Green Deployments. You can set them up again in the new VPC after the move.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available for Aurora PostgreSQL and Aurora MySQL in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.html#CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.AuroraPostgreSQL.ExpressConfig.SupportedRegions" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora PostgreSQL with express configuration is available. To get started, use the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS console&lt;/a&gt;, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/USER_VPC.WorkingWithRDSInstanceinaVPC.html#USER_VPC.WorkingWithRDSInstanceinaVPC.UpdatingVPC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB) now supports mutual TLS (mTLS) authentication for clients that present X.509 certificates. Until now, customers who needed mTLS with NLB built their own solutions or ran third-party proxies to authenticate clients. With this launch, NLB verifies client certificates on its TLS listeners, so only trusted clients can reach your targets. You can offload the compute-intensive processing required for mTLS authentication to NLB, without adding certificate logic to your backend applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Mutual TLS is commonly used to secure communication between IoT devices, APIs, and microservices, and in regulated environments where both sides must prove their identity. To use mTLS, create a trust store with a certificate authority (CA) certificate bundle from a CA of your choice or AWS Private Certificate Authority, and attach it to a new or existing NLB TLS listener. To block certificates you no longer trust, add one or more certificate revocation lists (CRLs) to the trust store. To admit only specific clients, add client certificate filters that match the Common Name, Organizational Unit, or the DNS name or URI in the Subject Alternative Name, such as a SPIFFE ID. You can set up mTLS with the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI), or APIs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Mutual TLS for NLB is available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions where NLB is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/introducing-mtls-for-network-load-balancer/"&gt;AWS Blog&lt;/a&gt; and the NLB &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/mutual-authentication.html"&gt;User Guide.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront (CF) now supports WebSocket and gRPC traffic to application origins in AWS regions, with mutual TLS authentication between CF and the application origin (origin mTLS). Customers can now use TLS certificates to authenticate requests from CloudFront while setting up WebSocket or gRPC connections to their origins. Origin mTLS protects internet facing applications that need persistent, bidirectional or streaming connections, such as chat platforms, collaborative editing tools or live dashboards from unauthenticated requests.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, customers serving WebSocket or gRPC traffic through CloudFront had to use shared secrets or IP allow-lists to identify requests originating from CloudFront. This was operationally complex and did not provide strong authentication. With Origin mTLS, customers can use one TLS based authentication for HTTP, WebSocket and gRPC traffic to an application origin. This works with application origins within AWS such as Application Load Balancer and API Gateway, on-premises origins, and origins hosted outside AWS.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;There is no additional charge for origin mTLS, and it is available with pay-as-you-go pricing and in the Business and Premium flat-rate pricing plans. To learn more, visit the CloudFront &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/origin-mtls-authentication.html"&gt;origin mutual TLS documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon API Gateway now offers a native JWT authorizer for REST APIs, a new authorizer type that validates JSON Web Tokens directly in the gateway. Until now, validating tokens from an OpenID Connect or OAuth 2.0 identity provider other than Amazon Cognito required a Lambda authorizer. You now configure token validation instead of writing and operating code for it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can define one or more issuers, the audiences accepted for each, the signing algorithms you permit, and any additional claims a token may present. API Gateway verifies each token's signature, issuer, audience, expiry, and claims on every request. You can attach one authorizer to many methods and require different scopes on each. This fits machine-to-machine APIs, mobile and web backends, and multi-tenant APIs that accept tokens from several identity providers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The JWT authorizer works on Regional, edge-optimized, and private REST APIs, and is available in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/rest-api-jwt-authorizer.html"&gt;Amazon API Gateway documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/securing-rest-apis-with-jwt-authorizers-in-amazon-api-gateway/"&gt;AWS blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon API Gateway now offers a native JWT authorizer for REST APIs, a new authorizer type that validates JSON Web Tokens directly in the gateway. Until now, validating tokens from an OpenID Connect or OAuth 2.0 identity provider other than Amazon Cognito required a Lambda authorizer. You now configure token validation instead of writing and operating code for it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can define one or more issuers, the audiences accepted for each, the signing algorithms you permit, and any additional claims a token may present. API Gateway verifies each token's signature, issuer, audience, expiry, and claims on every request. You can attach one authorizer to many methods and require different scopes on each. This fits machine-to-machine APIs, mobile and web backends, and multi-tenant APIs that accept tokens from several identity providers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The JWT authorizer works on Regional, edge-optimized, and private REST APIs, and is available in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/rest-api-jwt-authorizer.html"&gt;Amazon API Gateway documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/securing-rest-apis-with-jwt-authorizers-in-amazon-api-gateway/"&gt;AWS blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-185-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/16/2026 12:30 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Improper restriction of intended endpoints in the connection configuration handling of the AWS Labs Timestream for InfluxDB MCP Server (awslabs.timestream-for-influxdb-mcp-server) before 0.0.19 might allow a remote unauthenticated actor (via prompt injection) to obtain the InfluxDB API token configured on the server, sent to a crafted endpoint controlled through an InfluxDB URL introduced in the MCP client context.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;=0.0.12 AND &amp;lt;0.0.19&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-185-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-178-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/10/2026 12:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Incorrect default file permissions in Kiro CLI before version 2.27.0 on macOS and Linux might allow other local users on the same host to read a user's session transcripts and prompt history, including any credentials or source code they contain. The CLI created its local session storage files with permissions that made them readable by all local users.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; Kiro CLI &amp;gt;= 1.16.0 AND &amp;lt; 2.27.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-178-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-177-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/10/2026 10:45 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Untrusted search path in the cross-user process launcher in Open Job Description (OpenJD) Sessions for Python before 0.11.0, as used in the AWS Deadline Cloud worker agent before 0.33.0, on Linux might allow an authenticated remote user with permission to submit jobs to a queue to execute arbitrary code as the worker agent OS user and obtain the AWS credentials held by the worker agent via a crafted job that modifies the PATH environment variable.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - openjd-sessions &amp;lt; 0.11.0&lt;br&gt; - deadline-cloud-worker-agent &amp;lt; 0.33.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-177-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/"&gt;Amazon Route 53 Profiles&lt;/a&gt; now lets you associate more than one Profile with a VPC. Previously, a VPC could have only one Profile, so teams that managed Route 53 configurations separately (for example, security teams managing DNS Firewall rule groups while cloud infrastructure and network admin teams manage private hosted zones and Resolver forwarding rules) all had to share a single Profile and associate their private hosted zones, Resolver rules, and DNS Firewall rule groups with it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can now associate multiple Profiles with the same VPC and set a priority for every Profile. Route 53 evaluates a VPC's Profiles in priority order, starting with the lowest number, so you can keep shared Route 53 configurations in one Profile and team-specific configurations in another without merging them, while assigning them the right priority. Combined with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/amazon-route-53-profiles-granular-iam/"&gt;the granular IAM permissions&lt;/a&gt; launched in March 2026, multiple Profiles per VPC improves security posture by letting each team own and manage its own Profile with least-privilege permissions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where Route 53 Profiles is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Each Profile-VPC association is billed at standard Route 53 Profiles pricing. For details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Route 53 pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profiles.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Route 53 Profiles&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Route 53 Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS WAF AI traffic monetization now integrates with Stripe. Publishers, content owners, and API providers can collect stablecoin payments from AI bots and agents that settle in local currency directly to their Stripe balance. With this launch, customers can monetize agent access without holding stablecoins or managing a crypto wallet. Stripe is now available as a new payout method alongside existing stablecoin payouts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, connect your Stripe account to AWS WAF through Stripe Connect in the AWS WAF console. Your existing pricing and agent policies work unchanged. When an agent requests protected content, AWS WAF returns an HTTP 402 response with the valid payment credential types that it accepts. Once the agent returns with a valid payment credential and makes the request again, the content is returned ungated. Revenue from all payment providers appears in the AWS WAF console revenue analytics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Stripe integration is available at no additional charge to all AWS WAF customers using Amazon CloudFront, in all edge locations where AWS WAF web ACLs are associated with CloudFront distributions. Standard AWS WAF and Stripe processing fees apply. See &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/waf/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF pricing&lt;/a&gt; for details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/console/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF console&lt;/a&gt; or explore the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-ai-traffic-monetization-getting-started.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS WAF Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2), Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC), and AWS Global View now display related resources for all resource types in their consoles. Previously, only security groups had an option to view related resources. Now you can see relationships for all EC2 and VPC resource types, including EC2 instances, Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) volumes, Launch Templates, Auto Scaling groups, subnets, route tables, and more. The Related Resources tab has also been moved to the console toolbar for easier access.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability helps you better understand your infrastructure dependencies and troubleshoot issues more efficiently. For example, when investigating an EC2 instance issue, you can quickly view its associated EBS volumes, security groups, subnets, and route tables without navigating between multiple console pages. Network administrators can now trace connections from a subnet to its associated route tables, network ACLs, and instances, streamlining network configuration reviews and security audits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is now available in all commercial AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit Amazon EC2 and Amazon VPC in the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Management Console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;introduces support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, providing you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in transit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL versions 18 and higher support modification of the ssl_groups parameter. You can &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraPostgreSQL.Security.html#PostgreSQL.Concepts.General.SSL.Groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;select cryptographic groups&lt;/a&gt; from the&amp;nbsp;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL allow list to align with your organization's security standards.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Aurora is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full PostgreSQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-083-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/18/2026 12:30 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ion-java is a Java library that implements the Amazon Ion data format specification. We identified CVE-2026-75935, memory-amplification denial of service via declared-length preallocation, and CVE-2026-75936, memory-amplification denial of service via highly compressed data expansion.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Affected versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 1.12.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Studio now lets new customers sign up with their Google, GitHub, or Apple credentials and land inside a fully configured Studio environment - no traditional AWS account setup and no console navigation required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, new customers who discovered a model on Hugging Face or an AWS product page had to complete a multi-step AWS account creation process, provide a credit card, navigate the AWS Console to find SageMaker AI, configure an environment, and set up IAM permissions before they could begin working. With earlier launches, we reduced environment setup to twenty seconds, pre-configured model customization permissions, and introduced deep links from Hugging Face and AWS product pages so customers could land directly inside Studio with a model pre-loaded. Now, with new sign-in options, customers complete a simplified sign-up using an existing identity, receive free credits as part of the AWS Free Tier with no credit card required in most cases, and arrive inside a ready-to-use SageMaker Studio environment where they can immediately fine-tune, evaluate, and deploy foundation models. Customers can invite team members by email, and collaborators sign in with their own email credentials to access the shared Studio environment, including shared models, notebooks, and endpoints without configuring IAM users or permissions. When a project grows beyond free credits, customers can upgrade to a paid plan and set a spend limit to stay within budget. Verified customers continue to receive default GPU access to G5, G6, G6e, and G4dn instances across endpoint deployments, training jobs, and notebooks without requesting quota increases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Commercial Regions where Amazon SageMaker Studio is supported. To get started, visit any supported model on &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/models"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hugging Face&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and select "Customize and Deploy on SageMaker AI" or "Deploy on SageMaker AI," or click Get Started from the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/studio/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; page. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/sign-in-new.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS sign-up documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/onboard-quick-start.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio setup guide&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Studio now lets new customers sign up with their Google, GitHub, or Apple credentials and land inside a fully configured Studio environment - no traditional AWS account setup, no credit card, and no console navigation required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, new customers who discovered a model on Hugging Face or an AWS product page had to complete a multi-step AWS account creation process, provide a credit card, navigate the AWS Console to find SageMaker AI, configure an environment, and set up IAM permissions before they could begin working. With earlier launches, we reduced environment setup to twenty seconds, pre-configured model customization permissions, and introduced deep links from Hugging Face and AWS product pages so customers could land directly inside Studio with a model pre-loaded. Now, with social sign-in, new customers complete a simplified sign-up using an existing identity, receive free credits as part of the AWS Free Tier with no credit card required, and arrive inside a ready-to-use SageMaker Studio environment where they can immediately fine-tune, evaluate, and deploy foundation models. Customers can invite team members by email, and collaborators sign in with their own social credentials to access the shared Studio environment, including shared models, notebooks, and endpoints without configuring IAM users or permissions. When a project grows beyond free credits, customers can upgrade to a paid plan and set a spend limit to stay within budget. Verified customers continue to receive default GPU access to G5, G6, G6e, and G4dn instances across endpoint deployments, training jobs, and notebooks without requesting quota increases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Commercial Regions where Amazon SageMaker Studio is supported. To get started, visit any supported model on &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/models"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hugging Face&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and select "Customize and Deploy on SageMaker AI" or "Deploy on SageMaker AI," or click Get Started from the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/studio/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; page. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/sign-in-new.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS sign-up documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/onboard-quick-start.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio setup guide&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Secrets Manager now extends its managed external secrets capability to include Databricks and Twilio, enabling you to automatically rotate the credentials that your data pipelines and messaging applications use to call these services. You set up rotation directly from the AWS Management Console or API without writing or maintaining any custom rotation Lambda functions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Long-lived credentials often stay unrotated because rotation is hard to set up and hard to distribute safely to the applications that use the updated credential. With managed external secrets, you can use Secrets Manager to rotate the Databricks personal access tokens that your jobs and continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines use. You can also rotate the service principal OAuth client secrets that your automation workflows use. Each replacement personal access token retains the scopes of the token it replaces, so a narrowly scoped token stays narrowly scoped.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For Twilio, you can use Secrets Manager to rotate the API keys and Auth Tokens that your applications use to send messages and place calls. For both services, you store the credential in Secrets Manager as a managed external secret and set a rotation schedule. Secrets Manager always returns the current credential, so your applications pick up rotated values automatically, with no code changes required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These integrations join existing managed external secrets support for BigID, Cisco Security Platform, Confluent Cloud, Datadog, GitLab, Jenkins, MongoDB Atlas, Netskope, Paddle, Salesforce, Snowflake, and SonarQube.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Databricks and Twilio managed external secrets are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets is supported. To learn more, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/managed-external-secrets.html"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports GLM 5.3 from Z.ai, giving you a powerful new option for agentic coding and long-horizon software engineering work. Customers using GLM 5.3 on Amazon Bedrock have access to the latest open weight model with the AWS security and compliance posture.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GLM 5.3 is Z.ai’s flagship model, a mixture-of-experts architecture with 753B total parameters and roughly 40B active per token, built on the same base model as GLM 5.2 with all improvements driven by scaled post-training. It combines a 1-million-token context window with up to 128K output tokens, and reasoning is always enabled with selectable effort levels so you can trade latency and token consumption against task performance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bedrock support for GLM 5.3 includes explicit prompt caching with cache points on system prompts and messages, helping you reduce latency and input costs when reusing context across model calls.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GLM 5.3 is available to eligible enterprise customers, and is accessible through the US and Global cross-Region inference profiles. To get started with GLM 5.3, you can access the model in the&lt;a href="https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/home?region=us-east-1#/"&gt; Amazon Bedrock console&lt;/a&gt; or programmatically through supported Amazon Bedrock APIs. For information about supported&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-compatibility.html"&gt; AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;, endpoints, APIs, features, inference profiles and pricing, see the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-zai-glm-5-3.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, read the launch &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/introducing-glm-5-3-on-amazon-bedrock/"&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-122-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 10/01/2026 13:30 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Ion Python is an open-source Python implementation of the Amazon Ion data notation. We identified CVE-2026-104020, an issue in the Ion reader in Amazon Ion Python before version 0.15.0 where a crafted, deeply nested Ion value could cause the application to raise an error or crash, resulting in a denial of service.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 0.15.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-121-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 10/01/2026 10:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;security-agent-mcp-server is an open-source Model Context Protocol (MCP) server, published by AWS in the awslabs/mcp repository, that AI assistants use to run local security scans (including differential "diff" scans) over source code. We identified CVE-2026-97662, an argument injection issue in the diff scan operation: a crafted reference value supplied to the scan is interpreted as a command-line option rather than a revision, which lets a context-dependent actor create, overwrite, or truncate arbitrary files on the host outside the intended workspace directory, bypassing the server's workspace-confinement control.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 0.1.1 AND &amp;lt; 0.2.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Systems Manager now lets you share your Systems Manager Documents (SSM Documents) with an entire AWS organization or with specific organizational units (OUs) using AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM). Previously, you could only share a document publicly or with a list of individual account IDs. You can now easily share a document with your organization or OUs, and Systems Manager automatically keeps that sharing up to date as accounts are added to or removed from your organization or OU, so you no longer need to track and update accounts manually.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To share a document, you create an AWS RAM resource share, add the SSM Documents you want to share, and select the organizations or OUs to share them with. Because sharing is managed through AWS RAM and resource-based policies, access is granted through standard AWS authorization. When you share a document with an account outside your organization, that account receives a resource share invitation and gains access only after it accepts, giving both the document owner and the consumer more control over shared access.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in the Systems Manager console, the AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI), and the AWS SDKs, in all AWS Regions where AWS Systems Manager is available. There is no additional charge to share documents through AWS RAM. To get started, open the Documents page in the Systems Manager console, or see the AWS Systems Manager User Guide at &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/documents-ssm-sharing.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/documents-ssm-sharing.html&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/postgresql/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL&lt;/a&gt; introduces support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, providing you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in-transit.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;RDS for PostgreSQL versions 18 and higher support modification of the ssl_groups parameter. You can &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/PostgreSQL.Concepts.General.SSL.html#PostgreSQL.Concepts.General.SSL.Groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;select cryptographic groups&lt;/a&gt; from the RDS for PostgreSQL allow list to align with your organization's security standards.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL makes it simple to set up, operate, and scale PostgreSQL deployments in the cloud. Create or update a fully managed Amazon RDS database in the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS Management Console&lt;/a&gt; or by using the AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/"&gt;Amazon Elastic Container Service&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon ECS) now supports the IAM condition keys for CPU and memory resources on the RunTask and StartTask APIs. Administrators can use these keys to enforce consistent CPU and memory limits across all methods of launching ECS tasks. This helps organizations prevent unexpected cost overruns and keep workloads aligned with their resource policies.
 &lt;br&gt;
 Previously, the ecs:task-cpu and ecs:task-memory condition keys were available only on the RegisterTaskDefinition, CreateService, and UpdateService APIs. These condition keys are now extended on the RunTask and StartTask APIs. Now, IAM policies that reference these condition keys are evaluated when tasks are launched through RunTask and StartTask as well, giving administrators a single, unified mechanism to control resource allocation across their ECS environments.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This enhancement is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon ECS is available, at no additional cost. To learn more about using condition keys with Amazon ECS, refer to our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/service-authorization/latest/reference/list_amazonelasticcontainerservice.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon EMR now gives you more time, at no additional cost, to migrate off older releases before they reach end of support. If you are actively migrating, your workloads on eligible releases keep receiving support while you move to a current release, with no opt-in and at no charge.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To give you more time to plan and migrate, Amazon EMR provides critical security fixes for releases 5.36 and 6.6 through 6.15 on a best-effort basis through June 30, 2027, and extended Standard Support for 7.0 through 7.10 releases through August 31, 2027. To receive an extension, contact AWS Support with your migration plan and any help you need with the upgrade.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This applies to all deployment models of Amazon EMR (EMR on EC2, EMR on EKS, and EMR Serverless), in all AWS Regions where Amazon EMR is available. For the full list of releases and their support dates, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-standard-support.html"&gt;Amazon EMR standard support policy&lt;/a&gt;. To get the extended support, contact AWS Support with your migration plan, or use the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/spark-upgrades.html"&gt;Apache Spark Upgrade Agent&lt;/a&gt; to move your applications to a current release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transfer Family SFTP Connectors now continue running file transfers while you rotate the credentials used to authenticate with remote SFTP servers. You no longer need to update the connector to point to a new secret version each time a credential rotates, removing a manual step and helping avoid failed transfers during the rotation window.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Connectors can now retrieve credentials from an ordered list of AWS Secrets Manager version stages, such as the current and previous versions, during authentication. The connector automatically tries each version in the order you specify and proceeds with the first that succeeds, so transfers continue uninterrupted as credentials are rotated on either side. You configure this when you create or update a connector, and the connector's credentials must be stored in AWS Secrets Manager.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Support for continuing transfers during credential rotation is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Transfer Family SFTP Connectors are supported. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Transfer Family User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. Get started with AWS Transfer Family in the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/transfer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Transfer Family console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition 8.4 will support MySQL 8.4.8. In addition to several security enhancements and bug fixes, Aurora MySQL 8.4.8 includes several improvements, such as support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, transaction timeout, multi-source replication, and delayed replication. PQ-TLS provides you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in-transit. Transaction timeout helps prevent performance issues caused by long-running transactions blocking innoDB purge.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Multi-source replication allows consolidation of data from multiple sources into a single replica to enable critical use-cases, such as merging shards, reporting, and backups. Delayed replication lets you set a configurable replication lag to protect against accidental data loss. To learn more about these replication features, please refer to the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/amazon-aurora-mysql-multisourcerep-delayedrep/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;launch announcement&lt;/a&gt;. For more details, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraMySQLReleaseNotes/AuroraMySQL.Updates.84Updates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora MySQL 8.4&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/8.4/en/news-8-4-8.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MySQL 8.4.8&lt;/a&gt; release notes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can upgrade your databases during scheduled maintenance windows using automatic minor version upgrades. To simplify operations at scale, enable &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Updates.AMVU.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automatic minor version upgrades&lt;/a&gt; and use the AWS Organizations &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Aurora.Maintenance.AMVU.UpgradeRollout.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Upgrade Rollout Policy&lt;/a&gt; to orchestrate upgrades across your clusters in phases. You can perform minor version upgrades in-place or via snapshot restore. This release is supported in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Concepts.RegionsAndAvailabilityZones.html#Aurora.Overview.Availability.MySQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora MySQL is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full MySQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at Aurora’s &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_AuroraOverview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) now supports in-place major version upgrades (MVU) directly from engine versions 3.6 and 4.0 to version 8.0. This upgrade capability removes the need for intermediate version upgrades and allows you to upgrade your version 3.6 or 4.0 clusters while preserving existing data, configurations, and cluster settings.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Upgrading to version 8.0 provides access to the latest security patches, performance improvements, and new developer capabilities. Major version upgrades from DocumentDB 3.6 and 4.0 to 8.0 are available in all AWS regions where these versions are currently supported.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about upgrading to Amazon DocumentDB 8.0, including detailed guidance on version differences and upgrade paths, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/documentdb/latest/devguide/docdb-mvu.html#mvu-36-to-50-differences"&gt;Amazon DocumentDB MVU documentation&lt;/a&gt;, review &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/documentdb/latest/devguide/docdb-version-support-dates.html"&gt;version support dates&lt;/a&gt;, and explore the full list of new capabilities on the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/documentdb-8-o/"&gt;Amazon DocumentDB 8.0 announcement page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda MicroVMs now supports AWS PrivateLink, enabling private connectivity to Lambda MicroVMs directly from Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) resources without exposing traffic to the public internet. This launch helps regulated workloads in financial services, healthcare, and government meet strict network isolation requirements when building with Lambda MicroVMs.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;PrivateLink VPC Endpoints deliver private connectivity to AWS services from your VPC, ensuring that your traffic does not travel over the public internet when communicating with AWS services. Today's launch extends this capability to Lambda MicroVMs. Developers and IT teams needing private connectivity to Lambda MicroVMs can now create PrivateLink VPC Endpoints in their VPC to call MicroVM APIs (for example, to create MicroVM images or launch MicroVMs) and to connect to each MicroVM’s HTTP endpoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;PrivateLink VPC Endpoints for Lambda MicroVMs can be created using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS CloudFormation, or the AWS SDKs. This feature is supported in all Regions where Lambda MicroVMs is available. Visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt; for the latest region availability.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about using PrivateLink with Lambda MicroVMs, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/microvms-networking.html"&gt;developer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For pricing details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/privatelink/pricing/"&gt;AWS PrivateLink Pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about configuring private connectivity for AWS services, visit the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/interface-endpoints.html"&gt;AWS PrivateLink developer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-003-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/01/23 12:30 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Firecracker is an open source virtualization technology that is purpose-built for creating and managing secure, multi-tenant container and function-based services. Firecracker runs in user space and uses the Linux Kernel-based Virtual Machine (KVM) to create microVMs. Each Firecracker microVM is further isolated with common Linux user-space security barriers by a companion program called "jailer". The jailer provides a second line of defense in case a user escapes from the microVM boundaries and it is released at each Firecracker version.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We are aware of CVE-2026-1386, an issue that is related to the Firecracker jailer, which under certain circumstances can allow an user to overwrite arbitrary files in the host filesystem.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS services that use Firecracker are not impacted by the issue as we appropriately restrict access to the host and the jailer folder, blocking the preconditions required for the attack to happen.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; Firecracker version v1.13.1 and earlier and 1.14.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-001-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/01/09 13:15 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kiro is an agentic IDE users install on their desktop. We identified CVE-2026-0830 where opening a maliciously crafted workspace may lead to arbitrary command injection in Kiro IDE before Kiro version 0.6.18. This may occur if the workspace has specially crafted folder names within the workspace containing injected commands.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Resolution:&lt;/b&gt; Kiro IDE &amp;lt;0.6.18&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-009-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/03/17 12:15 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kiro is an AI-powered IDE for agentic software development. We identified CVE-2026-4295, where improper trust boundary enforcement allowed arbitrary code execution when a user opened a maliciously crafted project directory.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 0.8.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-019-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/04/24 13:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Multiple security issues have been identified in the tough library and tuftool CLI utility. tough is a Rust library used for generating, signing, and managing TUF (The Update Framework) repositories, and tuftool is the command-line interface for repository management Operations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The following issues have been identified:&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-6966&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-6967&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-6968&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - tough: versions 0.1.0 through 0.21.x (inclusive)&lt;br&gt; - tuftool: versions 0.1.0 through 0.14.x (inclusive)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-199-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/24/2026 10:30 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Graph Explorer is an open-source tool for browsing and searching graph data, which customers self-host against Amazon Neptune or other graph databases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-134066, an issue in the query construction of Graph Explorer before version 3.2.1. Graph Explorer inserted property keys and labels read from the connected graph database into Gremlin, openCypher and SPARQL query text without escaping. A user with write access to the connected graph database could create a crafted property key or label that, when another user performs a search, causes Graph Explorer to run unintended query logic that accesses and modifies data in that database with Graph Explorer's privileges. On Amazon Neptune, the SPARQL path is not affected.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 3.2.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-199-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-195-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/20/2026 10:30 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Improper limitation of a pathname to a restricted directory in the file access checks in Amazon awslabs.document-loader-mcp-server 1.0.10 through 1.0.20 might allow a context-dependent actor, via crafted document content processed by an MCP client, to read files outside the intended base directory and, in 1.0.12 through 1.0.20, to write PNG slide images outside it. This occurs when DOCUMENT_BASE_DIR is not set or is empty and the CI, GITHUB_ACTIONS or PYTEST_CURRENT_TEST environment variable is present. Versions before 1.0.10 do not restrict file access to a base directory.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 1.0.10 AND &amp;lt; 1.0.21&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-195-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-193-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/19/2026 15:05 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On November 18, 2026, the Ruby maintainers published fixes for seven issues in CRuby's regular expression engine and string case conversion, including CVE-2026-132825. These issues affect Ruby versions before 4.0.10, 3.4.13, and 3.3.14 and could allow remote code execution in some configurations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These issues are memory-safety issues in Ruby's regular expression engine and in string case conversion on certain non-UTF-8 encodings. When an application processes third party-controlled input through these code paths, a remote, unauthenticated actor might be able to crash the process, read memory, or run arbitrary code. Applications built on Rack, including Rails and Sinatra, might be reachable through default request handling.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-193-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon WorkSpaces Applications now supports WebAuthn redirection, so users can sign in to websites and applications in their streaming session with a FIDO2 authenticator on their local device, such as a security key, Windows Hello, or Touch ID. Previously, an authenticator attached to the user's computer was not available inside the streaming session, so organizations that require phishing-resistant multi-factor authentication (MFA) had to use weaker sign-in methods or keep those applications off WorkSpaces Applications. This helps organizations with FIDO2 requirements stream those applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When an application in the session makes a WebAuthn request, WorkSpaces Applications forwards it to the user's device, where the authenticator signs it and returns the response. Private keys never leave the user's device. Administrators turn it on per stack in the console or API, and on Windows fleets can limit it to an allow list of processes. For example, a bank can let employees sign in to Microsoft 365 in their streaming session with the same security key they use on their laptop.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;WebAuthn redirection is available at no additional charge in all AWS commercial Regions and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions where WorkSpaces Applications is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, select Enable WebAuthn redirection under User Settings for your stack, use an image with the WorkSpaces Applications agent released on or after October 20, 2026, and connect with the WorkSpaces Applications client for Windows (1.5.0 or later) or macOS (1.6.0 or later). To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/feature-support-webauthn-redirection.html" target="_blank"&gt;WebAuthn redirection&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon WorkSpaces Applications Administration Guide, or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/workspaces/applications/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon WorkSpaces Applications&lt;/a&gt; product page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Revenue Measurement (previously Partner Revenue Measurement) provides measurement of AWS consumption driven by Partner solutions. Today, AWS announces Identity Tagging in AWS Revenue Measurement, a new way for Partners to get attributed revenue visibility for the AWS usage their solutions drive. Partners can now tag the IAM user or role their solution uses instead of tagging individual resources or decorating individual SDK/API calls. This makes attribution simpler and needs no code changes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Until now, Partners using Resource Tagging had to apply tags to each resource their solution created or managed. That could be hard to keep consistent across customer environments. With Identity Tagging, Partners apply a tag to the IAM role or user their solution uses in the AWS account. AWS Revenue Measurement then attributes usage from resources created by that identity to the Partner's product. Identity Tagging works alongside existing methods such as Resource Tagging, User Agent string, and SaaS Account Tagging, so Partners can pick the method that best fits their architecture.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Revenue Measurement is generally available in all commercial regions. To learn more, review the AWS Revenue Measurement &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/PRM/latest/aws-prm-onboarding-guide/what-is-service.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;onboarding guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Continuum for Penetration Testing (formerly AWS Security Agent) now lets you pause a running penetration test job and resume it later from its last saved checkpoint. Continuum for Penetration Testing runs on-demand, AI-driven penetration tests against your web applications and APIs. Previously, a pen test job that failed due to client configuration error could not continue, so security teams had to start a new pen test job and repeat work the agent had already done.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When you resume a paused or failed pen test job, it keeps its findings and completed work and continues where it left off. You can pause a pen test job during a deployment or maintenance window, raise the task-hour limit of a pen test job that has reached it, or replace test credentials that expired mid-test. Before you resume, you can also update the network traffic settings or VPC configuration, while the pen test job's scope, including its target endpoints, stays the same.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can pause and resume pen test jobs in the web application or with the StopPentestJob and ResumePentestJob APIs. You can resume a pen test job within 7 days of its creation. Pause and resume is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Continuum for Penetration Testing is available. To learn more, visit our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityagent/latest/userguide/pause-resume-penetration-test.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon VPC Flow Logs now adds Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) metadata to flow log records. Each record identifies the pod that sent or received the traffic, along with its cluster, namespace and labels. This makes it faster to investigate security events, understand traffic patterns, and troubleshoot network issues in your EKS clusters, without running monitoring software on your nodes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon EKS runs pods on Amazon EC2 instances, called nodes, and many pods can share one node's network interfaces. Previously, flow log records showed the network interface and IP address for each flow, not the pod behind it. Pods are short-lived, so by the time you investigated unexpected traffic, that IP address could belong to a different pod. To trace traffic back to the right pod, teams built custom tools or ran monitoring software on every node. Now, each record names the pod that sent or received the traffic, so one log gives you both VPC and Kubernetes context. For example, if a pod sends unexpected traffic to a database in your VPC, the record shows the pod and its namespace, even after that pod is replaced.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a flow log for your cluster's VPC or subnets, and add the EKS fields to the log format. You don't need to change your cluster or applications. The fields are available for pods on Amazon EC2 instances, including EKS Auto Mode, managed node groups and self-managed nodes, that use the Amazon VPC CNI plugin for Kubernetes. Logs go to the same destinations as before, so your existing analytics tools keep working. EKS metadata is available at no additional charge. Standard VPC Flow Logs charges for log delivery and storage still apply. This feature is supported in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available. For more information, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-log-records.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon VPC Flow Logs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now replicate data in Amazon S3 buckets with fewer replication rules using two new S3 Replication capabilities, exclusions and Multi-Region key resolution. Buckets that many tenants or applications share often need many rules to skip certain data or to match encryption keys in each AWS Region. With these capabilities, customers who manage multi-tenant and shared buckets can replace many replication rules with a single rule.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With exclusions, you can replicate an entire bucket while skipping specific prefixes or tagged objects, such as logs or experimental data. With Multi-Region key resolution, S3 Replication encrypts each replica with the matching AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) Multi-Region key in the destination Region, so replicas meet the same encryption requirements as their source objects. For example, a bucket that stores data for hundreds of tenants, each with its own Multi-Region key, can replicate with a single rule, including tenants you add later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Exclusions and Multi-Region key resolution are optional and available at no additional charge in all AWS Regions. To get started, use the AWS SDKs, AWS CLI, S3 API, or S3 console. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/replication/" target="_blank"&gt;S3 Replication product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/replication.html" target="_blank"&gt;Replicating objects within and across Regions&lt;/a&gt; in the S3 User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transit Gateway now supports context sharing with security services, such as attached firewalls, allowing you to use Amazon Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs), VPC subnets, VPC security groups, and transit gateway attachments in firewall policies alongside IP addresses. Until now, attached firewalls segmented traffic by IP address, which is hard to plan and maintain, or by resource tags, which firewalls periodically synchronize with IP addresses and can miss new workloads in between. With this feature, you can enforce segmentation based on how workloads and attachments are defined in AWS.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Transit Gateway embeds resource context in the traffic it sends to your firewall, which can enforce the right policy from the first packet. For example, you can apply threat protection to traffic between web-tier and database-tier security groups, block development VPCs from reaching production VPCs, apply a common inspection policy to traffic entering AWS through AWS Direct Connect and AWS Site-to-Site VPN attachments, or apply policies to short-lived AWS Lambda functions as soon as they send traffic, all without updating rules as IP addresses change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall and Palo Alto Networks Software Firewalls support policies using resource context alongside IP-based rules. To get started, create a context store in AWS Network Manager and associate your transit gateways. The context store monitors resources connected to those transit gateways and shares their context with firewalls.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Context sharing with security services is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS Transit Gateway is available, except Middle East (Bahrain) and Middle East (UAE). It is also available in the AWS GovCloud (US-East) and AWS GovCloud (US-West) Regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transit-gateway/"&gt;AWS Transit Gateway product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-manager/latest/tgwnm/context-stores.html" target="_blank"&gt;context sharing with security services documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-183-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/16/2026 10:30 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;External control of a file path in Amazon Deep Java Library (DJL) versions 0.7.0 through 0.38.0 might allow a remote unauthenticated third party to read arbitrary files that the library process can access. To do this, the third party supplies a crafted model archive.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;=0.7.0, &amp;lt;=0.38.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-183-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup logically air-gapped vault now supports Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) DB instances. Logically air-gapped vaults are a type of AWS Backup vault that stores immutable, isolated backups, which are locked by default with AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode. Secure sharing across accounts and AWS Organizations enables direct restore, reducing recovery time from a data loss event.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now protect your Amazon RDS DB instances in logically air-gapped vaults. A logically air-gapped vault stores immutable backups that are locked by default, and isolated with encryption using AWS owned keys or customer-managed keys. This helps reduce the risk of downtime, ensure business continuity, and meet compliance requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, use the AWS Backup console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. Assign your RDS DB instances to a backup plan and specify a logically air-gapped vault in the same AWS Region as the copy destination. Share the vault for recovery or restore testing with other accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), or safeguard vault access during account compromise using &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/multipartyapproval.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Multi-party approval&lt;/a&gt;. Once shared, you can initiate direct restore jobs from that account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This support is available in most AWS Regions where both logically air-gapped vault and Amazon RDS are available. For more information and detailed regional availability, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/backup-feature-availability.html#features-by-resource" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Backup documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about logically air-gapped vaults for RDS, read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/introducing-aws-backup-logically-air-gapped-vault-support-for-amazon-rds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;launch blog&lt;/a&gt; and visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/logicallyairgappedvault.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;feature documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity now supports OAuth 2.0 authorization server discovery URLs for both inbound JWT authorization and outbound OAuth credential providers. This update enables developers to configure AgentCore with OAuth 2.0 authorization servers that do not use OpenID Connect (OIDC).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, AgentCore Identity accepted only OIDC discovery URLs. Authorization servers that implement OAuth 2.0 without OIDC publish their metadata at a different well-known path and could not be used with AgentCore. With this launch, you can use either OIDC or OAuth 2.0 authorization server discovery URLs when you configure a JWT authorizer or a custom OAuth credential provider on AgentCore.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Bedrock AgentCore is available, at no additional cost. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/inbound-jwt-authorizer.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Configure inbound JWT authorizer&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/identity-add-oauth-client-custom.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Add OAuth client using custom provider&lt;/a&gt; in the &lt;i&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Developer Guide&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-182-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/12/2026 13:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An untrusted search path issue in Aurora PostgreSQL dialect detection in the AWS Advanced Go Wrapper from release 2026-04-06 (awssql/v2.0.0) and before release 2026-07-29 (awssql/v2.0.3) could allow a remote authenticated user to escalate privileges to those of another database user, including a member of the rds_superuser role, via a crafted function that runs when that user connects to an Aurora PostgreSQL cluster through an affected version of the wrapper.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; AWS Advanced Go Wrapper &amp;gt;=2026-04-06, &amp;lt;2026-07-29&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-182-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-181-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/12/2026 13:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-127119, an issue in the EMR module of AWS SDK for pandas (awswrangler) from version 1.1.0 before 3.18.0. When a cluster is created without an explicit log location, the library uses a predictable default S3 bucket name for the cluster logs without verifying that the caller's account owns that bucket. A remote actor who creates that bucket first could read the cluster's logs, including job step output.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 1.1.0 AND &amp;lt; 3.18.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-181-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon MWAA Serverless now supports DuckDB operators, enabling you to analyze and transform data with high-performance in-memory analytics directly in your workflows. You can query data in Amazon S3 or run analytics locally, without manual credential configuration. DuckDB operators are also supported in SageMaker Unified Studio visual workflows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started in MWAA Serverless, add a DuckDB task to your workflow definition. A pre-created default connection automatically uses your workflow's execution role credentials for S3 access, so you can start analyzing data immediately. In SageMaker Unified Studio, add DuckDB operators from the task panel to your visual workflow canvas.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;DuckDB operators are available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/what-is-mwaa-serverless.html#regions" target="_blank"&gt;Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon MWAA Serverless is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/mwaa-serverless-userguide/code-examples-duckdb.html" target="_blank"&gt;Using DuckDB in Amazon MWAA Serverless workflows&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/serverless-visual-workflows.html" target="_blank"&gt;Using serverless visual workflows&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/athena/"&gt;Amazon Athena&lt;/a&gt; now offers system tables which automatically capture your usage data and make it available for observability, usage analysis, and audit use cases. When you enable system tables, usage data is available within minutes of query completion across your workgroups and stored as service-managed, read-only Iceberg tables on &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/"&gt;Amazon S3 Tables&lt;/a&gt; with configurable retention. You can query it directly with Athena, while AWS manages the tables for you, including schema definition, partitioning, compaction, and snapshot maintenance, removing all operational burden.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Athena provides usage data through APIs like GetQueryExecution, CloudWatch metrics, and CloudTrail logs. With this launch, usage data is also available as iceberg tables that you can search, filter, aggregate, and join directly without building or maintaining custom pipelines. Platform teams can query the query_executions table to build cost allocation dashboards that show data scanned by workgroup, by user, or by table using simple SQL statements, and without custom ETL. Additionally, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/system-table-skills"&gt;AWS Agent Toolkit&lt;/a&gt; provides skills for querying system tables to surface usage patterns and optimization recommendations. Because the data is stored in the open Apache Iceberg format, you can also query it with &lt;a href="https://duckdb.org/"&gt;DuckDB&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/pm/redshift/?trk=de3518c5-eb66-4ef5-ab94-ec69c34b86df&amp;amp;sc_channel=ps&amp;amp;ef_id=CjwKCAjws_DTBhB_EiwAXZknGRt-CfJCeVl3UL127isQ9p0q7arv1kekRNZF_pd22P4m8ReyZkhvahoCNwYQAvD_BwE&amp;amp;gads_camp=23522747487&amp;amp;gads_ag=196433734207&amp;amp;gads_ad=795876995213&amp;amp;gads_kw=amazon%20redshift&amp;amp;gads_matchtype=e&amp;amp;gads_network=g&amp;amp;gads_device=c&amp;amp;gads_geo=1027744&amp;amp;gad_campaignid=23522747487&amp;amp;gbraid=0AAAAADjHtp9cwZBKiv30JXu5qrPDkKSyb&amp;amp;gclid=CjwKCAjws_DTBhB_EiwAXZknGRt-CfJCeVl3UL127isQ9p0q7arv1kekRNZF_pd22P4m8ReyZkhvahoCNwYQAvD_BwE"&gt;Amazon Redshift,&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/emr/"&gt;Amazon EMR&lt;/a&gt; or any other Iceberg-compatible engine.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Athena system tables are available in all AWS Regions where Athena and Amazon S3 Tables are available. To get started, enable system tables from the Athena console or API. To learn more, visit the Amazon Athena &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/athena-system-tables.html"&gt;documentation &lt;/a&gt;or the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-athena-introduces-system-tables-for-query-execution-observability/" style="background-color: rgb(255,255,255);"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-172-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/05/2026 20:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An issue in the APT package manager (CVE-2026-109518) affects Ubuntu and Debian operating systems. Under specific conditions, when APT is configured to use HTTP package sources with retry settings enabled, a third party with a network position between the host and the package mirror could cause APT to install a package that did not pass verification. Updated APT packages are available from Canonical (USN-8996-1) and Debian (DSA-6607-1). The following fixed versions address this issue:&lt;br&gt; - Ubuntu 22.04 LTS: apt 2.4.15ubuntu0.1&lt;br&gt; - Ubuntu 24.04 LTS: apt 2.8.4ubuntu0.1&lt;br&gt; - Ubuntu 26.04 LTS: apt 3.2.1ubuntu0.1&lt;br&gt; - Debian (DSA-6607-1): See Debian Security Advisory for version details&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers running Ubuntu or Debian-based Amazon EC2 instances or container images should apply the updates. For details, see the full AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-172-aws/"&gt;Read this article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch&lt;/a&gt; now supports bearer token authentication, using API keys (bearer tokens), as an additional option for sending OpenTelemetry traces to the AWS X-Ray OpenTelemetry Protocol (OTLP) traces endpoint. Any OTLP/HTTP client that can set an Authorization header, including the OpenTelemetry Collector and OpenTelemetry SDK exporters, can now authenticate with a bearer token instead of signing requests with AWS Signature Version 4 (SigV4). This helps teams send traces from workloads that can't use the AWS SDK or short-term credentials, such as applications running on premises or in other clouds, CI/CD pipelines, and third-party tools that support only a static Authorization header.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SigV4 remains the recommended authentication method for sending traces to CloudWatch, and the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) SDKs continue to use SigV4 by default with no changes required. Traces sent with either method get the same CloudWatch capabilities, including Transaction Search, Amazon CloudWatch Application Signals, and Amazon CloudWatch Omni.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Bearer token&amp;nbsp;authentication for traces is available in all commercial AWS Regions where the AWS X-Ray OTLP traces endpoint is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/xray-opentelemetry-bearer-token.html"&gt;Setting up bearer token authentication for traces&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS X-Ray Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces light mode support in Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio for Identity Center (IDC) based domains. Customers can now configure the visual interface mode to match their preference, choosing between dark and light themes. With this launch, customers can set this visual preference in both IAM-based and IDC-based domains.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Light mode helps improve readability in bright environments and provides a familiar visual experience for customers who prefer lighter interfaces. Combined with the existing dark mode, this update gives you full control over the appearance of your development environment, improving accessibility and reducing eye strain across varying lighting conditions. In SageMaker Unified Studio settings, you can choose 'Customize appearance' under your Profile settings to select between visual modes including dark mode, light mode, and device setting, which matches your operating system's setting. The setting persists across browsers and devices.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/navigating-sagemaker-unified-studio.html#display-mode"&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio User Guide.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of AWS Continuum for code vulnerabilities. Continuum for code vulnerabilities&amp;nbsp;discovers, validates, prioritizes, and remediates code vulnerabilities at machine speed.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Continuum for code vulnerabilities builds a context graph from your AWS environment, including resource topology, identity relationships, and network reachability. It knows which resources are part of your application, and which findings carry real business risk. It ingests your existing vulnerability findings, scans your code, and then confirms which findings are exploitable by testing them in an isolated sandbox. The findings are prioritized by business impact. And finally, it provides remediation patches as pull requests directly to developer workflows with exploitability proof and blast radius assessment attached.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Continuum for code vulnerabilities picks the right AI model for each task. Continuum starts in Learn mode where it proposes the remediation fixes that you approve. When it builds sufficient trust in its precision, you switch to the Enforce mode and let it act autonomous within the guardrails you set.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Continuum works alongside your existing AWS security services, including Amazon GuardDuty and AWS Security Hub. For more information about the AWS Regions where AWS Continuum is available, see the &lt;a title="AWS Regional Table" href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region table.&lt;/a&gt; To learn more, see the AWS Continuum &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/continuum/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-096-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/03/2026 11:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The AWS FPGA Developer Kit is a hardware-software development kit that enables developers to create accelerators for the high-performance accelerator cards on EC2 F2 instances. We identified CVE-2026-85028, where a creation of a temporary file in a directory with insecure permissions in the FPGA management tool installation component in AWS FPGA Development Kit (aws-fpga) before 2.3.4 might allow local users to execute arbitrary code with root privileges via crafted shell content placed at a predictable path in a world-writable temporary directory, which the installation step reads after elevating its own privileges.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 2.3.4&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-092-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/31/2026 11:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;OpenSearch is an open-source search and analytics engine. We identified CVE-2026-83497 where a remote authenticated user with basic read/search permissions can run arbitrary code on the server by providing a crafted cursor parameter to the plugins/sql endpoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Affected Products &amp;amp; Versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;OpenSearch SQL Plugin (open-source, self-managed):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;br&gt; - Affected: v2.8 to v3.6&lt;br&gt; - Fixed: versions 3.7 and 2.19.6&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service (AWS Managed):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;br&gt; - Affected: v2.9 to v3.5&lt;br&gt; - Fixed: v2.9 to v3.5 (via service software update)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-088-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/21/2026 13:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service is a managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters. We identified CVE-2026-77811, a stored cross-site scripting issue in the dashboards-observability plugin in OpenSearch Dashboards. Improper input validation in the integrations static file endpoint allows a remote authenticated actor with write permissions to OpenSearch Dashboards saved objects to upload a custom integration containing arbitrary JavaScript. When another user accesses the static file endpoint directly, the script executes in their browser session and can perform actions on their behalf, including making API calls to OpenSearch with their privileges.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Affected products &amp;amp; versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;OpenSearch Dashboards dashboards-observability plugin (open-source, self-managed):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;br&gt; - Affected: versions before 3.4 and versions before 2.19.6&lt;br&gt; - Fixed: versions 3.4 and 2.19.6&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service (AWS Managed):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;br&gt; - Affected: versions before 3.3&lt;br&gt; - Fixed: fixed in all affected versions (via service software update)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-086-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/21/2026 10:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;FreeRTOS-Kernel is a real-time operating system kernel for microcontrollers and small microprocessors. AWS identified four issues with FreeRTOS-Kernel, affecting multiple versions.&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77234: This issue affects configurations that use the FreeRTOS MPU together with software timers; applications that do not use the FreeRTOS MPU are not affected.&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77235: This issue affects ARM TrustZone (ARMv8-M) configurations; applications that do not use ARM TrustZone secure contexts are not affected.&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77236: This issue affects ARM TrustZone (ARMv8-M) configurations; applications that do not use ARM TrustZone secure contexts are not affected.&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77237: This issue affects builds with queue sets enabled; applications built without queue sets are not affected.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77234: &amp;gt;=7.0.0 AND &amp;lt;=11.3.0 (MPU-enabled ports)&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77235: &amp;gt;=10.2.0 AND &amp;lt;=11.3.0 (ARMv8-M ports with TrustZone + MPU)&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77236: &amp;gt;=10.2.0 AND &amp;lt;=11.3.0 (ARMv8-M ports with TrustZone)&lt;br&gt; - CVE-2026-77237: &amp;gt;=7.4.0 AND &amp;lt;=11.3.0 (MPU-enabled ports with configUSE_QUEUE_SETS=1)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-081-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/13/2026 10:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;OpenSearch SQL plugin is a plugin that enables SQL and PPL query capabilities on OpenSearch clusters, including direct query integration with external data sources via Apache Spark. An issue exists where the Flint extension query handler validates SQL queries without sufficient restrictions, allowing a user with async query access to bypass the SQL grammar deny list via the direct query endpoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Affected Products &amp;amp; Versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;OpenSearch SQL Plugin (open-source, self-managed):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Affected:&lt;/b&gt; v2.13 to v3.6&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Fixed:&lt;/b&gt; versions 3.7 and 2.19.6&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service (AWS Managed):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Affected:&lt;/b&gt; v2.13 to v3.5&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Fixed:&lt;/b&gt; v2.13 to v3.5 (via service software update)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announced the expansion of 10 Gbps and 100 Gbps dedicated connections with MACsec encryption capabilities at the existing &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;AWS Direct Connect&lt;/a&gt; location in the Equinix MA3 data center near Manchester, England. You can now establish private, direct network access to all public AWS Regions (except those in China), AWS GovCloud Regions, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/localzones/"&gt;AWS Local Zones&lt;/a&gt; from this location. This is the third AWS Direct Connect location in England to provide 100 Gbps connections with MACsec encryption capabilities.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Direct Connect service enables you to establish a private, physical network connection between AWS and your data center, office, or colocation environment. These private connections can provide a more consistent network experience than those made over the public internet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information on the over 150 Direct Connect locations worldwide, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/locations/"&gt;locations&lt;/a&gt; section of the Direct Connect &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;product detail pages&lt;/a&gt;. Or, visit our &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/getting-started/"&gt;getting started&lt;/a&gt; page to learn more about how to purchase and deploy Direct Connect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/license-manager/"&gt;AWS License Manager&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; now supports in-place upgrades for EC2 Ubuntu LTS (Long Term Support) instances to Ubuntu Pro available on AWS Marketplace. This allows customers to strengthen their security posture and meet compliance requirements without rebuilding their fleet or migrating workloads to a new instance or AMI. With an Ubuntu Pro subscription, customers receive security patches for up to 10 years — extendable to 15 years with the Legacy add-on. This coverage extends beyond the base OS to thousands of additional packages, including widely used open-source applications such as NGINX, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, and Redis. Ubuntu Pro also supports Kernel Livepatch, which applies critical kernel security fixes without rebooting to minimize downtime.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, customers, who have an existing Canonical Ubuntu Pro subscription through AWS Marketplace, can upgrade directly during the conversion workflow. Customers who do not yet have a subscription can subscribe to Canonical Ubuntu Pro from within the AWS License Manager console. The new feature is available in &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;all commercial regions&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; where AWS License Manager is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, navigate to the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/license-manager/home?region=us-east-1#/"&gt;AWS License Manager console&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/license-manager/latest/userguide/license-asset-groups-overview.html"&gt;AWS License Manager User Guide&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, you can deliver web applications built on AWS Lambda Web Functions through CloudFront's global edge network. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With CloudFront in front of a Lambda Web Function, you get TLS termination at the edge for lower latency and faster connections, caching for better performance, and custom domains with managed TLS certificates. No changes to application code are needed. CloudFront Origin Access Control (OAC) restricts your Lambda Web Function so it's reachable only through CloudFront, so unintended requests that bypass your security protections do not reach your origin. With OAC in place, CloudFront's edge network protects your Lambda Web Function from network-layer DDoS attacks, and you can attach AWS WAF for additional application-layer security. For applications that authenticate their own users, OAC ensures that original credentials reach the application unchanged, so existing sign-in flows keep working.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudFront support for AWS Lambda Web Functions is available worldwide, except in the CloudFront China region. To get started, use the CloudFront Console, SDK, CLI, or CloudFormation to configure your Lambda Web Function as a CloudFront origin. For more information, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-lambda-web.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudFront Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;. There are no additional fees associated with this feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio now supports custom Tooling blueprints, giving domain administrators the ability to define the foundation of every project by using their own AWS CloudFormation templates. Administrators can tailor project environments to meet organization-specific requirements, such as IAM role names that comply with company naming standards or custom permissions boundaries instead of AWS managed policies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio administrators start by authoring a CloudFormation template that describes the project environment they need, then register it as a custom Tooling blueprint. The service validates the template at registration and again after each project deployment, confirming that the required resources exist before any team member uses the project. Templates can include any CloudFormation-supported resource, such as AWS Lake Formation grants, Amazon Athena workgroups, or VPC security groups, and work across multiple AWS accounts and Regions without per-project edits. At deployment time, the service automatically populates reserved template parameters, such as the project ID and domain ID, so administrators define the template once and apply it everywhere.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/supported-regions.html"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/tooling-custom-blueprints.html"&gt;Custom blueprints as Tooling documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Client VPN now supports device posture assessment, allowing you to verify that connecting user devices meet your security and compliance requirements before granting network access. This feature integrates with your existing device posture providers, so only trusted, compliant devices can access your AWS resources through Client VPN.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, Client VPN authenticated users through certificates, SAML, or Active Directory. With device posture assessment, you can now also integrate &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.crowdstrike.com/"&gt;CrowdStrike&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;, &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.jamf.com/"&gt;Jamf&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;, or &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://jumpcloud.com/"&gt;JumpCloud&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; with Client VPN to automatically evaluate device security signals such as compliance scores, encryption status, and risk level before allowing a connection. You define these requirements using &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://cedarpolicy.com/"&gt;Cedar policies&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;, giving you fine-grained control over which devices can connect. To help you author and validate these policies, Client VPN provides you a Test Policy tool that guides you through creating Cedar policies for your device posture requirements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature continuously re-evaluates device compliance during active sessions, and automatically disconnects a session if a device falls out of compliance, such as when risk score or security settings change. You can also use this feature in monitoring-only mode, which logs posture evaluation results without disconnecting sessions, so you can assess the impact of your policies before enforcing them. Device posture assessment works alongside your existing authorization rules to provide defense in depth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Client VPN is available, at no additional cost. This feature requires AWS VPN Client version 6.2.0 or later.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Client VPN:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
 &lt;li&gt;Visit the AWS Client VPN &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/vpn/client-vpn/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
 &lt;li&gt;Download the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/vpn/client-vpn-download/"&gt;AWS VPN Client&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
 &lt;li&gt;Read the AWS Client VPN &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/clientvpn-admin/what-is.html"&gt;administrator guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
 &lt;li&gt;Read the AWS Client VPN &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/clientvpn-user/client-vpn-user-what-is.html"&gt;user guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-125-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 10/02/2026 13:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is an AWS service that unifies data, analytics, and AI development. It lets you find and access your organization's data and act on it with integrated, purpose-built tools.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-104019, an issue with the startup of SageMaker Spaces in SageMaker Unified Studio. The startup script in a SageMaker Space performs a network validation against all the available SageMaker connections in a project. Under certain conditions, improper sanitization of connection details during this validation could allow arbitrary code to be executed in the Space of another project member. In projects with the Trusted Identity Propagation feature enabled, this issue could result in a user with project contributor permissions (or higher) gaining access to another member's temporary execution role credentials and calling downstream trusted identity propagation-enabled AWS services on their behalf.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We implemented a fix to all supported SageMaker Distribution versions to sanitize connection details during the startup validation process. The fix is deployed globally and will apply to all Spaces automatically on the next startup.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;- 2.8.x - 2.13.x: all versions affected, no fix (end of support)&lt;br&gt; - 2.14.x: &amp;lt; 2.14.12, fixed in 2.14.12&lt;br&gt; - 3.3.x - 3.8.x: all versions affected, no fix (end of support)&lt;br&gt; - 3.9.x: &amp;lt; 3.9.12, fixed in 3.9.12&lt;br&gt; - 4.0.x: &amp;lt; 4.0.11, fixed in 4.0.11&lt;br&gt; - 4.1.x: &amp;lt; 4.1.11, fixed in 4.1.11&lt;br&gt; - 4.2.x: &amp;lt; 4.2.8, fixed in 4.2.8&lt;br&gt; - 4.3.x: &amp;lt; 4.3.5, fixed in 4.3.5&lt;br&gt; - 4.4.x: &amp;lt; 4.4.3, fixed in 4.4.3&lt;br&gt; - 4.5.x: not affected&lt;br&gt; - &amp;lt; 2.8.0 and &amp;lt; 3.3.0: not affected&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS CloudTrail introduces Event Coverage, a new console experience that gives customers visibility into their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization level. You can now see which AWS services and resource types in your environment have data event logging enabled and which do not. This helps you quickly identify gaps in your logging posture without manually scanning accounts in your organization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Data events enable you to track data plane operations in AWS services. For example, you can log Amazon S3 object-level operations like GetObject and PutObject to detect unauthorized data access or exfiltration attempts. Without comprehensive data events coverage, these activities can go unnoticed, leaving blind spots in your security monitoring. With Event Coverage, you can view coverage across all supported data event sources in one place and subscribe to data events directly from the dashboard. This makes it easier to close coverage gaps in a few clicks, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts where tracking coverage across services can be time-consuming.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can access Event Coverage from the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. To learn more about logging Data Events, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html#logging-data-events"&gt;AWS CloudTrail documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Redshift now makes it easier to troubleshoot and audit queries on data protected by federated permissions with fine-grained access control (FGAC). Using the new DEBUG permission, data owners can let specific identities see unredacted secure logging records across multiple Redshift data warehouses.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With Amazon Redshift federated permissions, you define data permissions once, and Redshift enforces them automatically across every Redshift warehouse in your AWS account. When a query accesses FGAC-protected data, secure logging redacts sensitive values in system table records, such as rewritten query text, error messages, and object names. This protects the producer's data from consumers. At the same time, authorized auditors and administrators need visibility into these records to troubleshoot queries and meet compliance requirements, without turning off secure logging.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With the new DEBUG permission, a superuser or database owner can choose which identities see these records without redaction. You grant DEBUG with the standard Redshift GRANT command, typically to an IAM user, IAM role, or IAM Identity Center user or group, so that identity can see unredacted records for its own queries. You can also grant DEBUG to the administrators of a consumer account for full log visibility during troubleshooting and audit. Records authorized for account administrators also stay unredacted when you export Redshift system table data to Amazon S3 Tables. You get precise, auditable control over who can see logs, and secure logging stays on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Redshift is supported. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated-permissions.html"&gt;federated permissions&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT-usage-notes.html"&gt;Usage Notes&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing-secure-logging.html"&gt;secure logging&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Redshift documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup logically air-gapped vault now supports Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP. Logically air-gapped vaults are a type of AWS Backup vault that allows secure sharing of backups across accounts and AWS Organizations, supporting direct restore to reduce recovery time from a data loss event.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can now protect your Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP volumes in logically air-gapped vaults. A logically air-gapped vault stores immutable backups that are locked by default, and isolated with encryption using AWS owned keys or customer-managed keys. You can store your FSx for NetApp ONTAP backups in a logically air-gapped vault in the same account or across other accounts and Regions. This helps reduce the risk of downtime, ensure business continuity, and meet compliance and disaster recovery requirements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can get started using the AWS Backup console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. Target FSx for NetApp ONTAP backups to a logically air-gapped vault by specifying it as the primary target or copy destination in your backup plan. Share the vault for recovery or restore testing with other accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), or safeguard vault access during account compromise using &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/multipartyapproval.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Multi-party approval&lt;/a&gt;. Once available, you can initiate direct restore jobs from that account, eliminating the overhead of copying backups first.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This support is available in all AWS Regions where both logically air-gapped vault and Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP are available. For more information and detailed regional availability, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/backup-feature-availability.html#features-by-region" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Backup documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about logically air-gapped vaults, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/logicallyairgappedvault.html"&gt;feature documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-117-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/24/2026 10:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kiro is an agentic IDE that users install on their desktop. We identified CVE-2026-95985. The file write tool in Kiro IDE before version 1.0.242 might allow remote unauthenticated actors to execute arbitrary commands and to inject crafted instructions into the agent's context. When a user runs the agent in a crafted repository as an untrusted workspace, sending any message can cause agent modifications to auto-loaded global configuration paths.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 1.0.242&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-117-aws/"&gt;View article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon ElastiCache now supports resource tagging and tag-based access control (TBAC) for Global Datastore. Previously, ElastiCache supported tagging on all resources except Global Datastore, which prevented customers from applying a single, consistent permission and cost-allocation model across their ElastiCache fleet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can use AddTagsToResource, RemoveTagsFromResource, and ListTagsForResource on a Global Datastore, and reference those tags as conditions in IAM policies and Service Control Policies (SCPs) to grant permissions based on tag attributes rather than enumerating individual resources. Tag changes on a Global Datastore propagate automatically to every Region it spans, so access-control and cost-allocation policies stay consistent without per-Region operations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is no additional cost for this feature, and no change to how you create or manage Global Datastores. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where ElastiCache Global Datastore is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/Tagging-Resources.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tagging your ElastiCache resources&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/IAM.ConditionKeys.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Using condition keys with ElastiCache&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-116-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/22/2026 13:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;s2n-quic is a Rust implementation of the QUIC protocol. We identified CVE-2026-94450, an issue with improper validation of the Destination Connection ID length when a server is configured to send Retry packets. s2n-quic 1.88.0 and earlier allow an unauthenticated user to shut down a server endpoint via a single crafted UDP datagram. No AWS services are affected by this issue, and customers of AWS services do not need to take action. Only server endpoints specifically configured to issue Retry packets are affected.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;= v1.88.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS root user sign-in is now served across US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), and US West (Oregon), with sign-in traffic distributed across all three Regions. This change reduces reliance on US East (N. Virginia) and improves resiliency during service disruptions. AWS automatically routes your root user sign-in to a supported Region without requiring you to select a Region or change how you sign in. This improvement is available now for all AWS accounts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In AWS CloudTrail, ConsoleLogin events for root user sign-ins are recorded in the Region that processed the sign-in request. To maintain full visibility into root user sign-in activity, update your monitoring and alerting to cover US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), and US West (Oregon).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/introduction-to-root-user-sign-in-tutorial.html"&gt;AWS Sign-In documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-event-reference-aws-console-sign-in-events.html#cloudtrail-event-reference-aws-console-sign-in-events-root"&gt;CloudTrail ConsoleLogin event reference&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS announces the CheckIngestedDocumentAcl and GetIngestedDocumentAcl APIs for Amazon Bedrock Managed Knowledge Base, giving customers a self-service way to debug document access issues and audit document-level permissions. When a user doesn't see an expected document in retrieval results for ACL-enabled data sources, it can be difficult to determine whether the cause is an access control misconfiguration or something else entirely. These new APIs and the accompanying console experience close that gap, enabling you to quickly diagnose and resolve permission issues without opening a support case.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CheckIngestedDocumentAcl lets you verify whether a specific user has access to a given ingested document, while GetIngestedDocumentAcl returns the full ACL attached to a document so you can audit exactly what permissions are configured and catch misconfigurations. The console also introduces a new Document Access Control section on the data source details page, where you can check access by entering a document ID and user email or retrieve a document's full ACL by document ID. Together, these capabilities give administrators the visibility they need to manage access control at scale across enterprise knowledge bases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/APIReference/API_agent-runtime_CheckIngestedDocumentAcl.html" target="_blank"&gt;CheckIngestedDocumentAcl&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/APIReference/API_agent-runtime_GetIngestedDocumentAcl.html" target="_blank"&gt;GetIngestedDocumentAcl&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock API Reference. For more information about Amazon Bedrock Managed Knowledge Base, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/knowledge-bases/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, customers can subscribe and manage flat-rate pricing plans programmatically using the AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, CloudFormation, CDK, or the PricingPlanManager API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudFront flat-rate plans give you one monthly price covering global content delivery, WAF, DDoS, DNS, logging, and edge compute, with no usage-based overage charges regardless of traffic spikes or attacks. Previously, customers could only subscribe to flat-rate pricing plans using the console, which required manual steps when using the API or infrastructure as code (IaC) like CloudFormation to create and manage distributions. Now, customers can programmatically subscribe, upgrade, downgrade, and cancel flat-rate pricing plans using the API or IaC tools.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Paid plans support an optional two-phase activation flow: you first create the plan, then approve it to begin billing. This prevents you from being committed to charges before you confirm, and makes the API well-suited for automated workflows and agents that provision infrastructure on your behalf. Free plans activate immediately and don’t require approval. To learn more, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/PricingPlanManager/latest/UserGuide/getting-started-pricingplanmanager-api.html"&gt;Getting started with the PricingPlanManager API&lt;/a&gt;. There are no additional fees for using the API to manage flat-rate pricing plans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS DevOps Agent is now certified at the Federal Risk and Authorization Management (FedRAMP) Class D level in AWS GovCloud (US). AWS DevOps Agent is your always-available operations teammate that resolves and proactively prevents incidents, optimizes application reliability and performance, and handles on-demand SRE tasks across AWS, multicloud, and on-prem environments.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cloud Service Providers achieve and maintain FedRAMP Certification by meeting NIST 800-53 Rev 5 controls, undergoing annual independent assessment by a FedRAMP Recognized Independent Assessment Service, performing continuous monitoring and vulnerability management, publishing quarterly Ongoing Certification Reports, and issuing Significant Change Notifications to agencies for material changes to any AWS cloud service offerings the agency uses.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about AWS DevOps Agent, read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/announcing-general-availability-of-aws-devops-agent/"&gt;launch blog&lt;/a&gt; and see&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/devopsagent/latest/userguide/"&gt; getting started&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For information on FedRAMP Certification updates, including Significant Change Notifications, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/fedramp/"&gt;FedRAMP Compliance FAQ&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/stacksets-concepts.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS CloudFormation StackSets&lt;/a&gt; now supports custom execution roles for service-managed stack sets, which enables you to scope the permissions StackSets uses in each target AWS account. StackSets deploys the same infrastructure, such as networking and security settings, across thousands of accounts and Regions in one operation. With a custom execution role, you can also use nested stacks, macros, and transforms in service-managed stack sets, which allows you to deploy the reusable templates you use for individual stacks across your organization.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With a custom execution role, you define what StackSets can do in each account. By default, service-managed stack sets use a role in each account that can provision any resource in a template without additional setup, and that default remains available. Now, you can provide a template that defines an IAM role, called the execution role, and StackSets creates it in each account before deploying. For example, a stack set that creates Amazon S3 buckets can use an execution role limited to CloudFormation and Amazon S3 permissions. When you update the execution role template, StackSets updates the role in every account it deployed to.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, choose Set execution role when you create a stack set in the CloudFormation console, or use the AWS CLI or AWS SDKs. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/stacksets-custom-execution-role.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/announcing-custom-execution-roles-for-aws-cloudformation-stacksets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS DevOps Blog&lt;/a&gt;. This feature is available at no additional charge in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. Refer to the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt; for details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon VPC Lattice now publishes Amazon CloudWatch metrics for VPC resources. Resource owners — those who share resources via VPC Lattice — and resource consumers — those who access those shared resources — can now see TCP traffic to resources such as databases, clusters, and on-premises endpoints. Resource owners get metrics for resource gateways and resource configurations. Consumers get metrics for resource endpoints, service network endpoints, and service network VPC associations. Previously, resource access logs were the primary way to see this traffic, but they required additional processing and provided limited real-time visibility. Now you can monitor your layer-4 resources directly in CloudWatch.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With these metrics, you can proactively catch connectivity issues and optimize resource capacity. Capacity planning metrics — including bytes processed, new flows, and active flows — let resource owners monitor traffic per resource gateway and per resource configuration to track load on shared resources. Reliability metrics, such as dropped packets, help you detect problems early. Resource gateways also report port allocation errors, which show when a resource gateway needs more IPv4 addresses per network interface. Consumers can monitor traffic per resource endpoint, service network endpoint, or service network VPC association. They can set CloudWatch alarms for when new flows drop to zero or dropped packets increase, catching an unreachable resource before their applications do. Both owners and consumers can break metrics down by Availability Zone to isolate zonal issues or verify connections after configuration changes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudWatch metrics for VPC resources are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon VPC Lattice is available. For the list of Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc-lattice/latest/ug/what-is-vpc-lattice.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon VPC Lattice FAQs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc-lattice/latest/ug/monitoring-cloudwatch.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudWatch metrics for Amazon VPC Lattice&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon VPC Lattice User Guide, or visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc-lattice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon VPC Lattice product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-188-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/18/2026 10:00 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Relative path traversal in the object detection dataset preparation tool in AutoGluon Multimodal before 1.6.0 might allow adversary-in-the-middle attackers to write arbitrary files with the privileges of the user running the tool via a crafted dataset archive substituted for a dataset downloaded over plaintext HTTP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 1.6.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-188-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Nov 17, 2026, Amazon announced critical security patch update (CSPU) for Amazon Corretto Long-Term Support (LTS) and Feature Release (FR) versions of OpenJDK. Corretto 27.0.1.1.4, 25.0.5.1.2, 21.0.13.1.2, 17.0.21.1.2, 11.0.33.1.1, and 8u514 are now available for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto/"&gt;download&lt;/a&gt;. Amazon Corretto is a no-cost, multi-platform, production-ready distribution of OpenJDK.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Visit Corretto &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt; to download Corretto 27, Corretto 25, Corretto 21, Corretto 17, Corretto 11, or Corretto 8. You can also get the updates on your Linux system by configuring a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/corretto/latest/corretto-27-ug/generic-linux-install.html"&gt;Corretto Apt, Yum, or Apk repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Feedback is &lt;a href="https://github.com/corretto"&gt;welcomed&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) now supports DuckDB operators. DuckDB is an embedded analytical database that lets customers perform data quality checks, summaries, and transformations on data from sources DuckDB supports, including Amazon S3.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Native support is available for DuckDB on Amazon MWAA Provisioned environments running Apache Airflow 3.3.1. DuckDB comes preinstalled and preconfigured for AWS connections in the latest MWAA Airflow 3.3.1 update. After updating their MWAA environment to Airflow 3.3.1 and adding execution role access to their data stores, customers can start writing tasks with DuckDBExecuteQueryOperator with no additional setup.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon MWAA is available. For more information, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/get-started.html#regions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/samples-duckdb.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Using DuckDB with Amazon MWAA&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon MWAA User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-087-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/21/2026 12:30 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Athena is a serverless, interactive query service that lets you analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena Query Federation is a feature that allows you to connect to data sources outside of Amazon S3 like DynamoDB, Azure Synapse, and custom connectors using standard SQL syntax. These connectors are open source and deployed to the Athena service on a regular basis.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-77810, in the Neptune connector where a user with access to Neptune through Athena Federated Query could gain access to properties in the Lambda supplying the compute for the connector.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;=v2026.28.1 AND &amp;gt;=v2024.15.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-082-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/18/2026 10:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;OpenSearch Dashboards is the open-source visualization and management UI for OpenSearch, and ships as part of Amazon OpenSearch Service. We identified CVE-2026-75897, an improper input validation in the capabilities route handler in OpenSearch Dashboards. The handler does not bound the size of the request payload, which might allow remote attackers to cause a denial of service via a crafted HTTP request.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Affected Products and Versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;OpenSearch "Plugin Type" Plugin (open-source, self-managed):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Affected:&lt;/b&gt; All versions from 1.3.0 through 3.7.0 inclusive, including all 2.x releases up to and including 2.19.6. The issue is inherited from upstream Kibana and is also present in Kibana 7.7.1 through 7.10.2.&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Fixed:&lt;/b&gt; 3.8.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service (AWS Managed):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Affected:&lt;/b&gt; Engine versions OpenSearch 1.3, 2.11, 2.13, 2.15, 2.17, 2.19, 3.1, 3.3, and 3.5, and Elasticsearch-compatibility versions using Kibana 7.9 and 7.10.&lt;br&gt; - &lt;b&gt;Fixed:&lt;/b&gt; A patched service software release is available for all affected versions. Apply the latest available service software update to your domain.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-084-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/20/2026 13:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Athena is a serverless, interactive query service that lets you analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena Query Federation is a feature that allows you to connect to data sources outside of Amazon S3 like DynamoDB, Azure Synapse, and custom connectors using standard SQL syntax. These connectors are open source and deployed to the Athena service on a regular basis. We identified CVE-2026-75910. Incorrect privilege assignment in the ClickHouse connector deployment template before the v2026.17.1 release could allow an authenticated remote user to read arbitrary AWS Secrets Manager secrets in the deploying account by pointing the connector's connection string at an unrelated secret and at a database endpoint under the user's control, causing the connector to transmit the secret to that endpoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; V2026.17.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now use container images from registries such as Seqera (Wave), NVIDIA NGC, and Google Artifact Registry with AWS HealthOmics expanded support for third-party container registries. You can use the containerized tools your workflows already rely on, without manually migrating images into Amazon ECR or editing your workflow definitions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt; is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs at scale with fully managed bioinformatics workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With expanded container registry support, you can now instruct HealthOmics to copy container images, on your behalf, from newly supported registries into your Amazon ECR when running your private workflows.&amp;nbsp;Previously, AWS HealthOmics could pull images only from registries supported through&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/aws-healthomics-third-party-container-registries-private-workflows/"&gt;Amazon ECR pull-through cache&lt;/a&gt; (PTC). Your private workflows can now access Amazon ECR pull-through cache registries and the newly supported third party container registries, so you do not need to pull, re-push, or rewrite image URIs yourself.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Expanded container registry support is now available in all AWS Regions where HealthOmics is available: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Singapore, Seoul, Tokyo). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/workflows-ecr.html"&gt;third-party container image support documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon MSK Replicator now supports OAuth 2.0 (SASL/OAUTHBEARER) authentication for data replication from external Apache Kafka clusters - including on-premises, self-managed on AWS, or other cloud providers - to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-provisioned.html"&gt;Amazon MSK Provisioned clusters&lt;/a&gt;. With this capability, external Apache Kafka clusters configured with OAuth/OIDC authentication can now use MSK Replicator to migrate workloads to Amazon MSK, support disaster recovery by using MSK-based clusters as a failover or backup target and enable data distribution across hybrid and multi-cloud environments.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MSK Replicator is a feature of Amazon MSK that automates data replication between Kafka clusters, eliminating the need to manage custom replication infrastructure or configure open-source tools. Previously, MSK Replicator supported SASL/SCRAM and mTLS authentication for connecting to external Apache Kafka clusters. With this launch, you can now also use OAuth 2.0 authentication with MSK Replicator to replicate data from external Kafka clusters to Amazon MSK. Unlike self-managed replication tools, MSK Replicator lets you retain your original Kafka topic names during replication while automatically avoiding infinite replication loops. It also synchronizes consumer group offsets bidirectionally, enabling you to move producers and consumers across clusters independently, in any order, without coordination constraints or the risk of data loss.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This new capability is supported in all AWS Regions where MSK Replicator is available. Visit the MSK Replicator &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/features/msk-replicator/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;, and this &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-third-party-and-self-managed-apache-kafka-clusters-to-amazon-msk-express-brokers-with-amazon-msk-replicator/"&gt;AWS blog post&lt;/a&gt; to learn more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-008-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 2026/03/16 11:15 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A missing S3 ownership verification in the Bedrock AgentCore Starter Toolkit before version v0.1.13 may allow a remote actor to inject code during the build process, leading to code execution in the AgentCore Runtime.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; All versions of Bedrock AgentCore Starter Toolkit versions before v0.1.13. This issue only affects users of the Bedrock AgentCore Starter Toolkit before version v0.1.13 who build the Toolkit after September 24, 2025. Any users on a version &amp;gt;=v0.1.13, and any users on previous versions who built the toolkit before September 24, 2025 are not affected.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB now supports AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer managed keys for encrypting data at rest in InfluxDB 2 database instances, InfluxDB 2 Read Replicas, and InfluxDB 3 clusters. Customers select a symmetric AWS KMS key when creating a database resource.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Timestream for InfluxDB uses the selected key to encrypt the underlying database storage for InfluxDB 2 and InfluxDB 3 resources. The key must be in the same AWS account and AWS Region as the database resource. Customers specify the key during resource creation. The key cannot be changed after the resource is created.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customer managed key support is available through the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI), and Timestream for InfluxDB application programming interface (API). The feature is available in all AWS Regions where Timestream for InfluxDB is available. There is no additional Timestream for InfluxDB charge for using customer managed keys. Standard AWS KMS charges apply.&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; Support for Customer managed keys is available in all AWS &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/timestream.html"&gt;Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB is available. To get started, open the Amazon Timestream console. For more information, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/timestream/"&gt;Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/timestream/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, customers can protect their origins using Amazon S3 Multi-Region Access Points (MRAP) by using CloudFront Origin Access Control (OAC) to only allow access from designated CloudFront distributions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers use Amazon S3 MRAP with CloudFront to serve content from a single global endpoint that automatically routes to the closest available replicated bucket across regions during a cache miss, improving performance and resilience for globally distributed users. Previously, customers had to compute and forward their own Asymmetric Signature Version 4 (SigV4a) Authorization header using a custom Lambda@Edge Function. Now, CloudFront natively signs requests to S3 MRAP origins. Customers get faster cache-miss fills from the nearest region and restricted, OAC-secured MRAP access without&amp;nbsp; custom Authorization header computation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudFront OAC support for Amazon S3 MRAP origins is available worldwide, except in the CloudFront China region. To get started, use the CloudFront Console, SDK, CLI, or CloudFormation to enable OAC when configuring your Amazon S3 MRAP endpoint with CloudFront. For more information, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3-mrap.html"&gt;CloudFront Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;. There are no additional fees associated with this feature&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Aug 18, 2026, Amazon announced critical security patch update (CSPU) for Amazon Corretto Long-Term Support (LTS) and Feature Release (FR) versions of OpenJDK. Corretto 26.0.2.11.1, 25.0.4.8.1, 21.0.12.9.1, 17.0.20.10.1, 11.0.32.10.1, and 8u504 are now available for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto/"&gt;download&lt;/a&gt;. Amazon Corretto is a no-cost, multi-platform, production-ready distribution of OpenJDK.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Visit Corretto &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt; to download Corretto 26, Corretto 25, Corretto 21, Corretto 17, Corretto 11, or Corretto 8. You can also get the updates on your Linux system by configuring a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/corretto/latest/corretto-26-ug/generic-linux-install.html"&gt;Corretto Apt, Yum, or Apk repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Feedback is &lt;a href="https://github.com/corretto"&gt;welcomed&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of AgentCore payments, a capability within Amazon Bedrock AgentCore that enables AI agents to autonomously discover, access, and pay for paid APIs, MCPs, and content with just a few lines of code. AgentCore payments provides the security, guardrails, and observability enterprises need to deploy transacting agents in production at scale.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AgentCore payments integrates with Coinbase and Stripe Privy wallets for microtransactions, provides payment orchestration across protocols, enforces configurable payment limits at the infrastructure layer, and delivers end-to-end observability through AgentCore Observability. At general availability, AgentCore payments includes Quick Create for Coinbase credential provisioning directly within the AgentCore console, a curated Coinbase Bazar MCP server of pay-per-use x402 endpoints via AgentCore gateway, support for the Machine Payment Protocol (MPP), and the "upto" scheme in the x402 protocol for pay-per-inference and dynamic pricing use cases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Developers can get started using coding assistant skills such as Claude Code, Kiro, and Codex, AgentCore CLI, or AWS Management Console - follow this &lt;a href="https://github.com/awslabs/agentcore-samples/tree/main/01-features/08-agents-that-transact"&gt;link&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AgentCore payments is available in the regions listed &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/agentcore-regions.html"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/payments.html"&gt;AgentCore payments documentation&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/amazon-bedrock-agentcore-payments-is-now-generally-available-enabling-agents-to-transact-safely-and-autonomously-at-scale/"&gt;AWS News Blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn about pricing, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/pricing/"&gt;AgentCore pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-198-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/24/2026 10:00 AM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;pgactive is an open source PostgreSQL extension from AWS that provides active-active replication between PostgreSQL databases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-134068, an issue in the replication origin name parsing component of pgactive. Under certain circumstances, an authenticated database user with specific privileges might be able to execute arbitrary operating system commands as the database operating system user.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 2.1.0 AND &amp;lt; 2.1.10&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-198-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-196-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/23/2026 12:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt; We identified CVE-2026-134056, an out-of-bounds write issue in AWS-LC's padded block cipher decryption. It affects applications that decrypt CBC or ECB ciphertext with PKCS7 padding over multiple update calls. If the output buffer is the minimum documented size, AWS-LC can write one byte past its end. This might crash the application or corrupt adjacent memory. This issue does not disclose data. The aws-lc-rs StreamingDecryptingKey API has a similar issue with cbc_pkcs7 and ecb_pkcs7. Its less_safe_update function can write up to one block (16 bytes for AES) past the buffer. Single-call decryption is not affected. Neither is decryption without padding, nor modes such as CTR, CFB, and GCM.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers of AWS services do not need to take action.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-196-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront now supports HTTP/2 connections to custom origins and VPC origins. HTTP/2 lets CloudFront send many requests over fewer connections to your origin. This means fewer connections and TLS handshakes for your origin to handle, which improves performance for API and agentic workloads, such as AI agents calling MCP servers or mobile and web apps calling backend APIs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Until now, CloudFront connected to origins only over HTTP/1.1, which handles one request at a time per connection. When traffic spiked, for example when an AI agent made many tool calls at once, CloudFront had to open more origin connections. Each new connection added latency and load. With HTTP/2, those requests share connections, so your origin does less work and responds faster. HTTP/2 is also supported for WebSocket connections that support extended CONNECT. If your origin doesn't support HTTP/2, CloudFront automatically falls back to HTTP/1.1, so you don't need to change anything on your origin.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HTTP/2 to origins is available from all CloudFront edge locations. There is no additional cost for using HTTP/2 to origins. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/DownloadDistValuesOrigin.html#DownloadDistValuesOriginHttpVersion"&gt;Origin HTTP version&lt;/a&gt; in the CloudFront Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda now provides cost optimization recommendations for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-functions-chapter.html"&gt;Event Functions&lt;/a&gt; in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/technology/trusted-advisor/"&gt;AWS Trusted Advisor&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;These recommendations identify on-demand functions&amp;nbsp;where moving to &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/lambda-managed-instances/"&gt;Lambda Managed Instances&lt;/a&gt; (LMI) is estimated to reduce costs, and provide configuration guidance for running on LMI. With&amp;nbsp;these recommendations, you can lower your Lambda costs by focusing your efforts on the functions with the greatest estimated savings.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;LMI&amp;nbsp;lets you run Lambda functions on Amazon EC2 instances while maintaining the operational simplicity of Lambda. With LMI, you can access specialized compute configurations and drive cost efficiency through EC2 pricing options such as Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, without managing infrastructure. Previously, to determine whether a function was a good fit for LMI, you had to analyze its invocation patterns, estimate LMI costs, and select the right instance type and memory-to-vCPU ratio yourself. This manual process added overhead and delayed savings. With this launch, Lambda automatically analyzes the invocation patterns, duration, and resource utilization of your high-volume Event Functions and recommends those where LMI is estimated to reduce costs. Estimates are based on a simulation of each function's invocation patterns, using public&amp;nbsp;Lambda pricing and Amazon EC2 On-Demand pricing.&amp;nbsp;Each recommendation includes the function ARN,&amp;nbsp;the recommended EC2 instance type and memory-to-vCPU ratio, the estimated current&amp;nbsp;cost, the estimated LMI cost, and the estimated savings. You can use the recommended configuration to run a function on LMI without sizing it yourself and realize cost savings sooner.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These recommendations are available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Europe (Ireland), and Europe (Milan). Customers with eligible AWS Support plans&amp;nbsp;can access them in the Trusted Advisor console alongside other cost optimization checks. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-managed-instances.html"&gt;AWS Lambda Developer Guide,&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;the AWS Trusted Advisor &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awssupport/latest/user/trusted-advisor.html"&gt;User Guide,&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda pricing.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Continuum for Penetration Testing (formerly AWS Security Agent) now lets you estimate task hours before running a penetration test, so developers and security teams can see how many billable task hours a test is likely to need and choose a maximum task-hours limit with confidence. Continuum for Penetration Testing runs on-demand, AI-driven penetration tests against your web applications and APIs. Previously, the only way to know how long a test would take was to run it, making it difficult to plan budgets and set appropriate usage limits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With task-hour estimates, you open a penetration test, choose the Pentest cost estimate tab, and request an estimate. AWS Continuum runs a short preliminary analysis of your application's scope. It then draws on expertise from past penetration tests to estimate the task hours needed and shows the task hours that most similar applications finish within, and the higher range a few applications require. Requesting an estimate is free, and the estimate is a guide, not a quote or a limit. Actual task hours can be higher or lower, and only the maximum task-hours limit caps usage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Task-hour estimates are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Continuum for Penetration Testing is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityagent/latest/userguide/perform-penetration-test.html#task-hour-estimate" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/continuum/"&gt;AWS Continuum product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-189-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/18/2026 17:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS is aware of CVE-2026-114938, a memory issue in the Base64 decoder intrinsic of OpenJDK 17.0.6 and later on x86-64 processors that support AVX-512 VBMI. A fix is available in the November 2026 OpenJDK updates. AWS is working to deploy the fix to affected AWS services&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-189-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform custom now enables you to share transformation definitions (TDs) and their associated lessons across AWS accounts, organizational units (OUs), or your entire organization in AWS Organizations through AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM). AWS Transform custom is a capability of AWS Transform that performs transformations across your technology stack, including API and framework upgrades, migrations, and language translations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can share TDs as live resources rather than static copies, so consumers run the owner's latest published version by default while the owner maintains full control. As the TD owner, you select from four managed permissions ranging from execute-only (the default) to permissions that allow consumers to use your lessons, contribute lessons back to you, or download the TD. Lessons flow back to the owner only when the permission allows it and the consumer opts in.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is particularly valuable for platform engineering teams and AWS Partners who need to deploy standardized transformations across multiple accounts. A central team can create a TD once and share it organization-wide.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available via the AWS Transform CLI and AWS RAM in US East (N. Virginia), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (London), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Canada (Central) Regions. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/custom-sharing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Transform User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon WorkSpaces Applications now supports smart card redirection on single-session and multi-session fleets, so users can access the smart card connected to their local device from applications inside their streaming session. It also extends smart card sign-in, previously limited to single-session fleets, to multi-session fleets. This launch helps public sector, healthcare, and other regulated organizations that use Common Access Card (CAC) or Personal Identity Verification (PIV) cards run those workflows on multi-session fleets.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Administrators can turn smart card sign-in for Active Directory and smart card redirection on or off independently for each stack. Smart card sign-in lets users sign in to domain-joined streaming instances with their smart card instead of a password, while redirected smart cards support in-session authentication, digital signing, and email encryption. For example, a government agency can move CAC users from single-session to multi-session fleets and keep CAC sign-in.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available today in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) regions that offer WorkSpaces Applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, turn on either setting under User settings for your stack, use a Windows image with the latest WorkSpaces Applications agent, and connect with the WorkSpaces Applications client for Windows. New stacks have both settings turned off by default, and existing stacks that use smart card redirection keep it turned on. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/set-up-stacks-fleets-install.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Create a stack&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon WorkSpaces Applications Administration Guide, or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/workspaces/applications/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon WorkSpaces Applications&lt;/a&gt; product page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-184-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/16/2026 12:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Improper limitation of a pathname to a restricted directory in the cost report generation tool in AWS Pricing MCP Server (aws-pricing-mcp-server) before 1.0.31 might allow an actor who can influence the MCP agent to write actor controlled content to arbitrary locations outside the intended output directory via absolute paths or directory traversal sequences in the report output path.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - awslabs.aws-pricing-mcp-server &amp;lt; 1.0.31&lt;br&gt; - awslabs.cost-analysis-mcp-server &amp;lt;= 1.0.5&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-184-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio now supports layout customization, a new capability that gives domain administrators control over which tools and left navigation items project members see based on their designation. Organizations with data engineering, analytics, and machine learning teams on a single platform can configure a layout within each designation definition, and that layout applies across every project in the domain without per-project configuration.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, all project members saw the same interface regardless of their role, creating confusion and potential governance issues. With layout customization, administrators define a layout as part of a designation that specifies which left navigation items are visible for project members assigned to that designation. When a project member opens a project, SageMaker Unified Studio resolves the member's designation and renders only the tools included in their designation's layout. For example, a SQL analyst designation can be configured to show only the Query Editor, while an ML engineer designation shows notebooks, training jobs, and model endpoints. Navigation items are configurable across categories including Data Analytics, artificial intelligence and machine learning (AI/ML), Generative AI, and IDEs. If a project member navigates directly to a hidden page, they see an access-denied page. For members with multiple designations, visible tools are the union of all their designations' layouts. New navigation items added to SageMaker Unified Studio remain hidden for designations with a configured layout until an administrator adds them.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/supported-regions.html"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/designations.html"&gt;Designations&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-180-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/11/2026 13:00 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MServer-side request forgery (SSRF) in the server registration and update API in Amazon mcp-gateway-registry before 1.35.0 might allow an authenticated remote user to send requests from the gateway to internal network services and cloud instance metadata endpoints and read the responses via a crafted request&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= v1.0.3 AND &amp;lt;= 1.34.0&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-180-aws/"&gt;Refer to the article&lt;/a&gt; for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-175-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 11/09/2026 14:15 PM PST&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Braket SDK is an open source Python library that customers use to run quantum tasks and hybrid jobs on Amazon Braket.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-124034, an issue in the local hybrid job input data download in the Amazon Braket SDK. And we identified CVE-2026-124035, an issue in the error mitigation schema validation in Amazon Braket Schemas, the library the Amazon Braket SDK uses to parse quantum task results.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; - amazon-braket-sdk &amp;gt;= 1.10.0 AND &amp;lt; 1.127.7&lt;br&gt; - amazon-braket-schemas &amp;gt;= 1.17.0 AND &amp;lt; 1.32.2&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Refer to the article for the most up-to-date and complete information.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-175-aws/"&gt;Read this article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/dms/"&gt;AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) Schema Conversion&lt;/a&gt; now supports offline source conversion for Oracle Database. You can convert Oracle schemas and code without direct connectivity to your source databases. You can extract metadata using standard database commands in your own environment, then upload it to DMS Schema Conversion for processing. This removes the need for security reviews, firewall changes, and VPN setup that delay migration projects, and delivers the same conversion results as the connected approach.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Offline Source suits organizations whose security policies restrict external tool access to production Oracle databases. Database administrators can generate human-readable metadata files within their existing environment. Security teams can review the commands and output before upload, simplifying approval. By removing the connectivity requirement, Offline Source turns weeks of security reviews into a simple command-and-upload workflow.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Offline Source supports all DMS Schema Conversion targets at no additional conversion charge. For regional availability, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_SchemaConversion.html#schema-conversion-supported-regions"&gt;Supported AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; page. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/virtual-data-provider.html"&gt;Using Offline Source&lt;/a&gt; in the DMS Schema Conversion documentation.&amp;nbsp;To learn how to run offline assessment and conversion with AI agents, read the launch &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/offline-assessment-and-conversion-with-ai-agents-in-aws-dms/"&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle (&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle&lt;/a&gt;) now allows customers to join their RDS for Oracle DB instances directly to their self-managed Microsoft Active Directory (AD) domain. Your self-managed AD can run on premises, on Amazon EC2, or with another cloud provider. Users in your AD domain can authenticate to RDS for Oracle DB instances using Kerberos.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, in order to use Kerberos authentication with self-managed AD for their RDS for Oracle instances, customers were required to deploy AWS Managed Microsoft AD and establish trust between AWS managed domain and self-managed domain. Now, customers can join their existing self-managed AD directly to authenticate and authorize users without setting up an additional directory or trust relationship. You can set this up using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDK. You can use self-managed AD without any additional charge.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kerberos authentication with self-managed Active Directory in Amazon RDS for Oracle is available in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;all commercial AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon RDS for Oracle is available. To learn more about Kerberos authentication with self-managed Active Directory, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/oracle-smad.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Working with self-managed Active Directory with an Amazon RDS for Oracle DB instance&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon RDS User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) Capability for Argo CD now supports Argo CD Notifications, providing notifications when your applications sync, fail, or change health. You define triggers and message templates in your cluster and subscribe individual Applications with an annotation, the same way you do in upstream Argo CD.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can deliver notifications to 16 notification services, including Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Amazon SQS, and Amazon EventBridge. The Amazon SQS and Amazon EventBridge services authenticate with your IAM Capability Role, so you can route deployment events into your own event-driven workflows without storing credentials in your cluster. For a service that requires third-party credentials such as a Slack token, you can specify an AWS Secrets Manager ARN in place of the secret and EKS will automatically use it on your behalf.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Notifications are available in all AWS Regions where the EKS Capability for Argo CD is available, at no additional charge. Standard charges apply for resources related to the services your notifications use, for example Amazon SQS or Amazon EventBridge. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/eks/features/"&gt;Amazon EKS&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/argocd-notifications.html"&gt;Amazon EKS User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For organizations that use Amazon Quick, the desktop application now signs users in with the same sign-in method the organization already uses for Quick on the web. Administrators no longer need to register an OIDC application in their identity provider or set up separate desktop sign-in before users can get started. If a user can sign in to Quick on the web, that user can sign in to the desktop application.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To turn on desktop access, an administrator adds the desktop connector once in the Amazon Quick console. Users then enter their Quick account name, choose Sign In, and finish signing in through their browser. This works for accounts that use IAM Identity Center, IAM federation, or native Amazon Quick users. If your organization wants to manage desktop sign-in separately, you can still set up a dedicated identity provider configuration with providers such as Microsoft Entra ID, Google Workspace, Okta, or Ping Identity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick on desktop is available in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/regions.html#regions-qs" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions that support the full Amazon Quick feature set&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/amazon-quick-desktop.html"&gt;Amazon Quick on desktop&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Quick User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition 3 (compatible with MySQL 8.0) supports MySQL 8.0.46, which includes community MySQL fixes and Aurora-specific improvements. In addition to operational improvements, Aurora MySQL 3.14 introduces support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, providing you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in-transit. For detailed information on this release, refer to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraMySQLReleaseNotes/AuroraMySQL.Updates.30Updates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora MySQL 3&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/8.0/en/news-8-0-46.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MySQL 8.0.46&lt;/a&gt; release notes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can upgrade your databases during scheduled maintenance windows using automatic minor version upgrades. To simplify operations at scale, enable &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Updates.AMVU.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automatic minor version upgrades&lt;/a&gt; and use the AWS Organizations &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Aurora.Maintenance.AMVU.UpgradeRollout.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Upgrade Rollout Policy&lt;/a&gt; to orchestrate upgrades across your clusters in phases. You can perform minor version upgrades in-place or via snapshot restore. This release is supported in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Concepts.RegionsAndAvailabilityZones.html#Aurora.Overview.Availability.MySQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora MySQL is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full MySQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at Aurora’s &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_AuroraOverview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/features/router/"&gt;AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;now supports managing connections through a new endpoint registry, which allows you to register devices or software external to MediaConnect and connect those endpoints to your router inputs and outputs. You can share registered endpoints directly with other AWS accounts, eliminating the need to manually chain router inputs and outputs or coordinate IP addresses and encryption settings out of band.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-account endpoint sharing lowers costs by reducing required compute instances and decreases latency through fewer network hops. Sharing is managed through AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM), with Connection Manager providing visibility and control over each shared connection. Common use cases include content distributors sharing live feeds with downstream partners, broadcasters collaborating across organizational boundaries, and media workflows that require secure cross-account video transport.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MediaConnect endpoint registry is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router is available, at no additional cost. For more information, see the AWS Elemental MediaConnect &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/latest/ug/working-with-endpoints.html"&gt;User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Glue Data Catalog now supports OAuth 2.0 bearer token authentication for Apache Iceberg REST Catalog (IRC) APIs using AWS Sign-In. The Apache Iceberg REST Catalog specification defines OAuth 2.0 as its standard authentication mechanism, and query engines such as Apache Spark, Trino, and Apache Flink implement it natively. These engines and external integration partners can now authenticate to the Glue Data Catalog Iceberg endpoint using standard OAuth 2.0 bearer tokens, in addition to AWS Signature Version 4 (SigV4).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now grant partners federated access to your Iceberg tables without distributing AWS credentials. Using OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693), you exchange an OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity token for a short-lived, Glue-scoped bearer token and include it in IRC requests following the same standards-based pattern used across the Iceberg ecosystem. Access remains governed by AWS IAM and AWS Lake Formation permissions. Common use cases include enabling third-party analytics and data cloud platforms to query tables in the Glue Data Catalog through a single, consistent authentication path.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Glue and Lake Formation are available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connect-glu-iceberg-rest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Glue User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/configure-oauth-glue-iceberg-rest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Sign-In User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-097-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/04/2026 10:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon awslabs.dynamodb-mcp-server is an open-source Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that enables AI coding assistants to interact with Amazon DynamoDB, including table design, data modeling, and CDK infrastructure generation. We identified CVE-2026-85654, an improper neutralization of special elements used in a template engine in the CDK generator component. Under certain circumstances, a context-dependent actor could execute arbitrary code on the host that deploys the generated application via crafted table, index, or attribute names in a data model file.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;gt;= 2.0.10 AND &amp;lt;= 2.1.5&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-100-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/04/2026 12:30 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ion-java is a Java library that implements the Amazon Ion data format specification. We identified CVE-2026-85786, memory-amplification denial of service via highly compressed data expansion. ion-java 1.12.0 added a GZIP auto-decompression opt-out for CVE-2026-75936, but the implementation of the opt-out in 1.12.0 was insufficient to address the issue.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 1.12.1&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-095-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/03/2026 10:00 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon CodeCatalyst blueprints are reusable project templates that generate a software project. The @amazon-codecatalyst/blueprints.blueprint npm package is the open source framework that blueprint authors build on, published from github.com/aws/codecatalyst-blueprints.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We identified CVE-2026-85012 in the blueprint resynthesis framework. During resynthesis, the framework reads the .ownership-file from the existing project to determine which files a blueprint may modify. In versions before 0.3.156, the owner field of a [local] merge strategy entry in the .ownership-file was passed to an operating system command through a shell without validation. A user with permission to commit to a repository in the project could place shell metacharacters in that field and execute arbitrary commands in the environment performing resynthesis, with the privileges and credentials available to that environment.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;No action is required for use of the Amazon CodeCatalyst service. Resynthesis runs in an isolated per-project environment with scoped credentials, and the service applies server-side validation there that rejects [local] merge strategy commands outside a restricted allowlisted form, including for blueprint versions published before 0.3.156.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt;= 0.3.155&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-094-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 09/02/2026 13:30 AM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Ion-C (ion-c) is the C implementation of the Amazon Ion data serialization format. It is distributed as an open-source library (amazon-ion/ion-c) that applications embed to read and write Ion text and binary data. We identified CVE-2026-84851, an uncontrolled recursion issue in versions before 1.1.6 that might allow a remote unauthenticated actor to craft Ion data that exhausts the native call stack and crashes the application using the library, resulting in a denial of service.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 1.1.6&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-090-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/27/2026 13:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;awsdac (diagram-as-code) is a CLI tool that generates AWS architecture diagrams from YAML definitions, enabling version-controlled, code-driven diagramming. We identified CVE-2026-81838, a Zip Slip (path traversal) issue. When awsdac extracts a zip archive referenced by a ZipFile resource in a definition file, a crafted archive can write files outside the intended cache directory, to any path writable by the user running awsdac. Depending on the file written, this can lead to arbitrary code execution.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Leveraging this issue requires processing a definition file from an untrusted source. This can occur when:&lt;br&gt; - awsdac is run without definition trust restrictions (versions prior to 0.22.4 had no trust distinction; version 0.22.4 and later require the −−allow−untrusted−definitions flag), or&lt;br&gt; - a definition file is loaded from the local filesystem ('Type: LocalFile'), which bypasses the definition URL allowlist.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CI/CD environments that process definition files from untrusted or semi-trusted sources are the primary risk scenario.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;awsdac is a client-side CLI tool that renders architecture diagrams locally. This issue does not affect any AWS service, AWS account, or customer data. The impact is limited to the machine on which awsdac runs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; awsdac: versions 0.10 through 0.23 (inclusive)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bulletin ID:&lt;/b&gt; 2026-089-AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Scope:&lt;/b&gt; AWS&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Content Type:&lt;/b&gt; Important (requires attention)&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Publication Date:&lt;/b&gt; 08/25/2026 12:00 PM PDT&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Description:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Strands Agents is an open-source Python SDK for building and running AI agents. The strands-agents-tools package provides pre-built tools for use with the SDK, including the python_repl tool, which executes Python code on the agent's host, and the batch tool, which invokes several other tools in a single call. Before executing code, python_repl prompts the operator for approval. We identified CVE-2026-78379, a consent bypass in the python_repl tool. Improper neutralization of input used for LLM prompting in the python_repl tool in Amazon Strands Agents Tools before 0.8.5 might allow remote actors to execute arbitrary Python code on the agent's host by bypassing the human consent gate, via a crafted prompt that forwards non_interactive_mode as a keyword argument through the batch tool.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Impacted versions:&lt;/b&gt; &amp;lt; 0.8.5&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Please refer to the article below for the most up-to-date and complete information related to this AWS Security Bulletin.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform for migrations now gives you three ways to bring your network to AWS. In addition to migrating and modernizing your existing network, you can build a new network from scratch or convert your on-premises access rules into optimized security groups and deploy them into existing VPCs. Whichever path you choose, you arrive on AWS with a secure and scalable network foundation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On-premises networks accumulate years of definitions and access rules that you may not want to carry to AWS as-is. For a clean start, AWS Transform builds a new network aligned to AWS best practices. To bring your access rules with you, AWS Transform converts them into security groups and modernizes them as it goes, detecting stale rules and recommending optimizations, then deploys the groups into your existing VPCs without redeploying your network stack.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities&amp;nbsp;allow you migrate to any &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-connect-target-account.html#transform-vmware-cta-supported-regions"&gt;AWS commercial or GovCloud Region&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-migrate-network.html"&gt;Migrate networks&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform/migrations/"&gt;AWS Transform for migrations&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WorkSpaces for AI agents now handles authentication and desktop connections as part of the managed Model Context Protocol (MCP) service, so you can connect agents to governed cloud desktops, removing the need to write custom code. This makes it easy to use WorkSpaces with any client that supports MCP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Connecting an agent to a desktop previously required you to manage presession requirements; generate, invoke, and rotate streaming URLs manually, adding complexity every time a session expired. You can now create sessions with your existing AWS credentials through OAuth 2.0, and the service handles the connection lifecycle. You configure once which desktop stack to use, and your agents reach the right desktop without ever seeing or handling the underlying streaming connection. You can also query session state to see which desktop stack, fleet, and session an agent is connected to, and generate a link for real-time observability of your agent. This gives you visibility and auditability over active agent work to know what your agents are doing and where.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To help your agents work with multiple applications, they can now discover and launch applications through a catalog&amp;nbsp;MCP Tool rather than navigating menus. Using these new tools, agents can list the available applications, launch one or several at runtime, and switch between them, replacing the start-menu and taskbar navigation with a programmatic, deterministic path that improves accuracy and reduces token cost.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;WorkSpaces for AI agents runs agent sessions inside the same network isolation and compliance boundaries that govern your human workforce, inheriting the VPC controls, data-loss prevention policies, and session-level security posture that enterprises have already configured. The service is also now available in four additional AWS Regions: South America (São Paulo), Europe (Milan), Europe (Spain), and Asia Pacific (Malaysia).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started operating AI agents on WorkSpaces, visit: &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/agent-access.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/agent-access.html&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
